ered during A, B, C, and D term. The Seminar consists of presentations by experts on technical and broader professional topics. Presentations are also offered by graduate students on topics related to their directed research, dissertation, or industrial experiences. The Seminar is offered in pass/fail mode based on attendance. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" building="" days="fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Ameya Jagtap" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ameya Jagtap" room="Salisbury Labs 402 " seats="50" section="B01" starts="2:00PM" type="Seminar"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" crn="000000000000334992" max_waitlist="0" number="B03" part-of-term="B Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" The Seminar is a degree requirement for all graduate students and is offered during A, B, C, and D term. The Seminar consists of presentations by experts on technical and broader professional topics. Presentations are also offered by graduate students on topics related to their directed research, dissertation, or industrial experiences. The Seminar is offered in pass/fail mode based on attendance. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" building="" days="fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Ameya Jagtap" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ameya Jagtap" room="Online-synchronous " seats="25" section="B03" starts="2:00PM" type="Seminar"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="21" crn="000000000000336165" max_waitlist="10" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" The Seminar is a degree requirement for all graduate students and is offered during A, B, C, and D term. The Seminar consists of presentations by experts on technical and broader professional topics. Presentations are also offered by graduate students on topics related to their directed research, dissertation, or industrial experiences. The Seminar is offered in pass/fail mode based on attendance. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="21" building="" days="fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Ameya Jagtap" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ameya Jagtap" room="Higgins Labs 218 Discovery Classroom " seats="50" section="C01" starts="2:00PM" type="Seminar"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="13" crn="000000000000336814" max_waitlist="0" number="C03" part-of-term="C Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" The Seminar is a degree requirement for all graduate students and is offered during A, B, C, and D term. The Seminar consists of presentations by experts on technical and broader professional topics. Presentations are also offered by graduate students on topics related to their directed research, dissertation, or industrial experiences. The Seminar is offered in pass/fail mode based on attendance. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="13" building="" days="fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Ameya Jagtap" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ameya Jagtap" room="Online-synchronous " seats="25" section="C03" starts="2:00PM" type="Seminar"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="26" crn="000000000000336961" max_waitlist="30" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" The Seminar is a degree requirement for all graduate students and is offered during A, B, C, and D term. The Seminar consists of presentations by experts on technical and broader professional topics. Presentations are also offered by graduate students on topics related to their directed research, dissertation, or industrial experiences. The Seminar is offered in pass/fail mode based on attendance. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="26" building="" days="fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="30" professor="Not Assigned" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Not Assigned" room="Atwater Kent 233 " seats="50" section="D01" starts="2:00PM" type="Seminar"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="16" crn="000000000000337569" max_waitlist="0" number="D03" part-of-term="D Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" The Seminar is a degree requirement for all graduate students and is offered during A, B, C, and D term. The Seminar consists of presentations by experts on technical and broader professional topics. Presentations are also offered by graduate students on topics related to their directed research, dissertation, or industrial experiences. The Seminar is offered in pass/fail mode based on attendance. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="16" building="" days="fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Not Assigned" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Not Assigned" room="Online-synchronous " seats="25" section="D03" starts="2:00PM" type="Seminar"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course presents topics in incompressible fluid dynamics at the introductory graduate level. Topics are chosen from: continuum fluids; kinematics and deformation for Newtonian fluids; integral and differential form of the mass conservation, momentum, and energy equations; potential flows; unidirectional steady incompressible viscous flows; unidirectional transient incompressible viscous flows; boundary layers; vortical flows. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5101 “Fluid Dynamics” or AE/ME 5107 “Applied FluidDynamics.” " max-credits="2.0" min-credits="2.0" name="Incompressible Fluid Dynamics" number="5131"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" crn="000000000000336166" max_waitlist="10" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="35" sec_desc=" This course presents topics in incompressible fluid dynamics at the introductory graduate level. Topics are chosen from: continuum fluids; kinematics and deformation for Newtonian fluids; integral and differential form of the mass conservation, momentum, and energy equations; potential flows; unidirectional steady incompressible viscous flows; unidirectional transient incompressible viscous flows; boundary layers; vortical flows. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5101 “Fluid Dynamics” or AE/ME 5107 “Applied FluidDynamics.” " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Nikolaos Gatsonis" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Nikolaos Gatsonis" room="Higgins Labs 202 " seats="35" section="C01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="38" crn="000000000000336127" max_waitlist="0" number="C02" part-of-term="C Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" This course presents topics in incompressible fluid dynamics at the introductory graduate level. Topics are chosen from: continuum fluids; kinematics and deformation for Newtonian fluids; integral and differential form of the mass conservation, momentum, and energy equations; potential flows; unidirectional steady incompressible viscous flows; unidirectional transient incompressible viscous flows; boundary layers; vortical flows. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5101 “Fluid Dynamics” or AE/ME 5107 “Applied Fluid Dynamics.” " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="38" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Nikolaos Gatsonis" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Nikolaos Gatsonis" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="50" section="C02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course presents applications of compressible fluid dynamics at an introductory graduate level. Topics are chosen from: conservation laws; propagation of disturbances; compressible flow with friction; method of characteristics, analysis and design of supersonic nozzles, diffusers, and inlets; transonic and supersonic thin-airfoil theory; three-dimensional compressible flows; compressible boundary layers; hypersonic flows; unsteady compressible flows. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5093 ST: Applied Compressible Fluid Dynamics. " max-credits="2.0" min-credits="2.0" name="Compressible Fluid Dynamics" number="5132"><section actual_waitlist="5" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000336962" max_waitlist="5" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" This course presents applications of compressible fluid dynamics at an introductory graduate level. Topics are chosen from: conservation laws; propagation of disturbances; compressible flow with friction; method of characteristics, analysis and design of supersonic nozzles, diffusers, and inlets; transonic and supersonic thin-airfoil theory; three-dimensional compressible flows; compressible boundary layers; hypersonic flows; unsteady compressible flows. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5093 ST: Applied Compressible Fluid Dynamics. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="5" availableseats="0" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Jagannath Jayachandran" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Jagannath Jayachandran" room="Kaven Hall 115 " seats="20" section="D01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="33" crn="000000000000337272" max_waitlist="0" number="D02" part-of-term="D Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" This course presents applications of compressible fluid dynamics at an introductory graduate level. Topics are chosen from: conservation laws; propagation of disturbances; compressible flow with friction; method of characteristics, analysis and design of supersonic nozzles, diffusers, and inlets; transonic and supersonic thin-airfoil theory; three-dimensional compressible flows; compressible boundary layers; hypersonic flows; unsteady compressible flows. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5093 ST: Applied Compressible Fluid Dynamics. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="33" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Zachary Taillefer" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Zachary Taillefer" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="50" section="D02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This is an introductory graduate level course that covers principles of operation, design, and performance analysis of air-breathing propulsion engines. Topics will be chosen from: jet propulsion theory; cycle analysis of turbojets, turbofans, and ram compression engines; gas dynamics of inlet and nozzle flows; thermochemistry and chemical equilibrium; combustor modeling; hypersonic propulsion; and operation of detonation engines. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5106 “Air Breathing Propulsion”. " max-credits="2.0" min-credits="2.0" name="Air Breathing Propulsion" number="5231"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" crn="000000000000341845" max_waitlist="10" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="35" sec_desc=" This is an introductory graduate level course that covers principles of operation, design, and performance analysis of air-breathing propulsion engines. Topics will be chosen from: jet propulsion theory; cycle analysis of turbojets, turbofans, and ram compression engines; gas dynamics of inlet and nozzle flows; thermochemistry and chemical equilibrium; combustor modeling; hypersonic propulsion; and operation of detonation engines. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5106 “Air Breathing Propulsion”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Jagannath Jayachandran" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Jagannath Jayachandran" room="Higgins Labs 202 " seats="35" section="C01" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" crn="000000000000341886" max_waitlist="0" number="C02" part-of-term="C Term" seats="35" sec_desc=" This is an introductory graduate level course that covers principles of operation, design, and performance analysis of air-breathing propulsion engines. Topics will be chosen from: jet propulsion theory; cycle analysis of turbojets, turbofans, and ram compression engines; gas dynamics of inlet and nozzle flows; thermochemistry and chemical equilibrium; combustor modeling; hypersonic propulsion; and operation of detonation engines. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5106 “Air Breathing Propulsion”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Jagannath Jayachandran" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Jagannath Jayachandran" room="Online-synchronous " seats="35" section="C02" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course introduces concepts needed to evaluate the performance of the most commonly used electric and chemical spacecraft propulsion systems. Fundamental topics in electric propulsion include plasma generation and ion acceleration, magnetic field design, and beam neutralization. Applications include electrostatic ion and Hall thrusters. Fundamental topics in chemical propulsion include propellant thermochemistry and ideal performance. Applications include bipropellant and monopropellant chemistry, catalyst-bed, and nozzle design considerations. Discussion of each class of thruster will be supplemented with specific examples of flight hardware. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5111 “Spacecraft Propulsion”. " max-credits="2.0" min-credits="2.0" name="Spacecraft Propulsion" number="5232"><section actual_waitlist="9" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000338971" max_waitlist="10" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" This course introduces concepts needed to evaluate the performance of the most commonly used electric and chemical spacecraft propulsion systems. Fundamental topics in electric propulsion include plasma generation and ion acceleration, magnetic field design, and beam neutralization. Applications include electrostatic ion and Hall thrusters. Fundamental topics in chemical propulsion include propellant thermochemistry and ideal performance. Applications include bipropellant and monopropellant chemistry, catalyst-bed, and nozzle design considerations. Discussion of each class of thruster will be supplemented with specific examples of flight hardware. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5111 “Spacecraft Propulsion”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="9" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,wed" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="John Blandino" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="John Blandino" room="Olin Hall 126 " seats="30" section="D01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="19" crn="000000000000337245" max_waitlist="0" number="D02" part-of-term="D Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" This course introduces concepts needed to evaluate the performance of the most commonly used electric and chemical spacecraft propulsion systems. Fundamental topics in electric propulsion include plasma generation and ion acceleration, magnetic field design, and beam neutralization. Applications include electrostatic ion and Hall thrusters. Fundamental topics in chemical propulsion include propellant thermochemistry and ideal performance. Applications include bipropellant and monopropellant chemistry, catalyst-bed, and nozzle design considerations. Discussion of each class of thruster will be supplemented with specific examples of flight hardware. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5111 “Spacecraft Propulsion”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="19" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="John Blandino" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="John Blandino" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="30" section="D02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course covers analysis and synthesis of control laws for linear dynamical systems. Fundamental concepts including canonical representations, the state transition matrix, and the properties of controllability and observability will be discussed. The existence and synthesis of stabilizing feedback control laws using pole placement and linear quadratic optimal control will be discussed. The design of Luenberger observers and Kalman filters will be introduced. Examples pertaining to aerospace engineering, such as stability analysis and augmentation of longitudinal and lateral aircraft dynamics, will be considered. Assignments and term project (if any) will focus on the design, analysis, and implementation of linear control for current engineering problems. The use of Matlab®/Simulink® for analysis and design will be emphasized. Recommended background: Familiarity with Matlab®. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5220 “Control of Linear Dynamical Systems”. " max-credits="2.0" min-credits="2.0" name="Linear Control Systems" number="5331"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" crn="000000000000340351" max_waitlist="10" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" This course covers analysis and synthesis of control laws for linear dynamical systems. Fundamental concepts including canonical representations, the state transition matrix, and the properties of controllability and observability will be discussed. The existence and synthesis of stabilizing feedback control laws using pole placement and linear quadratic optimal control will be discussed. The design of Luenberger observers and Kalman filters will be introduced. Examples pertaining to aerospace engineering, such as stability analysis and augmentation of longitudinal and lateral aircraft dynamics, will be considered. Assignments and term project (if any) will focus on the design, analysis, and implementation of linear control for current engineering problems. The use of Matlab®/Simulink® for analysis and design will be emphasized. Recommended background: Familiarity with Matlab®. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5220 “Control of Linear Dynamical Systems”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Michael Demetriou" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Michael Demetriou" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="30" section="A01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" crn="000000000000340879" max_waitlist="10" number="A02" part-of-term="A Term" seats="45" sec_desc=" This course covers analysis and synthesis of control laws for linear dynamical systems. Fundamental concepts including canonical representations, the state transition matrix, and the properties of controllability and observability will be discussed. The existence and synthesis of stabilizing feedback control laws using pole placement and linear quadratic optimal control will be discussed. The design of Luenberger observers and Kalman filters will be introduced. Examples pertaining to aerospace engineering, such as stability analysis and augmentation of longitudinal and lateral aircraft dynamics, will be considered. Assignments and term project (if any) will focus on the design, analysis, and implementation of linear control for current engineering problems. The use of Matlab®/Simulink® for analysis and design will be emphasized. Recommended background: Familiarity with Matlab®. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5220 “Control of Linear Dynamical Systems”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Michael Demetriou" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Michael Demetriou" room="Stratton Hall 201 " seats="45" section="A02" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Overview of spacecraft orbital and rotational motion. Overview and sizing of actuating devices such as gas jet, electric thrusters, momentum wheels and magnetic torquers. Overview and selection of sensing devices such as sun sensors, magnetometers, GPS, IMUs. Formulation of spacecraft maneuvers as control design problems. Estimation techniques for orbit determination and attitude estimation. Static attitude determination methods. Kalman filtering for attitude estimation. Fundamentals of orbit determination. Attitude control based on Lyapunov methods. Case studies on feedback attitude regulators and algorithms for linear and nonlinear attitude tracking. Design and realization of attitude and orbital control schemes using Matlab®/Simulink®. Prerequisites: Fundamentals of spacecraft orbital motion and attitude dynamics at the undergraduate level. Fluency with the theory of linear dynamical systems and control (AE 5331 or similar) and with Matlab® programming. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5223 “Space Vehicle Dynamics and Control”. " max-credits="2.0" min-credits="2.0" name="Spacecraft Dynamics and Control" number="5334"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="16" crn="000000000000342235" max_waitlist="10" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" Overview of spacecraft orbital and rotational motion. Overview and sizing of actuating devices such as gas jet, electric thrusters, momentum wheels and magnetic torquers. Overview and selection of sensing devices such as sun sensors, magnetometers, GPS, IMUs. Formulation of spacecraft maneuvers as control design problems. Estimation techniques for orbit determination and attitude estimation. Static attitude determination methods. Kalman filtering for attitude estimation. Fundamentals of orbit determination. Attitude control based on Lyapunov methods. Case studies on feedback attitude regulators and algorithms for linear and nonlinear attitude tracking. Design and realization of attitude and orbital control schemes using Matlab®/Simulink®. Prerequisites: Fundamentals of spacecraft orbital motion and attitude dynamics at the undergraduate level. Fluency with the theory of linear dynamical systems and control (AE 5331 or similar) and with Matlab® programming. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5223 “Space Vehicle Dynamics and Control”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="16" building="" days="mon,wed" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Michael Demetriou" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Michael Demetriou" room="Stratton Hall 202 (new) " seats="30" section="B01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="17" crn="000000000000342265" max_waitlist="10" number="B02" part-of-term="B Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" Overview of spacecraft orbital and rotational motion. Overview and sizing of actuating devices such as gas jet, electric thrusters, momentum wheels and magnetic torquers. Overview and selection of sensing devices such as sun sensors, magnetometers, GPS, IMUs. Formulation of spacecraft maneuvers as control design problems. Estimation techniques for orbit determination and attitude estimation. Static attitude determination methods. Kalman filtering for attitude estimation. Fundamentals of orbit determination. Attitude control based on Lyapunov methods. Case studies on feedback attitude regulators and algorithms for linear and nonlinear attitude tracking. Design and realization of attitude and orbital control schemes using Matlab®/Simulink®. Prerequisites: Fundamentals of spacecraft orbital motion and attitude dynamics at the undergraduate level. Fluency with the theory of linear dynamical systems and control (AE 5331 or similar) and with Matlab® programming. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5223 “Space Vehicle Dynamics and Control”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="17" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Michael Demetriou" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Michael Demetriou" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="30" section="B02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course is an introductory graduate level course. Fundamental topics will be chosen from the following: three-dimensional states of stress; measures of strain; plane stress and plane strain; thermoelasticity; Airy stress function; and energy methods. Applied topics will be chosen from the following: bending and shear stresses on unsymmetric cross-sections; bending of composite beams; bending of curved beams; torsion of thin-walled noncircular cross sections; and failure criteria. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5380 “Foundations of Elasticity” or AE/ME 5381 “Applied Elasticity”. " max-credits="2.0" min-credits="2.0" name="Solid Mechanics for Aerospace Structures (2" number="5431"><section actual_waitlist="2" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000336168" max_waitlist="10" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="35" sec_desc=" This course is an introductory graduate level course. Fundamental topics will be chosen from the following: three-dimensional states of stress; measures of strain; plane stress and plane strain; thermoelasticity; Airy stress function; and energy methods. Applied topics will be chosen from the following: bending and shear stresses on unsymmetric cross-sections; bending of composite beams; bending of curved beams; torsion of thin-walled noncircular cross sections; and failure criteria. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5380 “Foundations of Elasticity” or AE/ME 5381 “Applied Elasticity”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="2" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,wed" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Mark Richman" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Mark Richman" room="Higgins Labs 154 " seats="35" section="C01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="40" crn="000000000000336512" max_waitlist="10" number="C02" part-of-term="C Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" This course is an introductory graduate level course. Fundamental topics will be chosen from the following: three-dimensional states of stress; measures of strain; plane stress and plane strain; thermoelasticity; Airy stress function; and energy methods. Applied topics will be chosen from the following: bending and shear stresses on unsymmetric cross-sections; bending of composite beams; bending of curved beams; torsion of thin-walled noncircular cross sections; and failure criteria. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5380 “Foundations of Elasticity” or AE/ME 5381 “Applied Elasticity”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="40" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Mark Richman" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Mark Richman" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="50" section="C02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course covers the anisotropic constitutive behavior and micromechanics of composite materials, and the mechanics of composite structures at an introductory graduate level. Topics covered will be chosen from: classification of composites (reinforcements and matrices) anisotropic elasticity, composite micromechanics, effect of reinforcement on toughness and strength of composites, laminate theory, statics and buckling of laminated beams and plates, statics of laminated shells, residual stresses and thermal effects in  laminates. Students cannot   receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5383 “Composite materials”.  " max-credits="2.0" min-credits="2.0" name="Composite Materials" number="5432"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="4" crn="000000000000338934" max_waitlist="10" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="35" sec_desc=" This course covers the anisotropic constitutive behavior and micromechanics of composite materials, and the mechanics of composite structures at an introductory graduate level. Topics covered will be chosen from: classification of composites (reinforcements and matrices) anisotropic elasticity, composite micromechanics, effect of reinforcement on toughness and strength of composites, laminate theory, statics and buckling of laminated beams and plates, statics of laminated shells, residual stresses and thermal effects in  laminates. Students cannot   receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5383 “Composite materials”.  " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="4" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Nikhil Karanjgaokar" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Nikhil Karanjgaokar" room="Higgins Labs 202 " seats="35" section="A01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" crn="000000000000339113" max_waitlist="10" number="A02" part-of-term="A Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" This course covers the anisotropic constitutive behavior and micromechanics of composite materials, and the mechanics of composite structures at an introductory graduate level. Topics covered will be chosen from: classification of composites (reinforcements and matrices) anisotropic elasticity, composite micromechanics, effect of reinforcement on toughness and strength of composites, laminate theory, statics and buckling of laminated beams and plates, statics of laminated shells, residual stresses and thermal effects in  laminates. Students cannot   receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5383 “Composite materials”.  " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Nikhil Karanjgaokar" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Nikhil Karanjgaokar" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="30" section="A02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" The course provides at an entry graduate level the theory and practice of finite difference and finite elements methods for partial differential equations (PDEs) encountered in fluid dynamics and solid mechanics. Topics covered include: classification of partial PDEs and characteristics; direct and iterative solution methods for solution of algebraic systems; finite difference and finite element spatial discretization; temporal discretization; consistency, stability and error analysis; explicit and implicit finite differencing and finite element schemes for linear hyperbolic, parabolic, elliptic PDEs. The course requires completion of several projects using MATLAB. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5108 “Computational Fluid Dynamics”. " max-credits="2.0" min-credits="2.0" name="Applied Computational Methods for Partial Differential Equations" number="5531"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" crn="000000000000335090" max_waitlist="10" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="40" sec_desc=" The course provides at an entry graduate level the theory and practice of finite difference and finite elements methods for partial differential equations (PDEs) encountered in fluid dynamics and solid mechanics. Topics covered include: classification of partial PDEs and characteristics; direct and iterative solution methods for solution of algebraic systems; finite difference and finite element spatial discretization; temporal discretization; consistency, stability and error analysis; explicit and implicit finite differencing and finite element schemes for linear hyperbolic, parabolic, elliptic PDEs. The course requires completion of several projects using MATLAB. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5108 “Computational Fluid Dynamics”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Zhangxian Yuan" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Zhangxian Yuan" room="Higgins Labs 154 " seats="40" section="B01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="28" crn="000000000000335504" max_waitlist="0" number="B02" part-of-term="B Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" The course provides at an entry graduate level the theory and practice of finite difference and finite elements methods for partial differential equations (PDEs) encountered in fluid dynamics and solid mechanics. Topics covered include: classification of partial PDEs and characteristics; direct and iterative solution methods for solution of algebraic systems; finite difference and finite element spatial discretization; temporal discretization; consistency, stability and error analysis; explicit and implicit finite differencing and finite element schemes for linear hyperbolic, parabolic, elliptic PDEs. The course requires completion of several projects using MATLAB. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5108 “Computational Fluid Dynamics”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="28" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Nikolaos Gatsonis; Zhangxian Yuan" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Nikolaos Gatsonis; Zhangxian Yuan" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="50" section="B02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course provides a graduate-level introduction to static and dynamic aeroelasticity, for conventional aircraft. Students will be presented with analytical and computational techniques used to model and simulate aeroelasticity. Topics covered will be chosen from: divergence; aileron reversal; airload redistribution; sweep effects; unsteady aerodynamics; and flutter of wings. Prerequisite: AE 4410 or equivalent course. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5382 “Aeroelasticity”. " max-credits="2.0" min-credits="2.0" name="Aeroelasticity" number="6433"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="35" crn="000000000000338990" max_waitlist="10" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="48" sec_desc=" This course provides a graduate-level introduction to static and dynamic aeroelasticity, for conventional aircraft. Students will be presented with analytical and computational techniques used to model and simulate aeroelasticity. Topics covered will be chosen from: divergence; aileron reversal; airload redistribution; sweep effects; unsteady aerodynamics; and flutter of wings. Prerequisite: AE 4410 or equivalent course. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5382 “Aeroelasticity”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="35" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Zhangxian Yuan" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Zhangxian Yuan" room="Olin Hall 223 " seats="48" section="B01" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" crn="000000000000341926" max_waitlist="10" number="B02" part-of-term="B Term" seats="24" sec_desc=" This course provides a graduate-level introduction to static and dynamic aeroelasticity, for conventional aircraft. Students will be presented with analytical and computational techniques used to model and simulate aeroelasticity. Topics covered will be chosen from: divergence; aileron reversal; airload redistribution; sweep effects; unsteady aerodynamics; and flutter of wings. Prerequisite: AE 4410 or equivalent course. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE/ME 5382 “Aeroelasticity”. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Zhangxian Yuan" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Zhangxian Yuan" room="Online-synchronous " seats="24" section="B02" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course introduces theoretical and practical aspects of deep learning, emphasizing applications in areas such as fluid dynamics, heat transfer, and solid mechanics. Topics covered include: foundational deep learning techniques, specialized loss functions, regularization and weight initialization strategies, advanced deep learning algorithms, physics-informed deep learning (PIDL), domain decomposition-based PIDL methods, and various performance enhancement techniques. Students will engage in multiple hands-on coding projects, utilizing prominent machine learning libraries, to solve governing partial differential equations. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5093 “Special Topics: Scientific Deep Learning for Engineers”. Recommended background: Linear Algebra (MA 2071 or equivalent), Computational Methods (AE 5531 or equivalent), Programming Language (Python preferred). Credits: 2 " max-credits="2.0" min-credits="2.0" name="Scientific Deep Learning for Engineers and Scientists" number="6531"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="19" crn="000000000000347175" max_waitlist="10" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" This course introduces theoretical and practical aspects of deep learning, emphasizing applications in areas such as fluid dynamics, heat transfer, and solid mechanics. Topics covered include: foundational deep learning techniques, specialized loss functions, regularization and weight initialization strategies, advanced deep learning algorithms, physics-informed deep learning (PIDL), domain decomposition-based PIDL methods, and various performance enhancement techniques. Students will engage in multiple hands-on coding projects, utilizing prominent machine learning libraries, to solve governing partial differential equations. Students cannot receive credit for this course if they have taken AE 5093 “Special Topics: Scientific Deep Learning for Engineers”. Recommended background: Linear Algebra (MA 2071 or equivalent), Computational Methods (AE 5531 or equivalent), Programming Language (Python preferred). Credits: 2 " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="19" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Ameya Jagtap" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ameya Jagtap" room="Innovation Studio 203 Active Learning Classroom South " seats="25" section="D01" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course></dept><dept abbrev="AR" name="Art"><course course_desc=" Cat. IThis course provides an introduction to the basic principles of two and three-dimensionalvisual organization. The course focuses on graphic expression, ideadevelopment, and visual literacy. Students will be expected to master basicrendering skills, perspective drawing, concept art, and storyboarding throughtraditional and/or computer-based tools. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Essentials Of Art" number="1100"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000334516" max_waitlist="12" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis course provides an introduction to the basic principles of two and three-dimensionalvisual organization. The course focuses on graphic expression, ideadevelopment, and visual literacy. Students will be expecte