Term" seats="25" sec_desc="Cat. IThis course will introduce basic and essential programming skills in modern engineering program language, Matlab, to all BME students. The course will include basic programming syntax, control structures, data structures (vectors, matrices, structures, cell arrays), 2D images, 3D image volumes, string manipulations, File I/O, figure plotting/visualization, image display, and basic graphical user interface (GUI) design.Recommended background: none.NOTE: The course does not count for engineering credits, but will fulfill the computer programming requirement for BME students." term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="19" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Not Assigned" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Not Assigned" room="Salisbury Labs 402 " seats="50" section="DL01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="6" building="" days="tue" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Songbai Ji" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Songbai Ji" room="Kaven Hall 203 CARS Computer Lab " seats="25" section="DX02" starts="10:00AM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. IThis beginning course provides important background for all science and engineering disciplines regarding the capabilities and limitations of materials relevant to the development of medical devices. Students are introduced to the fundamental theme of materials science-- structureproperty-processing relationships in biomaterials, specifically metals, ceramics, and plastics. Aspects of material structure range from the atomic to microstructural and macroscopic scales. In turn, these structural features determine the properties of materials. In particular, this course investigates connections between structure and mechanical properties, and how working and thermal treatments may transform structure and thus alter material properties. This knowledge is then applied to material selection decisions for the design of medical devices and engineered tissues.Recommended background: prior knowledge of college-level chemistry and physics.Students who have previously received credit for ES 2001 or BME 2811 may not receive credit for BME 2001. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Introduction To Biomaterials" number="2001"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" crn="000000000000336575" max_waitlist="40" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis beginning course provides important background for all science and engineering disciplines regarding the capabilities and limitations of materials relevant to the development of medical devices. Students are introduced to the fundamental theme of materials science-- structureproperty-processing relationships in biomaterials, specifically metals, ceramics, and plastics. Aspects of material structure range from the atomic to microstructural and macroscopic scales. In turn, these structural features determine the properties of materials. In particular, this course investigates connections between structure and mechanical properties, and how working and thermal treatments may transform structure and thus alter material properties. This knowledge is then applied to material selection decisions for the design of medical devices and engineered tissues.Recommended background: prior knowledge of college-level chemistry and physics.Students who have previously received credit for ES 2001 or BME 2811 may not receive credit for BME 2001. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="40" professor="Yonghui Ding" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Yonghui Ding" room="Unity Hall 420 " seats="50" section="C01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="20" crn="000000000000337385" max_waitlist="20" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat. I This beginning course provides important background for all science and engineering disciplines regarding the capabilities and limitations of materials relevant to the development of medical devices. Students are introduced to the fundamental theme of materials science-- structureproperty-processing relationships in biomaterials, specifically metals, ceramics, and plastics. Aspects of material structure range from the atomic to microstructural and macroscopic scales. In turn, these structural features determine the properties of materials. In particular, this course investigates connections between structure and mechanical properties, and how working and thermal treatments may transform structure and thus alter material properties. This knowledge is then applied to material selection decisions for the design of medical devices and engineered tissues. Recommended background: prior knowledge of college-level chemistry and physics. Students who have previously received credit for ES 2001 or BME 2811 may not receive credit for BME 2001. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="20" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Zoe Reidinger" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Zoe Reidinger" room="Salisbury Labs 104 " seats="50" section="D01" starts="4:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat I.  This course is an introduction to the instrumentation methods used to measure, store and analyze the signals produced by biomedical phenomena. The goal of this course is to familiarize students with the basic design and implementation of techniques for measuring a broad scope of signal types for molecular, cellular and physiological research. Sensors used for acquiring electrical, magnetic, optical/spectral and chemical signals will be covered. Topics include the underlying physics and chemistry of biomedical signals, biosensor types and usage, amplification and signal conditioning, data acquisition methods, and sources of artifact and noise.  Recommended background: PH 1120/21, CH 1010 or equivalent. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Biomedical Signals, Instruments And Measurements" number="2210"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" crn="000000335060339699" max_waitlist="0" number="BL01/BX01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat I.  This course is an introduction to the instrumentation methods used to measure, store and analyze the signals produced by biomedical phenomena. The goal of this course is to familiarize students with the basic design and implementation of techniques for measuring a broad scope of signal types for molecular, cellular and physiological research. Sensors used for acquiring electrical, magnetic, optical/spectral and chemical signals will be covered. Topics include the underlying physics and chemistry of biomedical signals, biosensor types and usage, amplification and signal conditioning, data acquisition methods, and sources of artifact and noise.  Recommended background: PH 1120/21, CH 1010 or equivalent. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="50" professor="Dirk Albrecht" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Dirk Albrecht" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="50" section="BL01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" building="" days="wed" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Dirk Albrecht" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Dirk Albrecht" room="Salisbury Labs 305 " seats="50" section="BX01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="20" crn="000000336576339798" max_waitlist="0" number="CL01/CX01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat I.This course is an introduction to the instrumentation methods used to measure,store and analyze the signals produced by biomedical phenomena. The goal ofthis course is to familiarize students with the basic design and implementation oftechniques for measuring a broad scope of signal types for molecular, cellularand physiological research. Sensors used for acquiring electrical, magnetic,optical/spectral and chemical signals will be covered. Topics include theunderlying physics and chemistry of biomedical signals, biosensor types andusage, amplification and signal conditioning, data acquisition methods, andsources of artifact and noise.Recommended background: PH 1120/21, CH 1010 or equivalent. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="20" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="25" professor="Dirk Albrecht" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Dirk Albrecht" room="Salisbury Labs 305 " seats="50" section="CL01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="20" building="" days="wed" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Dirk Albrecht" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Dirk Albrecht" room="Salisbury Labs 305 " seats="50" section="CX01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I  To learn the fundamentals of basic signal processing methods as well as linear time series analyses framework for modeling and mining biological data. Tools of data analysis include statistics for determining significance of a result, Laplace and Z transforms, convolution, correlation, sampling theorem, Fourier transform, transfer function, coherence function and various filtering techniques. The goal of this course is to offer the students an opportunity to learn and model and simulate static and dynamic physiological systems using linear systems theory. First principles of chemistry and physics are used to quantitatively model physiological systems. Most of the models are based on linear systems theory. Simulations and estimation are performed using Matlab and already-developed software.  Recommended background: BME 2210, CS 1004 or equivalent. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Biomedical Data Analysis" number="2211"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" crn="000000000000338922" max_waitlist="10" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="48" sec_desc=" Cat. I  To learn the fundamentals of basic signal processing methods as well as linear time series analyses framework for modeling and mining biological data. Tools of data analysis include statistics for determining significance of a result, Laplace and Z transforms, convolution, correlation, sampling theorem, Fourier transform, transfer function, coherence function and various filtering techniques. The goal of this course is to offer the students an opportunity to learn and model and simulate static and dynamic physiological systems using linear systems theory. First principles of chemistry and physics are used to quantitatively model physiological systems. Most of the models are based on linear systems theory. Simulations and estimation are performed using Matlab and already-developed software.  Recommended background: BME 2210, CS 1004 or equivalent. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Taimoor Afzal" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Taimoor Afzal" room="Unity Hall 405 " seats="48" section="A01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="4" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000336942" max_waitlist="50" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat. I To learn the fundamentals of basic signal processing methods as well as linear time series analyses framework for modeling and mining biological data. Tools of data analysis include statistics for determining significance of a result, Laplace and Z transforms, convolution, correlation, sampling theorem, Fourier transform, transfer function, coherence function and various filtering techniques. The goal of this course is to offer the students an opportunity to learn and model and simulate static and dynamic physiological systems using linear systems theory. First principles of chemistry and physics are used to quantitatively model physiological systems. Most of the models are based on linear systems theory. Simulations and estimation are performed using Matlab and already-developed software. Recommended background: BME 2210, CS 1004 or equivalent. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="4" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="50" professor="Taimoor Afzal" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Taimoor Afzal" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="50" section="D01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I  This is an introductory course that addresses the analysis of basic mechanical and structural elements relevant to biomechanics. Topics include general concepts of stresses, strains, and material properties of biomaterials and biological materials including viscoelasticity. Also covered are stress concentrations, two-dimensional stress transformations, principal stresses, and Mohr’s circle. Applications are to uniaxially loaded bars, circular shafts under torsion, bending and shearing and deflection of beams. Both statically determinate and indeterminate problems are analyzed.  Recommended background: Differential (MA 1021) and integral (MA 1022) calculus, vector algebra (MA 1023), physics mechanics (PH 1110 or PH 1111), and statics (ES 2501). Students who have previously received credit for BME 2511 or ES 2502 may not receive credit for BME 2502. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Introduction To Biomechanics: Stress Analysis" number="2502"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" crn="000000000000338947" max_waitlist="20" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat. I  This is an introductory course that addresses the analysis of basic mechanical and structural elements relevant to biomechanics. Topics include general concepts of stresses, strains, and material properties of biomaterials and biological materials including viscoelasticity. Also covered are stress concentrations, two-dimensional stress transformations, principal stresses, and Mohr’s circle. Applications are to uniaxially loaded bars, circular shafts under torsion, bending and shearing and deflection of beams. Both statically determinate and indeterminate problems are analyzed.  Recommended background: Differential (MA 1021) and integral (MA 1022) calculus, vector algebra (MA 1023), physics mechanics (PH 1110 or PH 1111), and statics (ES 2501). Students who have previously received credit for BME 2511 or ES 2502 may not receive credit for BME 2502. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Zhenglun Alan Wei" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Zhenglun Alan Wei" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="50" section="A01" starts="4:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="16" crn="000000000000336944" max_waitlist="20" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat. I This is an introductory course that addresses the analysis of basic mechanical and structural elements relevant to biomechanics. Topics include general concepts of stresses, strains, and material properties of biomaterials and biological materials including viscoelasticity. Also covered are stress concentrations, two-dimensional stress transformations, principal stresses, and Mohr’s circle. Applications are to uniaxially loaded bars, circular shafts under torsion, bending and shearing and deflection of beams. Both statically determinate and indeterminate problems are analyzed. Recommended background: Differential (MA 1021) and integral (MA 1022) calculus, vector algebra (MA 1023), physics mechanics (PH 1110 or PH 1111), and statics (ES 2501). Students who have previously received credit for BME 2511 or ES 2502 may not receive credit for BME 2502. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="16" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="John Obayemi" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="John Obayemi" room="Salisbury Labs 305 " seats="50" section="D01" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course is an introduction to fundamental material and energy balances related to the field of Biomedical Engineering. The fundamentals of bioprocess engineering calculations and data analysis, and bioengineering processes and process variables will be covered. Students will learn to identify a system, define boundary conditions, and characterize the system processes to generate appropriate material and energy balances using the principles of conservation of mass and energy. Fundamentals and applications in the human body and biomanufacturing are examined. Specific examples may include an organ, multiple organs or the entire body, bioprocess instrumentation, individual or groups of cells, cell culture bioreactors, tissue engineered scaffolds, and drug delivery systems. Recommended background: Basic knowledge of differential and integral calculus (e.g. MA 1021 and MA 1022 or equivalent), human biology (e.g., BB 1025 or equivalent), and chemistry (e.g. CH 1010 and CH1020 or equivalent). " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Introduction To Bioprocess Engineering" number="2610"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000334498" max_waitlist="20" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course is an introduction to fundamental material and energy balances related to the field of Biomedical Engineering. The fundamentals of bioprocess engineering calculations and data analysis, and bioengineering processes and process variables will be covered. Students will learn to identify a system, define boundary conditions, and characterize the system processes to generate appropriate material and energy balances using the principles of conservation of mass and energy. Fundamentals and applications in the human body and biomanufacturing are examined. Specific examples may include an organ, multiple organs or the entire body, bioprocess instrumentation, individual or groups of cells, cell culture bioreactors, tissue engineered scaffolds, and drug delivery systems. Recommended background: Basic knowledge of differential and integral calculus (e.g. MA 1021 and MA 1022 or equivalent), human biology (e.g., BB 1025 or equivalent), and chemistry (e.g. CH 1010 and CH1020 or equivalent). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Jeannine Coburn" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Jeannine Coburn" room="Salisbury Labs 402 " seats="50" section="A01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc="BME 3012 Biomedical Sensors Laboratory (1/6 units; Cat. I) This laboratory-based course is designed to develop hands-on experimental skills relevant to the selection and application of various sensors used to acquire biomedical signals. Recommended Background:BME 2210, ECE 2010, ECE 2019 or equivalent. Students who have previously taken BME 3011 may not receive credit for this course" max-credits="1.5" min-credits="1.5" name="Biomedical Sensors Laboratory: Techniques" number="3012"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="6" crn="000000334933334833" max_waitlist="0" number="BL01/BX01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="12" sec_desc="BME 3012 Biomedical Sensors Laboratory (1/6 units; Cat. I) This laboratory-based course is designed to develop hands-on experimental skills relevant to the selection and