" max_waitlist="0" number="DL01/DX04" part-of-term="D Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I We will examine the nature of molecular motions and their interaction with electromagnetic radiation, which provides us with all our structural information about molecules. In addition, students will be introduced to to important techniques such as the fundamentals of infrared (I.R.), nuclear magnetic resonance (N.M.R.), UV-Vis absorption and fluorescence spectroscopies, and electrochemistry. The concepts of these techniques will be discussed in the context of structural organic chemistry and polymer characterization. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="65" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="8:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Christopher Lambert" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Christopher Lambert" room="Salisbury Labs 115 Kinnicutt Hall " seats="80" section="DL01" starts="8:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="16" building="" days="wed,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Not Assigned" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Not Assigned" room="Goddard Hall 110 Chem Lab " seats="20" section="DX04" starts="9:00AM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="16" crn="000000336891337170" max_waitlist="0" number="DL01/DX05" part-of-term="D Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I We will examine the nature of molecular motions and their interaction with electromagnetic radiation, which provides us with all our structural information about molecules. In addition, students will be introduced to to important techniques such as the fundamentals of infrared (I.R.), nuclear magnetic resonance (N.M.R.), UV-Vis absorption and fluorescence spectroscopies, and electrochemistry. The concepts of these techniques will be discussed in the context of structural organic chemistry and polymer characterization. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="65" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="8:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Christopher Lambert" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Christopher Lambert" room="Salisbury Labs 115 Kinnicutt Hall " seats="80" section="DL01" starts="8:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="16" building="" days="wed,fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Not Assigned" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Not Assigned" room="Goddard Hall 110 Chem Lab " seats="20" section="DX05" starts="12:00PM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I A systematic survey of the major reaction types and functional groups in organic chemistry. The course will provide a representative collection of characteristic reactions and transformations of a variety of types of organic molecules. Most of the examples will be drawn from aliphatic chemistry. Some theoretical models will be introduced with a view toward establishing a general overview of the material.  The course is intended for chemists, chemical engineers, pre-medical students and all those interested in the biosciences. A familiarity with the material presented in the general chemistry courses is assumed. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Organic Chemistry I" number="2310"><section actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000335461" max_waitlist="20" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="130" sec_desc=" Cat. I A systematic survey of the major reaction types and functional groups in organic chemistry. The course will provide a representative collection of characteristic reactions and transformations of a variety of types of organic molecules. Most of the examples will be drawn from aliphatic chemistry. Some theoretical models will be introduced with a view toward establishing a general overview of the material.  The course is intended for chemists, chemical engineers, pre-medical students and all those interested in the biosciences. A familiarity with the material presented in the general chemistry courses is assumed. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="John MacDonald" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="John MacDonald" room="Salisbury Labs 115 Kinnicutt Hall " seats="130" section="B01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="34" crn="000000000000335463" max_waitlist="20" number="B02" part-of-term="B Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. I A systematic survey of the major reaction types and functional groups in organic chemistry. The course will provide a representative collection of characteristic reactions and transformations of a variety of types of organic molecules. Most of the examples will be drawn from aliphatic chemistry. Some theoretical models will be introduced with a view toward establishing a general overview of the material.  The course is intended for chemists, chemical engineers, pre-medical students and all those interested in the biosciences. A familiarity with the material presented in the general chemistry courses is assumed. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="34" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Anita Mattson" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Anita Mattson" room="Unity Hall 500 " seats="60" section="B02" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I Modern theories of aromaticity, including a general assessment of delocalized bonding. The chemistry of some significant functional groups not surveyed in Organic Chemistry I, and the meaning of acidity and basicity in organic chemistry, will be more fully explored. The course will provide an introduction to the systematic synthesis of polyfunctional organic compounds.  Recommended background: CH 2310. The course is intended for chemists, chemical engineers and bio-science majors. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Organic Chemistry II" number="2320"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" crn="000000000000336116" max_waitlist="10" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="110" sec_desc=" Cat. I Modern theories of aromaticity, including a general assessment of delocalized bonding. The chemistry of some significant functional groups not surveyed in Organic Chemistry I, and the meaning of acidity and basicity in organic chemistry, will be more fully explored. The course will provide an introduction to the systematic synthesis of polyfunctional organic compounds.  Recommended background: CH 2310. The course is intended for chemists, chemical engineers and bio-science majors. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="John MacDonald" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="John MacDonald" room="Atwater Kent 116 Newell Hall " seats="110" section="C01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="37" crn="000000000000336306" max_waitlist="20" number="C02" part-of-term="C Term" seats="80" sec_desc=" Cat. I Modern theories of aromaticity, including a general assessment of delocalized bonding. The chemistry of some significant functional groups not surveyed in Organic Chemistry I, and the meaning of acidity and basicity in organic chemistry, will be more fully explored. The course will provide an introduction to the systematic synthesis of polyfunctional organic compounds.  Recommended background: CH 2310. The course is intended for chemists, chemical engineers and bio-science majors. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="37" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Raul Orduna Picon" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Raul Orduna Picon" room="Higgins Labs 116 " seats="80" section="C02" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. IThis course fully explores three most important analytical methods in organic chemistry: infrared spectroscopy, mass spectrometry, and nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy. It will continue the coverage of aromatic chemistry. New topics to be introduced include structures, properties, and reactivities of aldehydes and ketones, carboxylic acids and their derivatives, amines, and the interaction among polyfunctional compounds. It reinforces the retrosynthetic analysis and multistep synthesis of organic compounds and revisits reaction mechanisms and stereochemistry of all the new functional groups studied.Recommended background: CH2310 and CH2320. The course is intended for biochemists, chemists, chemical engineers and bioscience majors. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Organic Chemistry III" number="2330"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" crn="000000000000337171" max_waitlist="20" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="80" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis course fully explores three most important analytical methods in organic chemistry: infrared spectroscopy, mass spectrometry, and nuclear magnetic resonance spectroscopy. It will continue the coverage of aromatic chemistry. New topics to be introduced include structures, properties, and reactivities of aldehydes and ketones, carboxylic acids and their derivatives, amines, and the interaction among polyfunctional compounds. It reinforces the retrosynthetic analysis and multistep synthesis of organic compounds and revisits reaction mechanisms and stereochemistry of all the new functional groups studied.Recommended background: CH2310 and CH2320. The course is intended for biochemists, chemists, chemical engineers and bioscience majors. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Uma Kumar" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Uma Kumar" room="Atwater Kent 116 Newell Hall " seats="80" section="D01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc="Cat. ILaboratory experience in standard methods for the preparation and purification of organic compounds. The course will provide sufficient training in laboratory technique so that no previous laboratory experience beyond that of general chemistry is required. This course may be taken concurrently or following lecture courses in organic chemistry. Recommended for pre-medical students and students majoring in disciplines outside of chemistry and biochemistry that desire laboratory experience in basic methods of organic synthesis.Recommended background: Fundamentals of chemistry, basic chemistry laboratory techniques (e.g., basic synthesis, spectral analysis and chemical separation skills)." max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Organic Laboratory" number="2360"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" crn="000000334156334157" max_waitlist="0" number="AL01/AX01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="20" sec_desc="Cat. ILaboratory experience in standard methods for the preparation and purification of organic compounds. The course will provide sufficient training in laboratory technique so that no previous laboratory experience beyond that of general chemistry is required. This course may be taken concurrently or following lecture courses in organic chemistry. Recommended for pre-medical students and students majoring in disciplines outside of chemistry and biochemistry that desire laboratory experience in basic methods of organic synthesis.Recommended background: Fundamentals of chemistry, basic chemistry laboratory techniques (e.g., basic synthesis, spectral analysis and chemical separation skills)." term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="23" building="" days="tue" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Uma Kumar" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Uma Kumar" ro