synthesis. Recommended background: Fundamentals of chemistry, basic chemistry laboratory techniques (e.g., basic synthesis, spectral analysis and chemical separation skills). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="9" availableseats="0" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="999" professor="N/A" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="N/A" room="Unknown" seats="0" section="Interest List-D Term" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000336896336898" max_waitlist="0" number="DL01/DX01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. ILaboratory experience in standard methods for the preparation and purification of organic compounds. The course will provide sufficient training in laboratory technique so that no previous laboratory experience beyond that of general chemistry is required. This course may be taken concurrently or following lecture courses in organic chemistry. Recommended for pre-medical students and students majoring in disciplines outside of chemistry and biochemistry that desire laboratory experience in basic methods of organic synthesis.Recommended background: Fundamentals of chemistry, basic chemistry laboratory techniques (e.g., basic synthesis, spectral analysis and chemical separation skills). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="tue" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="John MacDonald; Raul Orduna Picon" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="John MacDonald; Raul Orduna Picon" room="Salisbury Labs 406 " seats="40" section="DL01" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,wed,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Raul Orduna Picon" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Raul Orduna Picon" room="Goddard Hall 307 CH Lab " seats="20" section="DX01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000336896337026" max_waitlist="0" number="DL01/DX02" part-of-term="D Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. ILaboratory experience in standard methods for the preparation and purification of organic compounds. The course will provide sufficient training in laboratory technique so that no previous laboratory experience beyond that of general chemistry is required. This course may be taken concurrently or following lecture courses in organic chemistry. Recommended for pre-medical students and students majoring in disciplines outside of chemistry and biochemistry that desire laboratory experience in basic methods of organic synthesis.Recommended background: Fundamentals of chemistry, basic chemistry laboratory techniques (e.g., basic synthesis, spectral analysis and chemical separation skills). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="tue" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="John MacDonald; Raul Orduna Picon" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="John MacDonald; Raul Orduna Picon" room="Salisbury Labs 406 " seats="40" section="DL01" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,wed,fri" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="John MacDonald" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="John MacDonald" room="Goddard Hall 307 CH Lab " seats="20" section="DX02" starts="8:00AM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I  This laboratory course focuses on the application of modern instrumental methods of analysis to chemical, biochemical and environmental problems. Practical experience is gained in quantitative electrochemistry, ultraviolet-visible spectrophotometry, fluorometry and bioluminescence, high performance liquid chromatography, and capillary electrophoresis. Principles of experimental design and execution are developed as student teams select a chemical, biochemical or environmental problem, formulate an approach, conduct the analysis, and present findings to the class. Methods of data analysis and common statistical approaches are emphasized throughout the course.  Recommended background: CH 1010, CH 1020, CH 1030, CH 1040. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Experimental Chemistry I: Instrumental Analysis" number="2640"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" crn="000000334158334159" max_waitlist="0" number="AL01/AX01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I  This laboratory course focuses on the application of modern instrumental methods of analysis to chemical, biochemical and environmental problems. Practical experience is gained in quantitative electrochemistry, ultraviolet-visible spectrophotometry, fluorometry and bioluminescence, high performance liquid chromatography, and capillary electrophoresis. Principles of experimental design and execution are developed as student teams select a chemical, biochemical or environmental problem, formulate an approach, conduct the analysis, and present findings to the class. Methods of data analysis and common statistical approaches are emphasized throughout the course.  Recommended background: CH 1010, CH 1020, CH 1030, CH 1040. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" building="" days="wed" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Drew Brodeur" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Drew Brodeur" room="Salisbury Labs 402 " seats="40" section="AL01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" building="" days="mon,thu,fri" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Drew Brodeur" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Drew Brodeur" room="Goddard Hall 306 CH Lab " seats="20" section="AX01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" crn="000000334158334160" max_waitlist="0" number="AL01/AX02" part-of-term="A Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I  This laboratory course focuses on the application of modern instrumental methods of analysis to chemical, biochemical and environmental problems. Practical experience is gained in quantitative electrochemistry, ultraviolet-visible spectrophotometry, fluorometry and bioluminescence, high performance liquid chromatography, and capillary electrophoresis. Principles of experimental design and execution are developed as student teams select a chemical, biochemical or environmental problem, formulate an approach, conduct the analysis, and present findings to the class. Methods of data analysis and common statistical approaches are emphasized throughout the course.  Recommended background: CH 1010, CH 1020, CH 1030, CH 1040. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" building="" days="wed" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Drew Brodeur" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Drew Brodeur" room="Salisbury Labs 402 " seats="40" section="AL01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" building="" days="mon,thu,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Drew Brodeur" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Drew Brodeur" room="Goddard Hall 306 CH Lab " seats="20" section="AX02" starts="11:00AM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This laboratory course emphasizes principles, techniques, and instrumentation employed in modern physical chemistry with a view towards applications throughout the molecular sciences. Investigations include chemical thermodynamics and phase equilibria; gas-phase, solution-phase, and interfacial reaction kinetics and dynamics; and molecular modeling of small molecules. Emphasis includes data collection, interpretation, error analysis, and write-up. Recommended background: Fundamentals in chemistry (see CH 1010 – CH 1040), knowledge in thermodynamics (see CH 3510). " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Modern Physical Chemistry Methods" number="2650"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" crn="000000334728334731" max_waitlist="0" number="BL01/BX01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I This laboratory course emphasizes principles, techniques, and instrumentation employed in modern physical chemistry with a view towards applications throughout the molecular sciences. Investigations include chemical thermodynamics and phase equilibria; gas-phase, solution-phase, and interfacial reaction kinetics and dynamics; and molecular modeling of small molecules. Emphasis includes data collection, interpretation, error analysis, and write-up. Recommended background: Fundamentals in chemistry (see CH 1010 – CH 1040), knowledge in thermodynamics (see CH 3510). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" building="" days="fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Ronald Grimm" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ronald Grimm" room="Goddard Hall 307 CH Lab " seats="20" section="BL01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" building="" days="mon,tue,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Ronald Grimm" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ronald Grimm" room="Goddard Hall 306 CH Lab " seats="20" section="BX01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I The emphasis in CH 2660 is on fundamental techniques essential for the synthesis, purification, and characterization of organic compounds. These techniques include setting up, running and monitoring reactions, isolation and purification by solvent extraction, crystallization, distillation, and chromatographic techniques, followed by determination of physical properties and characterization by infrared (IR) and nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy. Micro-synthetic methods and multi-step synthesis are introduced. This course differs from CH 2360 by providing in-depth experience with spectroscopic characterization of molecular structure and hands-on training operating core instruments in addition to methods of organic synthesis. This course is required for students majoring in chemistry, and is recommended for students majoring in biochemistry and disciplines outside of chemistry that desire a strong background in methods of organic synthesis and characterization. Recommended background: Fundamentals of chemistry (see CH 1010, CH 1020, CH 1030) and chemical characterization techniques (see CH1040), basic chemistry laboratory techniques (e.g., basic chemical synthesis, spectral analysis and chemical separation skills). " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Organic Synthesis And Analysis Laboratory" number="2660"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="4" crn="000000336117336118" max_waitlist="0" number="CL01/CX01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I The emphasis in CH 2660 is on fundamental techniques essential for the synthesis, purification, and characterization of organic compounds. These techniques include setting up, running and monitoring reactions, isolation and purification by solvent extraction, crystallization, distillation, and chromatographic techniques, followed by determination of physical properties and characterization by infrared (IR) and nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) spectroscopy. Micro-synthetic methods and multi-step synthesis are introduced. This course differs from CH 2360 by providing in-depth experience with spectroscopic characterization of molecular structure and hands-on training operating core instruments in addition to methods of organic synthesis. This course is required for students majoring in chemistry, and is recommended for students majoring in biochemistry and disciplines outside of chemistry that desire a strong background in methods of organic synthesis and characterization. Recommended background: Fundamentals of chemistry (see CH 1010, CH 1020, CH 1030) and chemical characterization techniques (see CH1040), basic chemistry laboratory techniques (e.g., basic chemical synthesis, spectral analysis and chemical separation skills). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="4" building="" days="fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="John MacDonald" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="John MacDonald" room="Higgins Labs 154 " seats="20" section="CL01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="4" building="" days="mon,tue,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="John MacDonald" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="John MacDonald" room="Goddard Hall 307 CH Lab " seats="20" section="CX01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I The synthesis, isolation, and characterization of inorganic compounds are emphasized. Syntheses of main group compounds, classical transition metal complexes, and organotransition metal compounds are included. In addition to reinforcing and building on standard techniques of synthesis and characterization, several new techniques are introduced: synthesis under inert atmosphere, measurement of magnetic susceptibility by NMR, and cyclic voltammetry. Some exposure to 13C NMR is also provided. The final experiment of the course requires the student to design a synthesis for a compound selected from a list provided, based on strategies learned in the course. Recommended background: Principles of inorganic chemistry, chemical bonding and reactions, thermodynamic stability of inorganic species, solubility and precipitation of inorganic compounds. Advanced chemistry laboratory skills (see CH 2660) " max-credits="0.0" min-credits="0.0" name="Investigation Of Coordination Complexes Through Inquiry" number="2670"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000337223" max_waitlist="999" number="Interest List-D Term" part-of-term="D Term" seats="0" sec_desc=" Cat. I The synthesis, isolation, and characterization of inorganic compounds are emphasized. Syntheses of main group compounds, classical transition metal complexes, and organotransition metal compounds are included. In addition to reinforcing and building on standard techniques of synthesis and characterization, several new techniques are introduced: synthesis under inert atmosphere, measurement of magnetic susceptibility by NMR, and cyclic voltammetry. Some exposure to 13C NMR is also provided. The final experiment of the course requires the student to design a synthesis for a compound selected from a list provided, based on strategies learned in the course. Recommended background: Principles of inorganic chemistry, chemical bonding and reactions, thermodynamic stability of inorganic species, solubility and precipitation of inorganic compounds. Advanced chemistry laboratory skills (see CH 2660) " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="999" professor="N/A" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="N/A" room="Unknown" seats="0" section="Interest List-D Term" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" crn="000000336971337027" max_waitlist="0" number="DL01/DX01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. IThe synthesis, isolation, and characterization of inorganic compounds are emphasized. Syntheses of main group compounds, classical transition metal complexes, and organotransition metal compounds are included. In addition to reinforcing and building on standard techniques of synthesis and characterization, several new techniques are introduced: synthesis under inert atmosphere, measurement of magnetic susceptibility by NMR, and cyclic voltammetry. Some exposure to 13C NMR is also provided. The final experiment of the course requires the student to design a synthesis for a compound selected from a list provided, based on strategies learned in the course.Recommended background: Principles of inorganic chemistry, chemical bonding and reactions, thermodynamic stability of inorganic species, solubility and precipitation of inorganic compounds. Advanced chemistry laboratory skills (see CH 2660) " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" building="" days="wed" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Drew Brodeur" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Drew Brodeur" room="Salisbury Labs 104 " seats="20" section="DL01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" building="" days="mon,tue,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Drew Brodeur" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Drew Brodeur" room="Goddard Hall 307 CH Lab " seats="20" section="DX01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course provides the fundamental understanding of atomic, molecular and solid state structures and properties. Orbital structures of atoms, symmetry of molecules and point groups are used to understand chemical bonding and reactions. Various acid-base concepts are explored to analyze the acidity of cations and basicity of anions, solubility and precipitations of inorganic compounds, and metal-ligand binding affinities. Redox properties are discussed using Pourbaix diagrams. Thermodynamic stabilities of inorganic species are discussed using acid-base and redox concepts and thermochemical analyses are used to analyze chemical reactivity at atomic, molecular, and solid state level. Recommended background: Firm understanding of general chemistry topics (CH 1010 – CH 1030). " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Structure, Bonding, And Reactivity In Inorganic Chemistry" number="3410"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" crn="000000000000334475" max_waitlist="10" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course provides the fundamental understanding of atomic, molecular and solid state structures and properties. Orbital structures of atoms, symmetry of molecules and point groups are used to understand chemical bonding and reactions. Various acid-base concepts are explored to analyze the acidity of cations and basicity of anions, solubility and precipitations of inorganic compounds, and metal-ligand binding affinities. Redox properties are discussed using Pourbaix diagrams. Thermodynamic stabilities of inorganic species are discussed using acid-base and redox concepts and thermochemical analyses are used to analyze chemical reactivity at atomic, molecular, and solid state level. Recommended background: Firm understanding of general chemistry topics (CH 1010 – CH 1030). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Shawn Burdette" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Shawn Burdette" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="50" section="A01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I The content of this course will be the development of the principles of classical thermodynamics. The laws of thermodynamics will be developed by using a series of increasingly complex model systems and a universal equation of state is formulated which incorporates the relationships illustrated by these model systems. Using this equation it will be possible to appreciate that thermodynamic laws are applicable to all systems of matter, regardless of their complexity. Finally, the principles developed are applied to problems of a chemical nature, focusing on predicting the spontaneity of chemical reactions. The material in this course will be of greatest interest to those students enrolled in the basic sciences including biology, chemistry, and physics, and in applied fields such as chemical engineering, materials science and biotechnology. Recommended background: Students should be familiar with the material covered in the general chemistry sequence CH 1010-1040, and calculus including multi variables. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Chemical Thermodynamics" number="3510"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" crn="000000000000334163" max_waitlist="0" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. I The content of this course will be the development of the principles of classical thermodynamics. The laws of thermodynamics will be developed by using a series of increasingly complex model systems and a universal equation of state is formulated which incorporates the relationships illustrated by these model systems. Using this equation it will be possible to appreciate that thermodynamic laws are applicable to all systems of matter, regardless of their complexity. Finally, the principles developed are applied to problems of a chemical nature, focusing on predicting the spontaneity of chemical reactions. The material in this course will be of greatest interest to those students enrolled in the basic sciences including biology, chemistry, and physics, and in applied fields such as chemical engineering, materials science and biotechnology. Recommended background: Students should be familiar with the material covered in the general chemistry sequence CH 1010-1040, and calculus including multi variables. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Christopher Lambert" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Christopher Lambert" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="60" section="A01" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I An introduction to quantum mechanics with applications to atomic and molecular species. The course will be developed systematically beginning with the postulates of quantum mechanics. The Schroedinger equation will be applied to systems such as the particle in a box, the rigid rotor, the harmonic oscillator and the hydrogen atom. Emphasis will be given to a quantum mechanical description of multielectron atoms, molecular bonding and spectroscopy. Recommended background: a solid foundation in elementary physics and calculus. This course is normally for students in their third year. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Quantum Chemistry" number="3530"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" crn="000000000000336121" max_waitlist="10" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" Cat. I An introduction to quantum mechanics with applications to atomic and molecular species. The course will be developed systematically beginning with the postulates of quantum mechanics. The Schroedinger equation will be applied to systems such as the particle in a box, the rigid rotor, the harmonic oscillator and the hydrogen atom. Emphasis will be given to a quantum mechanical description of multielectron atoms, molecular bonding and spectroscopy. Recommended background: a solid foundation in elementary physics and calculus. This course is normally for students in their third year. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="George Kaminski" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="George Kaminski" room="Atwater Kent 232 " seats="30" section="C01" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course deals in a general way with the interactions between energy and molecules, and considers how energetic and structural considerations affect the outcome of molecular interactions. The manipulation of kinetic data and results is stressed. Selected topics from both organic and inorganic chemistry are analyzed in terms of reaction thermodynamics, rates and mechanisms. Students are expected to be familiar with thermodynamics, equilibria, reaction rates and the Periodic Table of the elements.  The following three courses, CH 4110, CH 4120, and CH 4130, are a three-term sequence intended to provide a strong emphasis in biochemistry. As background for this sequence, CH 1010, CH 1020, CH 1030, CH 1040, CH 2310, CH 2320, and CH 2330, or their equivalents, are recommended. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Chemical Dynamics" number="3550"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="6" crn="000000334733335469" max_waitlist="0" number="BL01/BD01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course deals in a general way with the interactions between energy and molecules, and considers how energetic and structural considerations affect the outcome of molecular interactions. The manipulation of kinetic data and results is stressed. Selected topics from both organic and inorganic chemistry are analyzed in terms of reaction thermodynamics, rates and mechanisms. Students are expected to be familiar with thermodynamics, equilibria, reaction rates and the Periodic Table of the elements.  The following three courses, CH 4110, CH 4120, and CH 4130, are a three-term sequence intended to provide a strong emphasis in biochemistry. As background for this sequence, CH 1010, CH 1020, CH 1030, CH 1040, CH 2310, CH 2320, and CH 2330, or their equivalents, are recommended. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="6" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="George Kaminski" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="George Kaminski" room="Higgins Labs 202 " seats="30" section="BL01" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="6" building="" days="wed" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="George Kaminski" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="George Kaminski" room="Higgins Labs 202 " seats="30" section="BD01" starts="10:00AM" type="Discussion"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I The fundamental concepts of protein architecture and dynamics are presented with an emphasis on the functional outcomes of chemistry coordinated in three dimensional space. Catalytic mechanics and enzyme function are outlined in detail. Current methods in the determination of enzyme structure and function will be discussed, and students will use common tools in macromolecular analysis and structural modeling. Case studies in enzyme dysfunction, disease, and current research will be used throughout the course. Recommended background: Familiarity with organic chemistry topics including functional groups, nucleophilic addition and substitution reactions, stereochemistry, and carbonyl chemistry. General knowledge of cellular architecture is also recommended. See CH 2310, CH 2320, CH 2330, and BB2550 or equivalent. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Protein Structure And Function" number="4110"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="12" crn="000000000000334164" max_waitlist="0" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="75" sec_desc=" Cat. I The fundamental concepts of protein architecture and dynamics are presented with an emphasis on the functional outcomes of chemistry coordinated in three dimensional space. Catalytic mechanics and enzyme function are outlined in detail. Current methods in the determination of enzyme structure and function will be discussed, and students will use common tools in macromolecular analysis and structural modeling. Case studies in enzyme dysfunction, disease, and current research will be used throughout the course. Recommended background: Familiarity with organic chemistry topics including functional groups, nucleophilic addition and substitution reactions, stereochemistry, and carbonyl chemistry. General knowledge of cellular architecture is also recommended. See CH 2310, CH 2320, CH 2330, and BB2550 or equivalent. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="12" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Destin Heilman" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Destin Heilman" room="Atwater Kent 116 Newell Hall " seats="75" section="A01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I Oriented around biological membranes, this course begins with a description of lipids and proteins forming biomembranes. Permeability and the mechanism of transmembrane mass transport are presented. Transport of electrons and redox equivalents is explained within the context of aerobic production of ATP and plant photosynthesis. Finally the transport of information across biomembranes in signal transduction and neurotransmission are discussed. Recommended background: Knowledge of organic chemistry fundamentals as well as concepts including protein structure and folding, catalytic mechanics, enzyme kinetics, and ATP synthesis and hydrolysis mechanisms. See CH 2310, BB 2550, and CH 4110 or equivalent. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Lipids And Biomembrane Functions" number="4120"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" crn="000000000000341252" max_waitlist="15" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat. I Oriented around biological membranes, this course begins with a description of lipids and proteins forming biomembranes. Permeability and the mechanism of transmembrane mass transport are presented. Transport of electrons and redox equivalents is explained within the context of aerobic production of ATP and plant photosynthesis. Finally the transport of information across biomembranes in signal transduction and neurotransmission are discussed. Recommended background: Knowledge of organic chemistry fundamentals as well as concepts including protein structure and folding, catalytic mechanics, enzyme kinetics, and ATP synthesis and hydrolysis mechanisms. See CH 2310, BB 2550, and CH 4110 or equivalent. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Aya Narunsky" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Aya Narunsky" room="Salisbury Labs 104 " seats="50" section="B01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course presents the structure and function of DNA. Precursors and biomolecules that give rise to DNA, the mechanism of DNA replication, RNA synthesis, and protein synthesis are described in detail. In addition to mechanistic studies, regulation of these processes is covered as well as those of genetic mutation, DNA repair, and epigenetics. Recommended background: Knowledge of organic chemistry fundamentals as well as concepts including protein structure and folding, catalytic mechanics, enzyme kinetics, and ATP synthesis and hydrolysis mechanisms. See CH 2310, CH 2320, CH 2330, BB 2550, and CH 4110 or equivalent. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Nucleic Acids And Bioinformation" number="4130"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="17" crn="000000000000341716" max_waitlist="15" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course presents the structure and function of DNA. Precursors and biomolecules that give rise to DNA, the mechanism of DNA replication, RNA synthesis, and protein synthesis are described in detail. In addition to mechanistic studies, regulation of these processes is covered as well as those of genetic mutation, DNA repair, and epigenetics. Recommended background: Knowledge of organic chemistry fundamentals as well as concepts including protein structure and folding, catalytic mechanics, enzyme kinetics, and ATP synthesis and hydrolysis mechanisms. See CH 2310, CH 2320, CH 2330, BB 2550, and CH 4110 or equivalent. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="17" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Robert Dempski" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Robert Dempski" room="Atwater Kent 219 " seats="50" section="C01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course presents a thorough analysis of the most relevant metabolic processes in cells. The catabolism of sugars and lipids will be presented in the context of energy generation and storage. Nucleotide and amino acid metabolism will discussed as building blocks for large biomolecules. Throughout the course the links between metabolism, hereditary pathologies, as well as risk of metabolic imbalances such as diabetes and obesity will be presented.Recommended background: Familiarity with organic chemistry topics including functional groups, nucleophilic addition and substitution reactions, stereochemistry, and carbonyl chemistry. General knowledge of cellular architecture is also recommended. See CH 2310, CH 2320, CH 2330, and BB2550 or equivalent.Specific concepts that we will discuss are: Glucose and glycogen metabolism; Gluconeogenesis; Citric Acid Cycle; Lipid, amino acid and nucleotide metabolisms; Mammalian Fuel Metabolism: Integration and Regulation. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Metabolism And Disease" number="4140"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" crn="000000000000336972" max_waitlist="15" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="54" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course presents a thorough analysis of the most relevant metabolic processes in cells. The catabolism of sugars and lipids will be presented in the context of energy generation and storage. Nucleotide and amino acid metabolism will discussed as building blocks for large biomolecules. Throughout the course the links between metabolism, hereditary pathologies, as well as risk of metabolic imbalances such as diabetes and obesity will be presented.Recommended background: Familiarity with organic chemistry topics including functional groups, nucleophilic addition and substitution reactions, stereochemistry, and carbonyl chemistry. General knowledge of cellular architecture is also recommended. See CH 2310, CH 2320, CH 2330, and BB2550 or equivalent.Specific concepts that we will discuss are: Glucose and glycogen metabolism; Gluconeogenesis; Citric Acid Cycle; Lipid, amino acid and nucleotide metabolisms; Mammalian Fuel Metabolism: Integration and Regulation. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Carissa Olsen" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Carissa Olsen" room="Unity Hall 520 " seats="54" section="D01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I The experiments in this laboratory course have been designed to acquaint the students with the basic skills necessary to perform biochemical studies. The course will cover, for instance, protein purification, subcellular fractionation, enzyme kinetics (Km, Vmax, specific activity, effector-protein interaction, etc.), exclusion and ion exchange chromatography, and electrophoresis. Recommended background: Knowledge of organic chemistry fundamentals as well as concepts including protein structure and folding, catalytic mechanics, enzyme kinetics, and ATP synthesis and hydrolysis mechanisms. See CH 2310, BB 2550, and CH 4110 or equivalent. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Enzymology And Protein Characterization Laboratory" number="4150"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" crn="000000335125335114" max_waitlist="0" number="BL01/BX01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I The experiments in this laboratory course have been designed to acquaint the students with the basic skills necessary to perform biochemical studies. The course will cover, for instance, protein purification, subcellular fractionation, enzyme kinetics (Km, Vmax, specific activity, effector-protein interaction, etc.), exclusion and ion exchange chromatography, and electrophoresis. Recommended background: Knowledge of organic chemistry fundamentals as well as concepts including protein structure and folding, catalytic mechanics, enzyme kinetics, and ATP synthesis and hydrolysis mechanisms. See CH 2310, BB 2550, and CH 4110 or equivalent. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Destin Heilman" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Destin Heilman" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="20" section="BL01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" building="" days="mon" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Destin Heilman" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Destin Heilman" room="Goddard Hall 307 CH Lab " seats="20" section="BX01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lab"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" building="" days="wed,fri" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Destin Heilman" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Destin Heilman" room=" Goddard Hall 306 CH Lab " seats="20" section="BX01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" crn="000000336437336125" max_waitlist="5" number="CL01/CX01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I The experiments in this laboratory course have been designed to acquaint the students with the basic skills necessary to perform biochemical studies. The course will cover, for instance, protein purification, subcellular fractionation, enzyme kinetics (Km, Vmax, specific activity, effector-protein interaction, etc.), exclusion and ion exchange chromatography, and electrophoresis. Recommended background: Knowledge of organic chemistry fundamentals as well as concepts including protein structure and folding, catalytic mechanics, enzyme kinetics, and ATP synthesis and hydrolysis mechanisms. See CH 2310, BB 2550, and CH 4110 or equivalent. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Ceren Yilmaz Akkaya" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ceren Yilmaz Akkaya" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="20" section="CL01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" building="" days="mon,wed,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Ceren Yilmaz Akkaya" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ceren Yilmaz Akkaya" room="Goddard Hall 306 CH Lab " seats="20" section="CX01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" crn="000000341238341383" max_waitlist="0" number="DL01/DX01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="24" sec_desc=" Cat. I The experiments in this laboratory course have been designed to acquaint the students with the basic skills necessary to perform biochemical studies. The course will cover, for instance, protein purification, subcellular fractionation, enzyme kinetics (Km, Vmax, specific activity, effector-protein interaction, etc.), exclusion and ion exchange chromatography, and electrophoresis. Recommended background: Knowledge of organic chemistry fundamentals as well as concepts including protein structure and folding, catalytic mechanics, enzyme kinetics, and ATP synthesis and hydrolysis mechanisms. See CH 2310, BB 2550, and CH 4110 or equivalent. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" building="" days="wed" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Ceren Yilmaz Akkaya" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ceren Yilmaz Akkaya" room="Atwater Kent 232 " seats="24" section="DL01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" building="" days="mon,wed,thu" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Ceren Yilmaz Akkaya" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ceren Yilmaz Akkaya" room="Goddard Hall 306 CH Lab " seats="24" section="DX01" starts="8:00AM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This laboratory course focuses on modern DNA technologies and general applications of gene manipulation. Topics include gene amplification and recombination, promoter and plasmid engineering, gene expression and analysis, model systems, CRISPR, genomics and transgenics. Experiments in this course are integrated into an overall genetic engineering project throughout the term that will involve techniques such as electrophoresis, quantitative spectrofluorimetry, and real-time quantitative PCR. Methods of data analysis, common statistical approaches and technical writing will be emphasized throughout the course. Recommended background: Knowledge of organic chemistry fundamentals as well as biochemical concepts including DNA replication and recombination, RNA synthesis and protein synthesis. Familiarity with cellular architecture is also recommended. See CH 2310, BB 2550, BB 4010 and CH 4110 or equivalent. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Experimental Genetic Engineering" number="4170"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" crn="000000335275335426" max_waitlist="0" number="BL01/BX01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I This laboratory course focuses on modern DNA technologies and general applications of gene manipulation. Topics include gene amplification and recombination, promoter and plasmid engineering, gene expression and analysis, model systems, CRISPR, genomics and transgenics. Experiments in this course are integrated into an overall genetic engineering project throughout the term that will involve techniques such as electrophoresis, quantitative spectrofluorimetry, and real-time quantitative PCR. Methods of data analysis, common statistical approaches and technical writing will be emphasized throughout the course. Recommended background: Knowledge of organic chemistry fundamentals as well as biochemical concepts including DNA replication and recombination, RNA synthesis and protein synthesis. Familiarity with cellular architecture is also recommended. See CH 2310, BB 2550, BB 4010 and CH 4110 or equivalent. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" building="" days="mon" ends="8:50AM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Ceren Yilmaz Akkaya" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ceren Yilmaz Akkaya" room="Olin Hall 109 " seats="20" section="BL01" starts="8:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" building="" days="tue,wed,thu" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Ceren Yilmaz Akkaya" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ceren Yilmaz Akkaya" room="Goddard Hall 306 CH Lab " seats="20" section="BX01" starts="8:00AM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" crn="000000338211338210" max_waitlist="0" number="DL01/DX01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I This laboratory course focuses on modern DNA technologies and general applications of gene manipulation. Topics include gene amplification and recombination, promoter and plasmid engineering, gene expression and analysis, model systems, CRISPR, genomics and transgenics. Experiments in this course are integrated into an overall genetic engineering project throughout the term that will involve techniques such as electrophoresis, quantitative spectrofluorimetry, and real-time quantitative PCR. Methods of data analysis, common statistical approaches and technical writing will be emphasized throughout the course. Recommended background: Knowledge of organic chemistry fundamentals as well as biochemical concepts including DNA replication and recombination, RNA synthesis and protein synthesis. Familiarity with cellular architecture is also recommended. See CH 2310, BB 2550, BB 4010 and CH 4110 or equivalent. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" building="" days="mon" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Destin Heilman" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Destin Heilman" room="Washburn 323 " seats="20" section="DL01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" building="" days="tue,wed,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Destin Heilman" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Destin Heilman" room="Goddard Hall 306 CH Lab " seats="20" section="DX01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I  Through lectures, problem sets, reading and discussion, and presentations this course will help elucidate for students the processes that allow regulated gene expression, mechanisms used in each type of regulation, and methods and techniques used for investigation of regulatory mechanisms . Readings from the current original research literature will explore the growing use of model systems and “omics” level approaches to enhance our ever expanding understanding of the gene regulatory mechanisms. The development of cell-based therapeutics and genetic engineering as they relate to gene regulation will be introduced.  Recommended background: a working knowledge of concepts in biochemistry and molecular genetics (CH 4110 and 4120, or equivalent, and CH 4130 or BB 3950 or equivalent)   " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Regulation Of Gene Expression" number="4190"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" crn="000000000000340931" max_waitlist="15" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="40" sec_desc=" Cat. I  Through lectures, problem sets, reading and discussion, and presentations this course will help elucidate for students the processes that allow regulated gene expression, mechanisms used in each type of regulation, and methods and techniques used for investigation of regulatory mechanisms . Readings from the current original research literature will explore the growing use of model systems and “omics” level approaches to enhance our ever expanding understanding of the gene regulatory mechanisms. The development of cell-based therapeutics and genetic engineering as they relate to gene regulation will be introduced.  Recommended background: a working knowledge of concepts in biochemistry and molecular genetics (CH 4110 and 4120, or equivalent, and CH 4130 or BB 3950 or equivalent)   " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Natalie Farny" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Natalie Farny" room="Higgins Labs 218 Discovery Classroom " seats="40" section="D01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000337303" max_waitlist="0" number="" part-of-term="D Term" seats="0" sec_desc=" Cat. I  Through lectures, problem sets, reading and discussion, and presentations this course will help elucidate for students the processes that allow regulated gene expression, mechanisms used in each type of regulation, and methods and techniques used for investigation of regulatory mechanisms . Readings from the current original research literature will explore the growing use of model systems and “omics” level approaches to enhance our ever expanding understanding of the gene regulatory mechanisms. The development of cell-based therapeutics and genetic engineering as they relate to gene regulation will be introduced.  Recommended background: a working knowledge of concepts in biochemistry and molecular genetics (CH 4110 and 4120, or equivalent, and CH 4130 or BB 3950 or equivalent)   " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Not Assigned" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Not Assigned" room="Unknown" seats="0" section="" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc="CH421X Applications of AI in Life Sciences Molecular Studies  &#10;This course is designed to introduce cutting-edge applications of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the field of molecular studies within the life sciences. As technology continues to advance, AI has emerged as a powerful tool in revolutionizing the way we analyze and interpret molecular data, enabling breakthroughs in areas such as genomics, proteomics, and drug discovery.  &#10;Recommended Background. Students are encouraged to have a fundamental understanding of chemistry and biochemistry topics before taking this class. &#10;This is proposed to be a category III course that we will first offer in A term of the 24-25AY." max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Applications of AI in Life Sciences Molecular Studies" number="421X"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" crn="000000000000333950" max_waitlist="5" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="20" sec_desc="CH421X Applications of AI in Life Sciences Molecular Studies  &#10;This course is designed to introduce cutting-edge applications of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the field of molecular studies within the life sciences. As technology continues to advance, AI has emerged as a powerful tool in revolutionizing the way we analyze and interpret molecular data, enabling breakthroughs in areas such as genomics, proteomics, and drug discovery.  &#10;Recommended Background. Students are encouraged to have a fundamental understanding of chemistry and biochemistry topics before taking this class. &#10;This is proposed to be a category III course that we will first offer in A term of the 24-25AY." term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Robert Dempski" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Robert Dempski" room="Stratton Hall 202 (new) " seats="20" section="A01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I  The principles and applications of group theory as a tool in chemistry are presented with an emphasis on systems in inorganic chemistry. Topics covered include the development of symmetry group representations and character tables, applications of group theory in quantum-mechanical wavefunctions, molecular vibrations, ligand field theory, and molecular orbital theory. Particular emphasis will be placed on transition metal complexes, including classical coordination complexes, metal carbonyl complexes, and organotransition metal complexes. Recommended Background: CH 1010 - CH 1040, CH 2640 - CH 2670, CH 3410, CH 3530, and CH 3550, and a fundamental understanding of atomic, molecular and solid state structures and properties. Thermodynamic stabilities of inorganic species. Acidity, solubility and precipitation of inorganic compounds (see CH3410). " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Principles and Applications of Group Theory in Chemistry" number="4420"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" crn="000000000000336784" max_waitlist="10" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I  The principles and applications of group theory as a tool in chemistry are presented with an emphasis on systems in inorganic chemistry. Topics covered include the development of symmetry group representations and character tables, applications of group theory in quantum-mechanical wavefunctions, molecular vibrations, ligand field theory, and molecular orbital theory. Particular emphasis will be placed on transition metal complexes, including classical coordination complexes, metal carbonyl complexes, and organotransition metal complexes. Recommended Background: CH 1010 - CH 1040, CH 2640 - CH 2670, CH 3410, CH 3530, and CH 3550, and a fundamental understanding of atomic, molecular and solid state structures and properties. Thermodynamic stabilities of inorganic species. Acidity, solubility and precipitation of inorganic compounds (see CH3410). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Bruce Bursten" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Bruce Bursten" room="Kaven Hall 115 " seats="20" section="C01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. II This course deals with how the electronic, translational, rotational and vibrational energy levels of individual molecules, or of macromolecular systems, are statistically related to the energy, entropy, and free energy of macroscopic systems, taking into account the quantum mechanical properties of the component particles. Ensembles, partition functions, and Boltzmann, Fermi-Dirac and Bose-Einstein statistics are used. A wealth of physical chemical phenomena, including material related to solids, liquids, gases, spectroscopy and chemical reactions are made understandable by the concepts learned in this course.  Recommended background: CH 3510 and CH 3530, or equivalent, and mathematics through differential and integral calculus. This course will be offered in 2019-20, and in alternating years thereafter. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Chemical Statistical Mechanics" number="4520"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="23" crn="000000000000338205" max_waitlist="10" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="24" sec_desc=" Cat. II This course deals with how the electronic, translational, rotational and vibrational energy levels of individual molecules, or of macromolecular systems, are statistically related to the energy, entropy, and free energy of macroscopic systems, taking into account the quantum mechanical properties of the component particles. Ensembles, partition functions, and Boltzmann, Fermi-Dirac and Bose-Einstein statistics are used. A wealth of physical chemical phenomena, including material related to solids, liquids, gases, spectroscopy and chemical reactions are made understandable by the concepts learned in this course.  Recommended background: CH 3510 and CH 3530, or equivalent, and mathematics through differential and integral calculus. This course will be offered in 2019-20, and in alternating years thereafter. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="23" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="George Kaminski" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="George Kaminski" room="Olin Hall 218 " seats="24" section="D01" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" The emphasis is on using a variety of spectroscopic data to arrive at molecular structures, particularly of organic molecules. Major emphasis is on Hand C-NMR, IR and MS. There is relatively little emphasis on theory or on sampling handling techniques. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Fundamentals and Applications of Chemical Spectroscopies" number="516"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="17" crn="000000000000341235" max_waitlist="5" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" The emphasis is on using a variety of spectroscopic data to arrive at molecular structures, particularly of organic molecules. Major emphasis is on Hand C-NMR, IR and MS. There is relatively little emphasis on theory or on sampling handling techniques. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="17" building="" days="wed" ends="5:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Ronald Grimm" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ronald Grimm" room="Olin Hall 126 " seats="20" section="C01" starts="4:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="17" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="5:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Ronald Grimm" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ronald Grimm" room=" Olin Hall 126 " seats="20" section="C01" starts="4:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cell signaling defines the way cells respond to changes in their environment including, heat, nutrients, drugs, hormones, and other factors. These external factors allow cells to grow, divide, migrate and proliferate depending on the stimulus, and inappropriate responses lead to cancer and other diseases. This course is directed for advanced undergraduates and graduate level course that is a combination of on-line lectures, discussions, and review of recent literature. Students who previously took the CH 555 version of this course cannot take CH 520 for credit. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Cell Signaling" number="520"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="27" crn="000000000000337131" max_waitlist="5" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" Cell signaling defines the way cells respond to changes in their environment including, heat, nutrients, drugs, hormones, and other factors. These external factors allow cells to grow, divide, migrate and proliferate depending on the stimulus, and inappropriate responses lead to cancer and other diseases. This course is directed for advanced undergraduates and graduate level course that is a combination of on-line lectures, discussions, and review of recent literature. Students who previously took the CH 555 version of this course cannot take CH 520 for credit. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="27" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="8:50AM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Suzanne Scarlata" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Suzanne Scarlata" room="Salisbury Labs 407 " seats="30" section="D01" starts="8:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="27" crn="000000000000337271" max_waitlist="0" number="D02" part-of-term="D Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" Cell signaling defines the way cells respond to changes in their environment including, heat, nutrients, drugs, hormones, and other factors. These external factors allow cells to grow, divide, migrate and proliferate depending on the stimulus, and inappropriate responses lead to cancer and other diseases. This course is directed for advanced undergraduates and graduate level course that is a combination of on-line lectures, discussions, and review of recent literature. Students who previously took the CH 555 version of this course cannot take CH 520 for credit. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="27" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Suzanne Scarlata" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Suzanne Scarlata" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="30" section="D02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course will focus on the medicinal chemistry aspects of drug discovery from an industrial pharmaceutical research and development perspective. Topics will include chemotherapeutic agents (such as antibacterial, antiviral and antitumor agents) and pharmacodynamic agents (such as antihypertensive, antiallergic, antiulcer and CNS agents). (Prerequisite: A good foundation in organic chemistry, e.g., CH 2310 Organic Chemistry I and CH 2320 Organic Chemistry II.) " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Medicinal Chemistry" number="538"><section actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="21" crn="000000000000340942" max_waitlist="10" number="S01" part-of-term="C Term, D Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" This course will focus on the medicinal chemistry aspects of drug discovery from an industrial pharmaceutical research and development perspective. Topics will include chemotherapeutic agents (such as antibacterial, antiviral and antitumor agents) and pharmacodynamic agents (such as antihypertensive, antiallergic, antiulcer and CNS agents). (Prerequisite: A good foundation in organic chemistry, e.g., CH 2310 Organic Chemistry I and CH 2320 Organic Chemistry II.) " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="21" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="James Stambuli" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="James Stambuli" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="30" section="S01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course covers the biochemical mechanisms involved in regulation of gene expression: modifications of DNA structures that influence transcription rates, transcriptional regulation, post-transcriptional processing of RNA including splicing and editing, nuclear/cytoplasmic transport, regulation of translation, and factors that control the half-lives of both mRNA and protein. During the course, common experimental methods are explored, including a discussion of the information available from each method. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Regulation Of Gene Expression" number="540"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" crn="000000000000337318" max_waitlist="0" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="10" sec_desc=" This course covers the biochemical mechanisms involved in regulation of gene expression: modifications of DNA structures that influence transcription rates, transcriptional regulation, post-transcriptional processing of RNA including splicing and editing, nuclear/cytoplasmic transport, regulation of translation, and factors that control the half-lives of both mRNA and protein. During the course, common experimental methods are explored, including a discussion of the information available from each method. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Natalie Farny" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Natalie Farny" room="Other " seats="10" section="D01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This class will introduce the concepts of basic neuropharmacology and the action of major neurotransmitter families in the brain. The mechanisms of action of the major psychoactive drugs families including cannabis, opioids, and psychedelics will be covered. The effects of pharmaceutical treatments for anxiety and depression on brain chemistry will be discussed as well. This course will be offered in 2020-2021 and alternating years thereafter. Recommended Background: Fundamental understanding of introductory biochemistry (CH4110, CH4120, and/or CH4130). " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Drugs In The Brain" number="542"><section actual_waitlist="2" availableseats="13" crn="000000000000335258" max_waitlist="10" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" This class will introduce the concepts of basic neuropharmacology and the action of major neurotransmitter families in the brain. The mechanisms of action of the major psychoactive drugs families including cannabis, opioids, and psychedelics will be covered. The effects of pharmaceutical treatments for anxiety and depression on brain chemistry will be discussed as well. This course will be offered in 2020-2021 and alternating years thereafter. Recommended Background: Fundamental understanding of introductory biochemistry (CH4110, CH4120, and/or CH4130). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="2" availableseats="13" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Carissa Olsen" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Carissa Olsen" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="30" section="B01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" In this advanced course, students will develop skill sets to independently understand, explain, and predict reactions of organic molecules. Principles of modern physical organic chemistry, such as bonding, hybridization, molecular orbital theory, non-covalent interactions, stereochemistry, and conformational analysis, will be introduced alongside experimental techniques related to thermodynamics and kinetics to provide scientists with tools to understand existing reaction pathways and study new reactions. The interplay between mechanistic hypotheses and experimental observations will be demonstrated using case studies from the primary literature. Recommended for graduate students and undergraduates who have completed the sequence in organic chemistry (CH2310, CH2320, and CH2330). " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Elucidation Of Organic Reaction Mechanisms" number="544"><section actual_waitlist="2" availableseats="20" crn="000000000000338201" max_waitlist="5" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" In this advanced course, students will develop skill sets to independently understand, explain, and predict reactions of organic molecules. Principles of modern physical organic chemistry, such as bonding, hybridization, molecular orbital theory, non-covalent interactions, stereochemistry, and conformational analysis, will be introduced alongside experimental techniques related to thermodynamics and kinetics to provide scientists with tools to understand existing reaction pathways and study new reactions. The interplay between mechanistic hypotheses and experimental observations will be demonstrated using case studies from the primary literature. Recommended for graduate students and undergraduates who have completed the sequence in organic chemistry (CH2310, CH2320, and CH2330). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="2" availableseats="20" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Anita Mattson" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Anita Mattson" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="30" section="B01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course trains students in the area of molecular modeling using a variety of quantum mechanical and force field methods. The approach will be toward practical applications, for researchers who want to answer specific questions about molecular geometry, transition states, reaction paths and photoexcited states. No experience in programming is necessary; however, a background at the introductory level in quantum mechanics is highly desirable. Methods to be explored include density functional theory, ab initio methods, semiempirical molecular orbital theory, and visualization software for the graphical display of molecules. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Molecular Modeling" number="554"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" crn="000000000000333961" max_waitlist="10" number="AL01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" This course trains students in the area of molecular modeling using a variety of quantum mechanical and force field methods. The approach will be toward practical applications, for researchers who want to answer specific questions about molecular geometry, transition states, reaction paths and photoexcited states. No experience in programming is necessary; however, a background at the introductory level in quantum mechanics is highly desirable. Methods to be explored include density functional theory, ab initio methods, semiempirical molecular orbital theory, and visualization software for the graphical display of molecules. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="N. Aaron Deskins" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="N. Aaron Deskins" room="Higgins Labs 230 Geom Modeling Computer Lab " seats="25" section="AL01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" A course of advanced study in selected areas whose content and format varies to suit the interest and needs of faculty and students.  This course may be repeated for different topics covered. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Advanced Topics" number="555"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" crn="000000000000333855" max_waitlist="5" number="A01 - ST: AI in Molecular Life Sciences" part-of-term="A Term" seats="20" sec_desc="A course of advanced study in selected areas whose content and format varies to suit the interest and needs of faculty and students.&#10;This course may be repeated for different topics covered." term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Robert Dempski" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Robert Dempski" room="Stratton Hall 202 (new) " seats="20" section="A01 - ST: AI in Molecular Life Sciences" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" crn="000000000000341250" max_waitlist="5" number="B01 - ST: Metals in Biology" part-of-term="B Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" A course of advanced study in selected areas whose content and format varies to suit the interest and needs of faculty and students.  This course may be repeated for different topics covered. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" building="" days="mon,wed" ends="6:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Shawn Burdette" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Shawn Burdette" room="Higgins Labs 114 " seats="20" section="B01 - ST: Metals in Biology" starts="5:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" crn="000000000000338212" max_waitlist="10" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="24" sec_desc=" A course of advanced study in selected areas whose content and format varies to suit the interest and needs of faculty and students.  This course may be repeated for different topics covered. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="John MacDonald" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="John MacDonald" room="Salisbury Labs 105 " seats="24" section="D01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="28" crn="000000000000339704" max_waitlist="10" number="D02 - AT: Introduction to Sensor Science and Technology" part-of-term="D Term" seats="30" sec_desc="  This course is designed for students to attain a broad and in-depth acquaintance with the mechanism, platform and targets of various sensors, with a focus on biosensors and chemical sensors. Topical concepts covered include: sensor components and characteristics; synthetic, biological and biomimetic materials used in sensor development; miniaturization and nanotechnology involved in sensor fabrication; various sensing modalities, data analysis and device prototyping. Students will be required to partner with others and consult with academic and industrial scientists to design sensors of their own interests as a final project for assessment.  " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="28" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Rong Wang" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Rong Wang" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="30" section="D02 - AT: Introduction to Sensor Science and Technology" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000340289" max_waitlist="10" number="F02 - AT: Plant Medicinal Chemistry" part-of-term="A Term, B Term" seats="22" sec_desc="   This course explores advanced concepts and state-of-the-art techniques in medicinal plant   chemistry. Lectures provide an in-depth examination of bioactive compounds, their plant   origins, secondary metabolite chemistry, the connections between primary and secondary   metabolism, and the historical and cultural significance of medicinal plants. The goal is to   develop a broad understanding and practical skills in medicinal plant chemistry and biology   research. Students will gain evidence-based scientific knowledge and enhance their research   and developmental skills.       Learning Objectives:   • Develop a strong sense of competence, confidence, and responsibility as a chemist.   • Apply critical, creative, and practical thinking to problems related to medicinal plant   chemistry.   • Formulate and test hypotheses using the scientific method to explore medicinal plant   chemistry.   • Educate and inform others about the significance of medicinal plant chemistry in both   personal and public contexts.   • Appreciate the historical, current, and future potential of plant-derived medicines.   • Identify and critically evaluate scientific literature and other key sources to support   ongoing learning in the diverse and rapidly evolving field of medicinal plant chemistry.   • Critically review and assess current evidence for the safety and efficacy of medicinal   plants for specification, medicinal properties, and possible application(s)   " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Ara Kirakosyan" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ara Kirakosyan" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="22" section="F02 - AT: Plant Medicinal Chemistry" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="24" crn="000000000000340248" max_waitlist="10" number="S01 - AT: Cannabis Chemistry" part-of-term="C Term, D Term" seats="30" sec_desc="   The Cannabis Chemistry course delves into the chemical compounds found in the cannabis plant,   their interactions, and their medicinal applications. Students will learn to determine and quantify   cannabinoids and terpenes, and assess their therapeutic potential. This course prepares students   for careers in the cannabis industry or further academic research.       Learning Outcomes   1. Explore the Chemical Composition of Cannabis: Students will identify and describe the major   chemical compounds in cannabis, elucidate their biosynthesis pathways, and understand their   roles in medicine.   2. Analyze Cannabinoids: Students will develop skills to extract, analyze, and quantify   cannabinoids using various chemical techniques. They will perform chromatographic and   spectroscopic analyses to determine the purity and concentration of cannabinoids in different   samples.   " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="24" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Ara Kirakosyan" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ara Kirakosyan" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="30" section="S01 - AT: Cannabis Chemistry" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" How do Food and Drug Administration (FDA) regulations and guidelines drive the drugdevelopment pathway for small-molecule products? We will outline the guidelines and regulations for pharmaceutical products including requirements for non-Good Laboratory Practice (GLP) and GLP safety studies, Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism and Excretion (ADME), Pharmacokinetic-Pharmacodynamic (PK/PD), and other pharmacokinetic and toxicological studies to establish the safety and efficacy of drugs as dictated by FDA regulations. Students will learn how to utilize safety (NOEL/”No Observed Effect Level” and NOAEL/”No Observed Adverse Effect Level”), efficacy, and pharmacokinetic studies to determine first in human doses and a therapeutic index, and requirements for Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP). Students will also learn about small, niche FDA regulatory pathways to include the animal rule (in which humans cannot be included in efficacy studies for safety reasons)/animal model development through discussions of biological warfare and outbreak pathogen therapeutic development. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Drug Development Pathways for Therapeutics" number="562"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" crn="000000000000345760" max_waitlist="10" number="F01" part-of-term="A Term, B Term" seats="24" sec_desc=" How do Food and Drug Administration (FDA) regulations and guidelines drive the drugdevelopment pathway for small-molecule products? We will outline the guidelines and regulations for pharmaceutical products including requirements for non-Good Laboratory Practice (GLP) and GLP safety studies, Absorption, Distribution, Metabolism and Excretion (ADME), Pharmacokinetic-Pharmacodynamic (PK/PD), and other pharmacokinetic and toxicological studies to establish the safety and efficacy of drugs as dictated by FDA regulations. Students will learn how to utilize safety (NOEL/”No Observed Effect Level” and NOAEL/”No Observed Adverse Effect Level”), efficacy, and pharmacokinetic studies to determine first in human doses and a therapeutic index, and requirements for Good Manufacturing Practices (GMP). Students will also learn about small, niche FDA regulatory pathways to include the animal rule (in which humans cannot be included in efficacy studies for safety reasons)/animal model development through discussions of biological warfare and outbreak pathogen therapeutic development. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Sallie Crenshaw" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Sallie Crenshaw" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="24" section="F01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" The course describes the evolution of the regulations that govern the pharmaceutical industry and examines how the resultant regulatory framework influences and guides pharmaceutical companies in the development, manufacture, testing and commercial distribution of drugs. Multiple aspects in the drug development process influenced by the regulations including quality system design and operation, regulatory affairs group structure and activities, compliance, good practices (GxPs), and ethics will be covered. The course will explore the global regulatory environment but will focus on the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and the European Medicines Agency (EMA) as primary study cases when examining the regulations, processes and controls for clinical development, marketing authorization approvals and post-approval changes for drugs, biologics and vaccines. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Drug Regulations and Regulatory Affairs" number="563"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="2" crn="000000000000345716" max_waitlist="10" number="F01" part-of-term="A Term, B Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" The course describes the evolution of the regulations that govern the pharmaceutical industry and examines how the resultant regulatory framework influences and guides pharmaceutical companies in the development, manufacture, testing and commercial distribution of drugs. Multiple aspects in the drug development process influenced by the regulations including quality system design and operation, regulatory affairs group structure and activities, compliance, good practices (GxPs), and ethics will be covered. The course will explore the global regulatory environment but will focus on the US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) and the European Medicines Agency (EMA) as primary study cases when examining the regulations, processes and controls for clinical development, marketing authorization approvals and post-approval changes for drugs, biologics and vaccines. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="2" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Enda Moran" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Enda Moran" room="Online-asynchronous " seats="20" section="F01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Reports on current advances in the various branches of chemistry. " max-credits="1.0" min-credits="1.0" name="Seminar" number="571"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" crn="000000000000336035" max_waitlist="0" number="F01" part-of-term="A Term, B Term" seats="16" sec_desc=" Reports on current advances in the various branches of chemistry. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" building="" days="fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Suzanne Scarlata" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Suzanne Scarlata" room="Gateway Park 1 Room 4104 " seats="16" section="F01" starts="12:00PM" type="Seminar"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" crn="000000000000337888" max_waitlist="5" number="S01" part-of-term="C Term, D Term" seats="16" sec_desc=" Reports on current advances in the various branches of chemistry. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" building="" days="fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Not Assigned" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Not Assigned" room="Gateway Park 1 Room 4104 " seats="16" section="S01" starts="12:00PM" type="Seminar"/></section></course></dept><dept abbrev="CHE" name="Chemical Engineering"><course course_desc=" Cat. I. This course provides an introduction to the broad and vital discipline of chemical engineering including conventional and developing chemical technologies. An introduction is provided to the first principles of chemical engineering, as well as environmental, health, safety and ethical issues in chemical engineering practice. An overview is provided of the chemical engineering profession, career choices, the course of study, and a survey of the chemical industry, e.g., polymer, pharmaceutical, food processing, microelectronic, electrochemical, biotechnology, process control, energy, and petroleum refining. Course activities include guest speakers and plant trips. Recommended for first-year students with a basic knowledge of chemistry. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Introduction To Chemical Engineering" number="1011"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" crn="000000000000335005" max_waitlist="10" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" Cat. I. This course provides an introduction to the broad and vital discipline of chemical engineering including conventional and developing chemical technologies. An introduction is provided to the first principles of chemical engineering, as well as environmental, health, safety and ethical issues in chemical engineering practice. An overview is provided of the chemical engineering profession, career choices, the course of study, and a survey of the chemical industry, e.g., polymer, pharmaceutical, food processing, microelectronic, electrochemical, biotechnology, process control, energy, and petroleum refining. Course activities include guest speakers and plant trips. Recommended for first-year students with a basic knowledge of chemistry. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="N. Aaron Deskins" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="N. Aaron Deskins" room="Salisbury Labs 402 " seats="30" section="B01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This first course in chemical engineering is designed to give students the ability to use techniques and solve problems of interest to chemical engineers. Students will learn fundamental material by completing analysis, design, and/or laboratory projects. Topics covered include: material balances and stoichiometry, pressure, volume, and temperature behavior of pure fluids, 1st law of thermodynamics, vapor-liquid equilibria with ideal thermodynamics, and staged separation processes. Recommended background: Elementary college chemistry and calculus. Students may not receive credit towards CHE distribution requirements for both CHE 2011 and CM 2001. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Chemical Engineering Fundamentals" number="2011"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" crn="000000000000334412" max_waitlist="20" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="80" sec_desc=" Cat. I This first course in chemical engineering is designed to give students the ability to use techniques and solve problems of interest to chemical engineers. Students will learn fundamental material by completing analysis, design, and/or laboratory projects. Topics covered include: material balances and stoichiometry, pressure, volume, and temperature behavior of pure fluids, 1st law of thermodynamics, vapor-liquid equilibria with ideal thermodynamics, and staged separation processes. Recommended background: Elementary college chemistry and calculus. Students may not receive credit towards CHE distribution requirements for both CHE 2011 and CM 2001. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Christina Bailey-Hytholt" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Christina Bailey-Hytholt" room="Higgins Labs 116 " seats="80" section="A01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course aims to build a strong foundation in analysis of chemical processes via a project-based approach. Topics covered include analysis and design of stagewise separation processes such as distillation, 1st and 2nd law (of thermodynamics) analysis of power and refrigeration cycles, and application of material and energy balances in industrial chemical processes, including those with recycle and non-ideal systems. Recommended background: Elementary college chemistry and calculus and some familiarity with the topics listed in CHE 2011. Students may not receive credit towards CHE distribution requirements for both CHE 2012 and ES 3000. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Elementary Chemical Processes" number="2012"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="34" crn="000000335006335008" max_waitlist="0" number="BL01/BD01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="100" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course aims to build a strong foundation in analysis of chemical processes via a project-based approach. Topics covered include analysis and design of stagewise separation processes such as distillation, 1st and 2nd law (of thermodynamics) analysis of power and refrigeration cycles, and application of material and energy balances in industrial chemical processes, including those with recycle and non-ideal systems. Recommended background: Elementary college chemistry and calculus and some familiarity with the topics listed in CHE 2011. Students may not receive credit towards CHE distribution requirements for both CHE 2012 and ES 3000. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="34" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Stephen Kmiotek" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Stephen Kmiotek" room="Fuller Labs PHL Perreault Hall - Lower Section " seats="100" section="BL01" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="34" building="" days="wed" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Stephen Kmiotek" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Stephen Kmiotek" room="Fuller Labs PHL Perreault Hall - Lower Section " seats="100" section="BD01" starts="9:00AM" type="Discussion"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. IThis course uses a project-based approach to build confidence and competence in the use of chemical engineering thermodynamics for the analysis and design of chemical processes. Topics covered include extractive separation systems, solution thermodynamics and nonreacting multicomponent mixtures, phase equilibria and property changes on mixing.Recommended background: Elementary college chemistry and calculus and some familiarity with the topics listed in CHE 2011 and CHE 2012.Students may not receive credit towards CHE distribution requirements for both CHE 2013 and CM 2102. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Applied Chemical Engineering Thermodynamics" number="2013"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="33" crn="000000336847336568" max_waitlist="0" number="CL01/CD01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="100" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis course uses a project-based approach to build confidence and competence in the use of chemical engineering thermodynamics for the analysis and design of chemical processes. Topics covered include extractive separation systems, solution thermodynamics and nonreacting multicomponent mixtures, phase equilibria and property changes on mixing.Recommended background: Elementary college chemistry and calculus and some familiarity with the topics listed in CHE 2011 and CHE 2012.Students may not receive credit towards CHE distribution requirements for both CHE 2013 and CM 2102. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="33" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Alex Maag" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Alex Maag" room="Salisbury Labs 115 Kinnicutt Hall " seats="100" section="CL01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="33" building="" days="wed" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Alex Maag" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Alex Maag" room="Salisbury Labs 115 Kinnicutt Hall " seats="100" section="CD01" starts="10:00AM" type="Discussion"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course builds on prior work in material and energy balances, chemical engineering thermodynamics, and stagewise separation processes to facilitate student mastery and design of more complex processes. Topics covered include chemical reaction equilibria, material and energy balances for non-steady state systems, combined material and energy balances, humidification, and batch distillation. Recommended background: Elementary college chemistry and calculus and some familiarity with the topics listed in CHE 2011, CHE 2012, and CHE 2013. Students may not receive credit towards CHE distribution requirements for both CHE 2014 and CM 2002.   Some sections of this course may be offered as Writing Intensive (WI).   " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Advanced Chemical Processes" number="2014"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" crn="000000337142337330" max_waitlist="0" number="DL01/DD01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="90" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis course builds on prior work in material and energy balances, chemical engineering thermodynamics, and stagewise separation processes to facilitate student mastery and design of more complex processes. Topics covered include chemical reaction equilibria, material and energy balances for non-steady state systems, combined material and energy balances, humidification, and batch distillation.Recommended background: Elementary college chemistry and calculus and some familiarity with the topics listed in CHE 2011, CHE 2012, and CHE 2013.Students may not receive credit towards CHE distribution requirements for both CHE 2014 and CM 2002. Some sections of this course may be offered as Writing Intensive (WI). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Susan Zhou" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Susan Zhou" room="Higgins Labs 116 " seats="90" section="DL01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" building="" days="mon,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Susan Zhou" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Susan Zhou" room="Higgins Labs 116 " seats="90" section="DD01" starts="12:00PM" type="Discussion"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. ITechniques for experimentally determining rate laws for simple and complex chemical reactions, the mechanisms and theories of chemical reactions, the function of catalysts, and the design of isothermal, adiabatic, batch and flow reactors. The course is intended to provide chemists and chemical engineers with the conceptual base needed to study reactions and perform in the design and analysis of reactors.Recommended background: differential equations, thermodynamics and someorganic chemistry. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Kinetics And Reactor Design" number="3201"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="34" crn="000000000000336569" max_waitlist="20" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. ITechniques for experimentally determining rate laws for simple and complex chemical reactions, the mechanisms and theories of chemical reactions, the function of catalysts, and the design of isothermal, adiabatic, batch and flow reactors. The course is intended to provide chemists and chemical engineers with the conceptual base needed to study reactions and perform in the design and analysis of reactors.Recommended background: differential equations, thermodynamics and someorganic chemistry. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="34" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Andrew Teixeira" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Andrew Teixeira" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="60" section="C01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="35" crn="000000000000337157" max_waitlist="20" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. ITechniques for experimentally determining rate laws for simple and complex chemical reactions, the mechanisms and theories of chemical reactions, the function of catalysts, and the design of isothermal, adiabatic, batch and flow reactors. The course is intended to provide chemists and chemical engineers with the conceptual base needed to study reactions and perform in the design and analysis of reactors.Recommended background: differential equations, thermodynamics and someorganic chemistry. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="35" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Fanglin Che" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Fanglin Che" room="Washburn 229 " seats="60" section="D01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. II This course is an introduction to the chemical engineering principles involved in modern applications of biological engineering. Topics may include: an introduction to biology, biochemistry, physiology, and genomics; biological process engineering including fermentation, mammalian cell culture, biocatalysis, and downstream bioseparations; drug discovery, development, and delivery; environmental biotechnology; and chemical engineering aspects of biomedical devices. Recommended background: material and energy balances, thermodynamics, organic chemistry, and differential equations. This course will be offered in 2019-20, and in alternating years thereafter. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Introduction To Biological Engineering" number="3301"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" crn="000000000000338641" max_waitlist="10" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="40" sec_desc=" Cat. II This course is an introduction to the chemical engineering principles involved in modern applications of biological engineering. Topics may include: an introduction to biology, biochemistry, physiology, and genomics; biological process engineering including fermentation, mammalian cell culture, biocatalysis, and downstream bioseparations; drug discovery, development, and delivery; environmental biotechnology; and chemical engineering aspects of biomedical devices. Recommended background: material and energy balances, thermodynamics, organic chemistry, and differential equations. This course will be offered in 2019-20, and in alternating years thereafter. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Susan Roberts" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Susan Roberts" room="Salisbury Labs 105 " seats="40" section="C01" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. IThe consolidation of the methods of mathematics into a form that can be used for setting up and solving chemical engineering problems. Mathematical formulation of problems corresponding to specific physical situations such as momentum, energy and mass transfer, and chemical reactions. Analytical and numerical techniques for handling the resulting ordinary and partial differential equations and finite difference equations.Recommended background: ordinary differential equations, partial derivatives and vectors, momentum heat and mass transfer. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Applied Mathematics In Chemical Engineering" number="3501"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" crn="337147337149337161" max_waitlist="0" number="DL01/DD01/DX01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="27" sec_desc=" Cat. IThe consolidation of the methods of mathematics into a form that can be used for setting up and solving chemical engineering problems. Mathematical formulation of problems corresponding to specific physical situations such as momentum, energy and mass transfer, and chemical reactions. Analytical and numerical techniques for handling the resulting ordinary and partial differential equations and finite difference equations.Recommended background: ordinary differential equations, partial derivatives and vectors, momentum heat and mass transfer. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" building="" days="mon,tue,fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Anthony Dixon" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Anthony Dixon" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="27" section="DL01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" building="" days="tue" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Anthony Dixon" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Anthony Dixon" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="27" section="DD01" starts="3:00PM" type="Discussion"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" building="" days="thu" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Anthony Dixon" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Anthony Dixon" room="Salisbury Labs 123 Computer Lab " seats="27" section="DX01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat II. The primary goal of this course is to provide students the necessary understanding and tools to evaluate biochemical and thermochemical biofuel production technologies. The secondary goals include developing understanding of 1) fuel properties, 2) biomass resources, 3) basic enzyme kinetics, 4) biochemical reactor design, 5) the corn ethanol process, 6) challenges to cellulosic ethanol, 7) biomass gasification reactions and thermochemistry, 8) gasification reactor design, and 9) techno economic concepts of biofuel processes. Recommended background: Knowledge of chemistry (CH 1010, 1020, and 1030 or equivalent), differential and integral calculus and differential equations (MA 1021-1024 and 2051 or equivalent), and chemical processing (CHE 2011 or equivalent). Students may not receive credit for both CHE 372X and CHE 3722.  This course will be offered in 2020-21, and in alternating years thereafter. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Bioenergy" number="3722"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" crn="000000000000338645" max_waitlist="15" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat II. The primary goal of this course is to provide students the necessary understanding and tools to evaluate biochemical and thermochemical biofuel production technologies. The secondary goals include developing understanding of 1) fuel properties, 2) biomass resources, 3) basic enzyme kinetics, 4) biochemical reactor design, 5) the corn ethanol process, 6) challenges to cellulosic ethanol, 7) biomass gasification reactions and thermochemistry, 8) gasification reactor design, and 9) techno economic concepts of biofuel processes. Recommended background: Knowledge of chemistry (CH 1010, 1020, and 1030 or equivalent), differential and integral calculus and differential equations (MA 1021-1024 and 2051 or equivalent), and chemical processing (CHE 2011 or equivalent). Students may not receive credit for both CHE 372X and CHE 3722.  This course will be offered in 2020-21, and in alternating years thereafter. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Geoffrey Tompsett" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Geoffrey Tompsett" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="50" section="D01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. II In this course, students will learn to make quantitative relationships between human activities and the effects on water, soil, and air in the environment. Students will learn the scientific and engineering principles that are needed to understand how contaminants enter and move in the environment, how compounds react in the environment, how to predict their concentrations in the environment, and how to develop solutions to environmental problems. Topics to be covered may include water quality engineering (including microbial interactions), air quality engineering, and hazardous waste management.  Recommended Background: familiarity with transport phenomena, such as in ES 3004 (Fluid Mechanics) and ES 3002 (Mass Transfer), and familiarity with reaction kinetics and reactor design, such as through CHE 3201 (Kinetics and Reactor Design). Background such as CE 3059 (Environmental Engineering), CE 3060 (Water Treatment), or CE3061 (Wastewater Treatment) is suggested.  This course will be offered in 2020-21, and in alternating years thereafter " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Transport &amp; Transformations In The Environment" number="4063"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="32" crn="000000000000341676" max_waitlist="20" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat. II In this course, students will learn to make quantitative relationships between human activities and the effects on water, soil, and air in the environment. Students will learn the scientific and engineering principles that are needed to understand how contaminants enter and move in the environment, how compounds react in the environment, how to predict their concentrations in the environment, and how to develop solutions to environmental problems. Topics to be covered may include water quality engineering (including microbial interactions), air quality engineering, and hazardous waste management.  Recommended Background: familiarity with transport phenomena, such as in ES 3004 (Fluid Mechanics) and ES 3002 (Mass Transfer), and familiarity with reaction kinetics and reactor design, such as through CHE 3201 (Kinetics and Reactor Design). Background such as CE 3059 (Environmental Engineering), CE 3060 (Water Treatment), or CE3061 (Wastewater Treatment) is suggested.  This course will be offered in 2020-21, and in alternating years thereafter " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="32" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Stephen Kmiotek" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Stephen Kmiotek" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="50" section="D01" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I  Laboratory-application of fundamental theories to practical chemical engineering operations. Emphasis is on building the student’s understanding and ability to approach the problems of design and operations of large scale chemical processing equipment.  The course is a combination of lectures and laboratory projects in the area of unit operations. Laboratory projects include experiments in fluid-flow phenomena through various media such as: friction in conduits, filtration, pressure drop in packed towers, fluidization of solids, and spray drying.  Students are expected to carry out the planning and execution of experimental work as well as the analysis and reporting of experimental results in both written and oral format.  Recommended background: knowledge of chemistry, mathematics and engineering principles. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Unit Operations Of Chemical Engineering I" number="4401"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="-4" crn="000000334214334212" max_waitlist="0" number="AL01/AX01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="16" sec_desc=" Cat. I  Laboratory-application of fundamental theories to practical chemical engineering operations. Emphasis is on building the student’s understanding and ability to approach the problems of design and operations of large scale chemical processing equipment.  The course is a combination of lectures and laboratory projects in the area of unit operations. Laboratory projects include experiments in fluid-flow phenomena through various media such as: friction in conduits, filtration, pressure drop in packed towers, fluidization of solids, and spray drying.  Students are expected to carry out the planning and execution of experimental work as well as the analysis and reporting of experimental results in both written and oral format.  Recommended background: knowledge of chemistry, mathematics and engineering principles. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="-4" building="" days="wed" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Laila Abu-Lail; David DiBiasio" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Laila Abu-Lail; David DiBiasio" room="Atwater Kent 233 " seats="32" section="AL01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="-1" building="" days="thu" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Laila Abu-Lail; David DiBiasio" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Laila Abu-Lail; David DiBiasio" room="Goddard Hall 116 Chem Lab " seats="16" section="AX01" starts="8:00AM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="-4" crn="000000334214334215" max_waitlist="0" number="AL01/AX02" part-of-term="A Term" seats="16" sec_desc=" Cat. I  Laboratory-application of fundamental theories to practical chemical engineering operations. Emphasis is on building the student’s understanding and ability to approach the problems of design and operations of large scale chemical processing equipment.  The course is a combination of lectures and laboratory projects in the area of unit operations. Laboratory projects include experiments in fluid-flow phenomena through various media such as: friction in conduits, filtration, pressure drop in packed towers, fluidization of solids, and spray drying.  Students are expected to carry out the planning and execution of experimental work as well as the analysis and reporting of experimental results in both written and oral format.  Recommended background: knowledge of chemistry, mathematics and engineering principles. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="-4" building="" days="wed" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Laila Abu-Lail; David DiBiasio" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Laila Abu-Lail; David DiBiasio" room="Atwater Kent 233 " seats="32" section="AL01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="-3" building="" days="tue" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Laila Abu-Lail; David DiBiasio" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Laila Abu-Lail; David DiBiasio" room="Goddard Hall 116 Chem Lab " seats="16" section="AX02" starts="8:00AM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I Overall format and procedure are essentially the same as in Unit Operations of Chemical Engineering I. Laboratory projects include experiments in heat and mass transfer such as: heat transfer in two heaters and a cooler, climbing film evaporation, multiple effect evaporation, absorption, extraction, distillation and rotary drying of solids. Recommended background: familiarity with techniques and procedures emphasized in CHE 4401. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Unit Operations Of Chemical Engineering II" number="4402"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" crn="000000000000335031" max_waitlist="10" number="BX01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="24" sec_desc=" Cat. I Overall format and procedure are essentially the same as in Unit Operations of Chemical Engineering I. Laboratory projects include experiments in heat and mass transfer such as: heat transfer in two heaters and a cooler, climbing film evaporation, multiple effect evaporation, absorption, extraction, distillation and rotary drying of solids. Recommended background: familiarity with techniques and procedures emphasized in CHE 4401. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" building="" days="thu" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Susan Zhou; Laila Abu-Lail" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Susan Zhou; Laila Abu-Lail" room="Goddard Hall 116 Chem Lab " seats="24" section="BX01" starts="8:00AM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" crn="000000000000335034" max_waitlist="10" number="BX02" part-of-term="B Term" seats="16" sec_desc=" Cat. I Overall format and procedure are essentially the same as in Unit Operations of Chemical Engineering I. Laboratory projects include experiments in heat and mass transfer such as: heat transfer in two heaters and a cooler, climbing film evaporation, multiple effect evaporation, absorption, extraction, distillation and rotary drying of solids. Recommended background: familiarity with techniques and procedures emphasized in CHE 4401. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" building="" days="tue" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Susan Zhou; Laila Abu-Lail" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Susan Zhou; Laila Abu-Lail" room="Goddard Hall 116 Chem Lab " seats="16" section="BX02" starts="8:00AM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I Design of equipment, systems and plants; discussion of factors important in chemical plant design such as: economics, cost estimation, profitability, process selection, materials of construction, process control, plant location and safety. Introduction to optimization and computer-aided design. Principles are illustrated with short industrial-type problems. Recommended background: thermodynamics; heat, mass and momentum transfer; inorganic and organic chemistry; chemical kinetics and reactor design. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Chemical Engineering Design" number="4403"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="27" crn="000000334477334479" max_waitlist="0" number="AL01/AD01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. I Design of equipment, systems and plants; discussion of factors important in chemical plant design such as: economics, cost estimation, profitability, process selection, materials of construction, process control, plant location and safety. Introduction to optimization and computer-aided design. Principles are illustrated with short industrial-type problems. Recommended background: thermodynamics; heat, mass and momentum transfer; inorganic and organic chemistry; chemical kinetics and reactor design. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="27" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Anthony Dixon" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Anthony Dixon" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="60" section="AL01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="27" building="" days="tue" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Anthony Dixon" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Anthony Dixon" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="60" section="AD01" starts="2:00PM" type="Discussion"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I Application of Chemical Engineering design principles to the design of a major chemical plant. Students work in groups to produce a preliminary practical process flowsheet, equipment and plant design, and economic analysis. Recommended background: familiarity with techniques and procedures emphasized in CHE 4403. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Chemical Plant Design Project" number="4404"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="23" crn="000000000000335337" max_waitlist="25" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. I Application of Chemical Engineering design principles to the design of a major chemical plant. Students work in groups to produce a preliminary practical process flowsheet, equipment and plant design, and economic analysis. Recommended background: familiarity with techniques and procedures emphasized in CHE 4403. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="23" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="25" professor="Stephen Kmiotek; Xiaowei Teng" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Stephen Kmiotek; Xiaowei Teng" room="Salisbury Labs 402 " seats="60" section="B01" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. IThis course is intended to provide laboratory application of fundamental principles of chemical process dynamics and feedback control. This includes open-loop dynamics of typical chemical engineering processes such as distillation, fluid flow, chemical reactors and heated stirred tanks. Closed-loop experiments will involve control loop design, controller tuning, multivariable, and computer control.Students will be required to design and execute their own experiments based on supplied objectives. Analysis and presentation of the results will be done through oral and written reports.Recommended background: knowledge of fluid flow and heat transfer, mathematics and chemical engineering principles. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Chemical Process Dynamics And Control Laboratory" number="4405"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="40" crn="000000336580336583" max_waitlist="0" number="CL01/CD01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis course is intended to provide laboratory application of fundamental principles of chemical process dynamics and feedback control. This includes open-loop dynamics of typical chemical engineering processes such as distillation, fluid flow, chemical reactors and heated stirred tanks. Closed-loop experiments will involve control loop design, controller tuning, multivariable, and computer control.Students will be required to design and execute their own experiments based on supplied objectives. Analysis and presentation of the results will be done through oral and written reports.Recommended background: knowledge of fluid flow and heat transfer, mathematics and chemical engineering principles. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="40" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Nikolaos Kazantzis" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Nikolaos Kazantzis" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="60" section="CL01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="40" building="" days="wed" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Nikolaos Kazantzis" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Nikolaos Kazantzis" room="Higgins Labs 116 " seats="60" section="CD01" starts="12:00PM" type="Discussion"/></section></course><course course_desc=" CHE 501: Professional Development for Chemical Engineering Doctoral Students (0 Credits) This course provides professional development for chemical engineering students in the Ph.D. program. Topics covered may include: developing professional identities and networks, exploring chemical engineering career paths, setting career goals, improving technical and non-technical communication skills, analyzing ethical challenges in chemical engineering, and supporting diversity, equity, inclusion, and wellness in professional environments. Must be taken for eight semesters during Ph.D. program. " max-credits="0.0" min-credits="0.0" name="Professional Development for Chemical Engineering Doctoral Students" number="501"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" crn="000000000000335971" max_waitlist="10" number="F01" part-of-term="A Term, B Term" seats="32" sec_desc=" CHE 501: Professional Development for Chemical Engineering Doctoral Students (0 Credits) This course provides professional development for chemical engineering students in the Ph.D. program. Topics covered may include: developing professional identities and networks, exploring chemical engineering career paths, setting career goals, improving technical and non-technical communication skills, analyzing ethical challenges in chemical engineering, and supporting diversity, equity, inclusion, and wellness in professional environments. Must be taken for eight semesters during Ph.D. program. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" building="" days="wed" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Elizabeth Stewart" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Elizabeth Stewart" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="32" section="F01" starts="11:00AM" type="Seminar"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="54" crn="000000000000337741" max_waitlist="5" number="S01" part-of-term="C Term, D Term" seats="64" sec_desc=" CHE 501: Professional Development for Chemical Engineering Doctoral Students (0 Credits) This course provides professional development for chemical engineering students in the Ph.D. program. Topics covered may include: developing professional identities and networks, exploring chemical engineering career paths, setting career goals, improving technical and non-technical communication skills, analyzing ethical challenges in chemical engineering, and supporting diversity, equity, inclusion, and wellness in professional environments. Must be taken for eight semesters during Ph.D. program. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="54" building="" days="wed" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Elizabeth Stewart" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Elizabeth Stewart" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="64" section="S01" starts="11:00AM" type="Seminar"/></section></course><course course_desc=" CHE 502: Professional Development for Chemical Engineering Master’s Students (0 Credits) This course provides professional development for chemical engineering graduate students in the M.S. program. Topics covered may include: developing professional identities and networks, exploring chemical engineering career paths, setting career goals, improving technical and nontechnical communication skills, analyzing ethical challenges in chemical engineering, and supporting diversity, equity, inclusion, and wellness in professional environments. Must be taken for two semesters during M.S. program. " max-credits="0.0" min-credits="0.0" name="Professional Development for Chemical Engineering Master’s Students" number="502"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="21" crn="000000000000335755" max_waitlist="5" number="F01" part-of-term="A Term, B Term" seats="32" sec_desc=" CHE 502: Professional Development for Chemical Engineering Master’s Students (0 Credits) This course provides professional development for chemical engineering graduate students in the M.S. program. Topics covered may include: developing professional identities and networks, exploring chemical engineering career paths, setting career goals, improving technical and nontechnical communication skills, analyzing ethical challenges in chemical engineering, and supporting diversity, equity, inclusion, and wellness in professional environments. Must be taken for two semesters during M.S. program. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="21" building="" days="wed" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Elizabeth Stewart" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Elizabeth Stewart" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="32" section="F01" starts="11:00AM" type="Seminar"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="54" crn="000000000000337849" max_waitlist="5" number="S01" part-of-term="C Term, D Term" seats="64" sec_desc=" CHE 502: Professional Development for Chemical Engineering Master’s Students (0 Credits) This course provides professional development for chemical engineering graduate students in the M.S. program. Topics covered may include: developing professional identities and networks, exploring chemical engineering career paths, setting career goals, improving technical and nontechnical communication skills, analyzing ethical challenges in chemical engineering, and supporting diversity, equity, inclusion, and wellness in professional environments. Must be taken for two semesters during M.S. program. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="54" building="" days="wed" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Elizabeth Stewart" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Elizabeth Stewart" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="64" section="S01" starts="11:00AM" type="Seminar"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Presentations on scientific advances by recognized experts in various fields of chemical engineering and related disciplines. The course will be graded on a Pass/Fail basis. " max-credits="0.0" min-credits="0.0" name="Colloquium" number="503"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="21" crn="000000000000335972" max_waitlist="10" number="F01" part-of-term="A Term, B Term" seats="64" sec_desc=" Presentations on scientific advances by recognized experts in various fields of chemical engineering and related disciplines. The course will be graded on a Pass/Fail basis. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="21" building="" days="wed" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Elizabeth Stewart" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Elizabeth Stewart" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="64" section="F01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="51" crn="000000000000337684" max_waitlist="10" number="S01" part-of-term="C Term, D Term" seats="64" sec_desc=" Presentations on scientific advances by recognized experts in various fields of chemical engineering and related disciplines. The course will be graded on a Pass/Fail basis. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="51" building="" days="wed" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Elizabeth Stewart" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Elizabeth Stewart" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="64" section="S01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" An essential skill of an engineer is to provide analytical and numerical solutions to relevant problems. This course will provide students with a solid mathematical background required to solve chemical engineering problems in fields such as fluid mechanics, reactor design, thermodynamics, and process design. Methods of mathematical analysis relevant to engineering will be selected from such topics as vector analysis, matrices, eigenvalue problems, Fourier analysis, Fourier transforms, Laplace transformation, solution of ordinary and partial differential equations, integral equations, calculus of variation, optimization methods, and numerical methods. Students should have a background in undergraduate calculus and differential equations. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Mathematical Analysis In Chemical Engineering" number="504"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" crn="000000000000337681" max_waitlist="5" number="S01" part-of-term="C Term, D Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" An essential skill of an engineer is to provide analytical and numerical solutions to relevant problems. This course will provide students with a solid mathematical background required to solve chemical engineering problems in fields such as fluid mechanics, reactor design, thermodynamics, and process design. Methods of mathematical analysis relevant to engineering will be selected from such topics as vector analysis, matrices, eigenvalue problems, Fourier analysis, Fourier transforms, Laplace transformation, solution of ordinary and partial differential equations, integral equations, calculus of variation, optimization methods, and numerical methods. Students should have a background in undergraduate calculus and differential equations. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" building="" days="mon,wed" ends="4:20PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Anthony Dixon" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Anthony Dixon" room="Salisbury Labs 411 " seats="20" section="S01" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course includes a review of prototypical chemical reactors, including design of batch, stirred tank, and tubular reactors. Theories of reaction kinetics and catalysis for simple and complex reactions are addressed. Reactor design is discussed within the context of complex transport phenomena and reaction kinetics, including effects of bulk and pore diffusion and multiphase reactions/reactors. Techniques for experimentation, reaction data treatment, catalyst preparation and characterization, and computational tools are also included. Students cannot receive credit for this course and CHE 506 or CHE 507, which this class replaces. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Reactor Design And Kinetics" number="509"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" crn="000000000000335736" max_waitlist="10" number="F01" part-of-term="A Term, B Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" This course includes a review of prototypical chemical reactors, including design of batch, stirred tank, and tubular reactors. Theories of reaction kinetics and catalysis for simple and complex reactions are addressed. Reactor design is discussed within the context of complex transport phenomena and reaction kinetics, including effects of bulk and pore diffusion and multiphase reactions/reactors. Techniques for experimentation, reaction data treatment, catalyst preparation and characterization, and computational tools are also included. Students cannot receive credit for this course and CHE 506 or CHE 507, which this class replaces. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Ravindra Datta" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Ravindra Datta" room="Kaven Hall 203 CARS Computer Lab " seats="25" section="F01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" The course emphasizes the basic concepts of biological systems which are relevant to study by chemical engineers. Topics covered include Ligand binding and membrane transport processes, growth kinetics of animal cells and micro-organisms, kinetics of interacting multiple populations, biological reactor design and analysis, soluble immobilized enzyme kinetics, optimization and control of fermentation, biopolymer structure and function, properties of biological molecules, biological separation processes, scale-up of bioprocesses; laboratory work may be included when possible. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Biochemical Engineering" number="521"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" crn="000000000000341597" max_waitlist="10" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" The course emphasizes the basic concepts of biological systems which are relevant to study by chemical engineers. Topics covered include Ligand binding and membrane transport processes, growth kinetics of animal cells and micro-organisms, kinetics of interacting multiple populations, biological reactor design and analysis, soluble immobilized enzyme kinetics, optimization and control of fermentation, biopolymer structure and function, properties of biological molecules, biological separation processes, scale-up of bioprocesses; laboratory work may be included when possible. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" building="" days="mon,wed" ends="5:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Eric Young" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Eric Young" room="Higgins Labs 154 " seats="25" section="B01" starts="4:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course trains students in the area of molecular modeling using a variety of quantum mechanical and force field methods. The approach will be toward practical applications, for researchers who want to answer specific questions about molecular geometry, transition states, reaction paths and photoexcited states. No experience in programming is necessary; however, a background at the introductory level in quantum mechanics is highly desirable. Methods to be explored include density functional theory, ab initio methods, semiempirical molecular orbital theory, and visualization software for the graphical display of molecules. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Molecular Modeling" number="554"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" crn="000000000000333961" max_waitlist="10" number="AL01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" This course trains students in the area of molecular modeling using a variety of quantum mechanical and force field methods. The approach will be toward practical applications, for researchers who want to answer specific questions about molecular geometry, transition states, reaction paths and photoexcited states. No experience in programming is necessary; however, a background at the introductory level in quantum mechanics is highly desirable. Methods to be explored include density functional theory, ab initio methods, semiempirical molecular orbital theory, and visualization software for the graphical display of molecules. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="N. Aaron Deskins" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="N. Aaron Deskins" room="Higgins Labs 230 Geom Modeling Computer Lab " seats="25" section="AL01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc="Thermodynamics is at the heart of many systems of interest to chemical engineers, from the efficiency of simple mechanical processes to the equilibria of complex reactions. This course is a rigorous treatment of classicalthermodynamics, with reference to the field of statistical thermodynamics. Key modules include First and Second Law analysis; behavior and interrelationships of thermodynamic properties; and fluid phase and chemical equilibria. Exampletopics may include analysis of open and dynamic systems; fundamental relationships; Legendre transforms and generalized potentials; Maxwell relationships; stability theory; thermodynamics of mixtures; fugacity, activity, and chemical potential; phase equilibria of systems containing two or more components; and generalized treatment of chemical equilibria." max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Thermodynamics" number="561"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="21" crn="000000000000336027" max_waitlist="10" number="F01" part-of-term="A Term, B Term" seats="40" sec_desc="Thermodynamics is at the heart of many systems of interest to chemical engineers, from the efficiency of simple mechanical processes to the equilibria of complex reactions. This course is a rigorous treatment of classicalthermodynamics, with reference to the field of statistical thermodynamics. Key modules include First and Second Law analysis; behavior and interrelationships of thermodynamic properties; and fluid phase and chemical equilibria. Exampletopics may include analysis of open and dynamic systems; fundamental relationships; Legendre transforms and generalized potentials; Maxwell relationships; stability theory; thermodynamics of mixtures; fugacity, activity, and chemical potential; phase equilibria of systems containing two or more components; and generalized treatment of chemical equilibria." term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="21" building="" days="mon,wed,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Michael Timko" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Michael Timko" room="Stratton Hall 207 (new) " seats="40" section="F01" starts="8:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Advanced topics in process synthesis, optimization and process control are examined. Optimization topics include objective functions, multivariable optimization, constrained optimization, mixed integer linear programming and applications of optimization to process industries. Control topics include model predictive control, adaptive control,batch process control, and plant-wide control.Recommended background: Undergraduate degree in Chemical Engineering " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Advanced Process Engineering" number="565"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="13" crn="000000000000337151" max_waitlist="5" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Advanced topics in process synthesis, optimization and process control are examined. Optimization topics include objective functions, multivariable optimization, constrained optimization, mixed integer linear programming and applications of optimization to process industries. Control topics include model predictive control, adaptive control,batch process control, and plant-wide control.Recommended background: Undergraduate degree in Chemical Engineering " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="13" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Walter Zurawsky" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Walter Zurawsky" room="Kaven Hall 204 " seats="20" section="D01" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Transport rates of mass, energy, and momentum are key to the design of many chemical technologies. This class adopts a unified approach to transport phenomena, providing the fundamental background required for analysis ofcomplex problems. Students will use mathematical techniques for analytic and approximate solutions such as: separation of variables, similarity solutions, perturbation theory, and Laplace and Fourier transform methods. Methods involvingnon-dimensionalization and scaling will be emphasized. Special problems to be covered may include the lubrication approximation, creeping flow, and potential and laminar boundary-layer flows, as well as heat and mass transport in multicomponent systems. Students are expected to have taken previous courses on transport processesand have mathematical background that includes solution of differential equations. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Transport Phenomena" number="571"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" crn="000000000000337686" max_waitlist="5" number="S01" part-of-term="C Term, D Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" Transport rates of mass, energy, and momentum are key to the design of many chemical technologies. This class adopts a unified approach to transport phenomena, providing the fundamental background required for analysis ofcomplex problems. Students will use mathematical techniques for analytic and approximate solutions such as: separation of variables, similarity solutions, perturbation theory, and Laplace and Fourier transform methods. Methods involvingnon-dimensionalization and scaling will be emphasized. Special problems to be covered may include the lubrication approximation, creeping flow, and potential and laminar boundary-layer flows, as well as heat and mass transport in multicomponent systems. Students are expected to have taken previous courses on transport processesand have mathematical background that includes solution of differential equations. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" building="" days="mon,wed" ends="5:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Walter Zurawsky" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Walter Zurawsky" room="Olin Hall 218 " seats="25" section="S01" starts="4:30PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" This course will focus on various topics of current interest related to faculty research experience. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Special Topics" number="580"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" crn="000000000000334027" max_waitlist="10" number="A01 - ST: Pathways to Chemical Engineering" part-of-term="A Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" This course will focus on various topics of current interest related to faculty research experience. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Nikolaos Kazantzis" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Nikolaos Kazantzis" room="Olin Hall 109 " seats="20" section="A01 - ST: Pathways to Chemical Engineering" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="12" crn="000000000000340238" max_waitlist="10" number="C01 - ST: Applied Machine Learning in Chemical Engineering and Materials Science" part-of-term="C Term" seats="15" sec_desc=" This course will focus on various topics of current interest related to faculty research experience.     This course introduces the fundamentals of machine learning with a specific focus on applications in chemical engineering and materials science. Students will explore predictive modeling, feature engineering, and model assessment, emphasizing the small-data regime common in these fields. Key machine learning techniques, including regression, classification, clustering, and optimization, will be discussed in the context of solving engineering problems such as reaction kinetics modeling, catalysis design, nanomaterials characterization, and process optimization.    Advanced topics such as deep learning (e.g., artificial neural networks, convolutional neural networks) and reinforcement learning will be introduced through case studies in molecular property prediction, catalyst screening, and materials discovery. The course will emphasize the integration of machine learning methodologies with fundamental engineering principles to accelerate innovation in chemical engineering and materials science.  " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="12" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Fanglin Che" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Fanglin Che" room="Kaven Hall 204 " seats="15" section="C01 - ST: Applied Machine Learning in Chemical Engineering and Materials Science" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="20" crn="000000000000337389" max_waitlist="10" number="D01 - ST: Biomaterials for Women's Health" part-of-term="D Term" seats="25" sec_desc="This course will focus on various topics of current interest related to faculty research experience." term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="20" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Christina Bailey-Hytholt" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Christina Bailey-Hytholt" room="Stratton Hall 202 (new) " seats="25" section="D01 - ST: Biomaterials for Women's Health" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course></dept><dept abbrev="CN" name="Chinese"><course course_desc=" CN 1541 Elementary Chinese I (1/3 unit; Cat. I) An intensive course to introduce Mandarin Chinese to students with no or little background in Chinese. Pronunciation, basic grammar rules, and character recognition will be the emphasis of the course. Major aspects of Chinese culture will be introduced throughout the course. Students who have previous Chinese language background should have a placement evaluation with the coordinator of the Chinese track before enrolling in Elementary Chinese I. This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers, except with written permission from the coordinator of the Chinese track. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Elementary Chinese I" number="1541"><section actual_waitlist="2" availableseats="-2" crn="000000000000334547" max_waitlist="9" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" CN 1541 Elementary Chinese I (1/3 unit; Cat. I) An intensive course to introduce Mandarin Chinese to students with no or little background in Chinese. Pronunciation, basic grammar rules, and character recognition will be the emphasis of the course. Major aspects of Chinese culture will be introduced throughout the course. Students who have previous Chinese language background should have a placement evaluation with the coordinator of the Chinese track before enrolling in Elementary Chinese I. This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers, except with written permission from the coordinator of the Chinese track. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="2" availableseats="-2" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="9" professor="Hsinhan Hung" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Hsinhan Hung" room="Salisbury Labs 407 " seats="18" section="A01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="2" availableseats="-1" crn="000000000000333899" max_waitlist="9" number="A02" part-of-term="A Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" CN 1541 Elementary Chinese I (1/3 unit; Cat. I) An intensive course to introduce Mandarin Chinese to students with no or little background in Chinese. Pronunciation, basic grammar rules, and character recognition will be the emphasis of the course. Major aspects of Chinese culture will be introduced throughout the course. Students who have previous Chinese language background should have a placement evaluation with the coordinator of the Chinese track before enrolling in Elementary Chinese I. This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers, except with written permission from the coordinator of the Chinese track. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="2" availableseats="-1" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="9" professor="Huili Zheng" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Huili Zheng" room="Salisbury Labs 011 " seats="18" section="A02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" crn="000000000000334446" max_waitlist="10" number="A03" part-of-term="A Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" CN 1541 Elementary Chinese I (1/3 unit; Cat. I) An intensive course to introduce Mandarin Chinese to students with no or little background in Chinese. Pronunciation, basic grammar rules, and character recognition will be the emphasis of the course. Major aspects of Chinese culture will be introduced throughout the course. Students who have previous Chinese language background should have a placement evaluation with the coordinator of the Chinese track before enrolling in Elementary Chinese I. This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers, except with written permission from the coordinator of the Chinese track. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Hsinhan Hung" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Hsinhan Hung" room="Kaven Hall 204 " seats="18" section="A03" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. IContinuation of CN 1541 for non-native, non-heritage speakers. Emphasis on oral communication and vocabulary acquisition continues. Basics of writing system introduced.Recommended background: CN 1541.This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Elementary Chinese II" number="1542"><section actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000335229" max_waitlist="5" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. IContinuation of CN 1541 for non-native, non-heritage speakers. Emphasis on oral communication and vocabulary acquisition continues. Basics of writing system introduced.Recommended background: CN 1541.This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Huili Zheng" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Huili Zheng" room="Salisbury Labs 406 " seats="18" section="B01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="2" crn="000000000000335230" max_waitlist="5" number="B02" part-of-term="B Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. IContinuation of CN 1541 for non-native, non-heritage speakers. Emphasis on oral communication and vocabulary acquisition continues. Basics of writing system introduced.Recommended background: CN 1541.This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="2" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Huili Zheng" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Huili Zheng" room="Higgins Labs 154 " seats="18" section="B02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" crn="000000000000346751" max_waitlist="5" number="B03" part-of-term="B Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" CN 1542 Elementary Chinese II (1/3 unit; Cat. I) A continuation of Elementary Chinese I, with progressive expansion of vocabulary and grammar. Pronunciation, basic grammar rules, and character recognition will continue to be the emphasis of the course. Major aspects of Chinese culture will be introduced throughout the course. This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers, except with written permission from the coordinator of the Chinese track. Recommendation Background: CN 1541 " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Not Assigned" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Not Assigned" room="Salisbury Labs 011 " seats="18" section="B03" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. IContinuation of CN 1542 Mandarin Chinese. Primary emphasis is on conversational skills, with increased character introduction. Recognition of the most-commonly-used Chinese characters will be required by term end.Recommended background: CN 1542.This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Elementary Chinese III" number="1543"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" crn="000000000000336494" max_waitlist="5" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. IContinuation of CN 1542 Mandarin Chinese. Primary emphasis is on conversational skills, with increased character introduction. Recognition of the most-commonly-used Chinese characters will be required by term end.Recommended background: CN 1542.This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Huili Zheng" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Huili Zheng" room="Salisbury Labs 407 " seats="18" section="C01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" crn="000000000000336748" max_waitlist="5" number="C02" part-of-term="C Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. I  A continuation of Elementary Chinese II, with progressive expansion of vocabulary and grammar. Pronunciation, basic grammar rules, and character recognition will continue to be the emphasis of the course. Handwriting of Chinese characters is not emphasized at this stage, and students are encouraged to typewrite the characters. Major aspects of Chinese culture will be introduced throughout the course.  Recommended background: CN 1542.   This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers, except with written permission from the coordinator of the Chinese track.  " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="15" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Huili Zheng" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Huili Zheng" room="Salisbury Labs 407 " seats="18" section="C02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. IContinuation of CN 1542. Course will focus on practical conversations and recognition of Chinese characters, with greater emphasis placed on reading and writing.Recommended background: CN 1543.This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Intermediate Chinese I" number="2541"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="16" crn="000000000000337482" max_waitlist="5" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. IContinuation of CN 1542. Course will focus on practical conversations and recognition of Chinese characters, with greater emphasis placed on reading and writing.Recommended background: CN 1543.This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="16" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Huili Zheng" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Huili Zheng" room="Kaven Hall 204 " seats="18" section="D01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" crn="000000000000337279" max_waitlist="5" number="D02" part-of-term="D Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. I  Building upon the foundation of the Elementary Chinese course series, this course is designed to expand students' skills in listening comprehension, speaking, reading, and writing. The course continues to enhance students’ vocabulary and introduces more complex grammatical patterns, with more emphasis placed on improving communication skills both orally and in writing.  Recommended background: CN 1543.   This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers, except with written permission from the coordinator of the Chinese track.  " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Huili Zheng" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Huili Zheng" room="Kaven Hall 115 " seats="18" section="D02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course will build on intermediate Chinese conversational patterns. Class time will focus on dialogue and mastery of grammatical constructions, as well as character recognition and reading ability. Conversational drills, audio recordings, video, and group interaction will enhance classroom learning. Recommended background: CN 2541 Intermediate Chinese I or the equivalent.  This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor.  " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Intermediate Chinese II" number="2542"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" crn="000000000000333901" max_waitlist="10" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course will build on intermediate Chinese conversational patterns. Class time will focus on dialogue and mastery of grammatical constructions, as well as character recognition and reading ability. Conversational drills, audio recordings, video, and group interaction will enhance classroom learning. Recommended background: CN 2541 Intermediate Chinese I or the equivalent.  This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor.  " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Wen-Hua Du" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Wen-Hua Du" room="Innovation Studio 205 Active Learning Classroom North " seats="18" section="A01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" crn="000000000000334073" max_waitlist="10" number="A02" part-of-term="A Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. I  A continuation of Intermediate Chinese I. This course is designed to expand students' skills in listening comprehension, speaking, reading, and writing. The course continues to enhance students’ vocabulary and introduces more complex grammatical patterns, with more emphasis placed on improving communication skills both orally and in writing.  Recommended background: CN 2541.   This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers, except with written permission from the coordinator of the Chinese track.  " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Hsinhan Hung" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Hsinhan Hung" room="Kaven Hall 204 " seats="18" section="A02" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I Continuation of CN 2542. This course continues to build on students’ Chinese conversational skills with a focus on dialogue and mastery of grammatical constructions, as well as character recognition and reading ability. Conversational drills, audio recordings, video, and group interaction will enhance classroom learning. Recommended background: CN 2542 or the equivalent. This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Intermediate Chinese III" number="2543"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" crn="000000000000335231" max_waitlist="10" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. I Continuation of CN 2542. This course continues to build on students’ Chinese conversational skills with a focus on dialogue and mastery of grammatical constructions, as well as character recognition and reading ability. Conversational drills, audio recordings, video, and group interaction will enhance classroom learning. Recommended background: CN 2542 or the equivalent. This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Wen-Hua Du" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Wen-Hua Du" room="Olin Hall 126 " seats="18" section="B01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000334696" max_waitlist="10" number="B02" part-of-term="B Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. I  A continuation of Intermediate Chinese II. This course is designed to expand students' skills in listening comprehension, speaking, reading, and writing. The course continues to enhance students’ vocabulary and introduces more complex grammatical patterns, with more emphasis placed on improving communication skills both orally and in writing.  Recommended background: CN 2542.  This course satisfies the Inquiry Practicum requirement.   This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers, except with written permission from the coordinator of the Chinese track.  " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Wen-Hua Du" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Wen-Hua Du" room="Olin Hall 126 " seats="18" section="B02" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. IContinuation of CN 2543. Students continue to build their conversational skills through more complex dialogue and more complicated grammatical constructions. Character recognition and reading ability become more central to class assignments. Conversational drills, audio recordings, video, and group interaction will enhance classroom learning.Recommended background: CN 2543 or equivalent.This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Intermediate Chinese IV" number="2544"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" crn="000000000000336515" max_waitlist="5" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. IContinuation of CN 2543. Students continue to build their conversational skills through more complex dialogue and more complicated grammatical constructions. Character recognition and reading ability become more central to class assignments. Conversational drills, audio recordings, video, and group interaction will enhance classroom learning.Recommended background: CN 2543 or equivalent.This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Hsinhan Hung" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Hsinhan Hung" room="Washburn 323 " seats="18" section="C01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. IThis course focuses on increasingly sophisticated conversational patterns as well as acquiring the vocabulary necessary for reading texts. Emphasis is on developing active skills to move students to a high-intermediate level of proficiency in reading, writing, listening, and speaking, with continued attention on grammar, phrases, sentence patterns, and character recognition.Recommended background: CN 2544 or the equivalent.This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Advanced Chinese I" number="3541"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="9" crn="000000000000336985" max_waitlist="5" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis course focuses on increasingly sophisticated conversational patterns as well as acquiring the vocabulary necessary for reading texts. Emphasis is on developing active skills to move students to a high-intermediate level of proficiency in reading, writing, listening, and speaking, with continued attention on grammar, phrases, sentence patterns, and character recognition.Recommended background: CN 2544 or the equivalent.This course is closed to native speakers of Chinese and heritage speakers except with written permission from the instructor. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="9" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Hsinhan Hung" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Hsinhan Hung" room="Salisbury Labs 011 " seats="18" section="D01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I  This course builds on advanced intermediate Chinese skills, focusing on both conversational patterns and reading/writing. Class time will focus on dialogue and mastery of increasingly complex grammatical constructions, with emphasis on character recognition and production for reading and writing. Emphasis will be placed on integrating materials in real-world applications. Not open to native or heritage speakers without written permission of instructor.  Recommended background: CN 3541 Advanced Intermediate Chinese I or equivalent. Students may not receive credit for both CN 3542 and CN 354X. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Advanced Chinese II" number="3542"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" crn="000000000000333904" max_waitlist="10" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. I  This course builds on advanced intermediate Chinese skills, focusing on both conversational patterns and reading/writing. Class time will focus on dialogue and mastery of increasingly complex grammatical constructions, with emphasis on character recognition and production for reading and writing. Emphasis will be placed on integrating materials in real-world applications. Not open to native or heritage speakers without written permission of instructor.  Recommended background: CN 3541 Advanced Intermediate Chinese I or equivalent. Students may not receive credit for both CN 3542 and CN 354X. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Wen-Hua Du" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Wen-Hua Du" room="Stratton Hall 205 " seats="18" section="A01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course continues to build on students’ advanced intermediate Chinese skills with increasing emphasis on reading and writing. Writing assignments will be geared towards expressing more complex topics in Chinese that are related to cultural phenomena in contemporary Chinese societies. Not open to native or heritage speakers without written permission of instructor. Recommended background: CN 3542 Advanced Intermediate Chinese II or equivalent. Students may not receive credit for both CN 3543 and CN 355X. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Advanced Chinese III" number="3543"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="12" crn="000000000000335233" max_waitlist="10" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course continues to build on students’ advanced intermediate Chinese skills with increasing emphasis on reading and writing. Writing assignments will be geared towards expressing more complex topics in Chinese that are related to cultural phenomena in contemporary Chinese societies. Not open to native or heritage speakers without written permission of instructor. Recommended background: CN 3542 Advanced Intermediate Chinese II or equivalent. Students may not receive credit for both CN 3543 and CN 355X. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="12" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Hsinhan Hung" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Hsinhan Hung" room="Washburn 323 " seats="18" section="B01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" CN 3571 Contemporary China: Culture and Trends (Cat II) This advanced language course aims to develop students’ in-depth understanding and perspectives on social-cultural issues in contemporary Greater China. Building upon the foundation of CN3543 Advanced Chinese III, this course continues to advance students’ language skills through the exploration of topics that reflect the transitional changes of traditional Chinese cultural values due to the influence of Western culture and the impact of globalization. Students will learn essential vocabulary to understand, discuss, analyze, and examine the unique cultural phenomena associated with the selected topics through comparison with their own culture. Students who have completed CN 357X cannot receive credit for CN 3571. Recommended Background: This course is designed for CFL (Chinese as a Foreign Language) and CHL (Chinese as a Heritage) students who have completed the prerequisite of CN3543 or equivalen " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Contemporary China: Culture and Trends" number="3571"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" crn="000000000000339269" max_waitlist="5" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="18" sec_desc=" CN 3571 Contemporary China: Culture and Trends (Cat II) This advanced language course aims to develop students’ in-depth understanding and perspectives on social-cultural issues in contemporary Greater China. Building upon the foundation of CN3543 Advanced Chinese III, this course continues to advance students’ language skills through the exploration of topics that reflect the transitional changes of traditional Chinese cultural values due to the influence of Western culture and the impact of globalization. Students will learn essential vocabulary to understand, discuss, analyze, and examine the unique cultural phenomena associated with the selected topics through comparison with their own culture. Students who have completed CN 357X cannot receive credit for CN 3571. Recommended Background: This course is designed for CFL (Chinese as a Foreign Language) and CHL (Chinese as a Heritage) students who have completed the prerequisite of CN3543 or equivalen " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="11" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Hsinhan Hung" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Hsinhan Hung" room="Atwater Kent 232 " seats="18" section="C01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course></dept><dept abbrev="CS" name="Computer Science"><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces students to the fundamental principles of programming in imperative and scripting languages. Topics include control structures, iterators, functional decomposition, basic data structures (such as records). Students will be expected to implement, test and debug programs. Through the use of compelling applications and lab exercises, students will learn how to interface with external data systems and control devices. Recommended background: none. All Computer Science students and other students wishing to prepare for 3000-level courses in Computer Science should take CS 1101/1102 instead of CS 1004. This course provides sufficient background for CS 2301 Systems Programming for Non-Majors.  Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 1005. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Introduction To Programming For Non-Majors" number="1004"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000338675338676" max_waitlist="0" number="CL01/CX01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="24" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces students to the fundamental principles of programming in imperative and scripting languages. Topics include control structures, iterators, functional decomposition, basic data structures (such as records). Students will be expected to implement, test and debug programs. Through the use of compelling applications and lab exercises, students will learn how to interface with external data systems and control devices. Recommended background: none. All Computer Science students and other students wishing to prepare for 3000-level courses in Computer Science should take CS 1101/1102 instead of CS 1004. This course provides sufficient background for CS 2301 Systems Programming for Non-Majors.  Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 1005. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="6" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Jennifer Mortensen" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Jennifer Mortensen" room="Unity Hall 420 " seats="72" section="CL01" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="wed" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="No