eats="27" sec_desc=" This course introduces principles of computation and programming with an emphasis on program design. Topics include the design, implementation, and testing of programs that use a variety of data structures (such as structures, lists, and trees), functions, conditionals, recursion, and higher-order functions. Students will be expected to design simple data models, and implement and debug programs in a functional programming language.Recommended background: none. Either CS 1101 or CS 1102 provides sufficient background for further courses in the CS department. Undergraduate credit may not be earned for both this course and CS 1102. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="99" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Matthew Ahrens" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Matthew Ahrens" room="Salisbury Labs 115 Kinnicutt Hall " seats="135" section="CL01" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="25" building="" days="wed" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Not Assigned" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Not Assigned" room="Salisbury Labs 123 Computer Lab " seats="27" section="CX04" starts="2:00PM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" In the first half of the term, this course covers the same functional programming material as CS 1101 at roughly twice the pace. The second half of the term is a preview of selected advanced Computer Science topics, such as the design and implementation of application-specific languages, macros, programming with the HTTP protocol and continuation-passing style. Students will be expected to complete an open-ended individual programming project.Recommended background: Substantial prior programming experience (including functions, recursion, and lists, as would be covered in high-school Advanced Placement Computer Science A courses, but not necessarily AP CS Principles courses). Either CS 1101 or CS 1102 provides sufficient background for further courses in the CS department. Undergraduate credit may not be earned for both this course and CS 1101. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Accelerated Introduction To Program Design" number="1102"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" crn="000000334127334131" max_waitlist="0" number="AL01/AX01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" In the first half of the term, this course covers the same functional programming material as CS 1101 at roughly twice the pace. The second half of the term is a preview of selected advanced Computer Science topics, such as the design and implementation of application-specific languages, macros, programming with the HTTP protocol and continuation-passing style. Students will be expected to complete an open-ended individual programming project.Recommended background: Substantial prior programming experience (including functions, recursion, and lists, as would be covered in high-school Advanced Placement Computer Science A courses, but not necessarily AP CS Principles courses). Either CS 1101 or CS 1102 provides sufficient background for further courses in the CS department. Undergraduate credit may not be earned for both this course and CS 1101. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="12" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Sakire Arslan Ay" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Sakire Arslan Ay" room="Fuller Labs PHL Perreault Hall - Lower Section " seats="100" section="AL01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" building="" days="wed" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Sakire Arslan Ay" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Sakire Arslan Ay" room="Kaven Hall 202 Stats Computer Lab " seats="25" section="AX01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" crn="000000334127334128" max_waitlist="0" number="AL01/AX02" part-of-term="A Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" In the first half of the term, this course covers the same functional programming material as CS 1101 at roughly twice the pace. The second half of the term is a preview of selected advanced Computer Science topics, such as the design and implementation of application-specific languages, macros, programming with the HTTP protocol and continuation-passing style. Students will be expected to complete an open-ended individual programming project.Recommended background: Substantial prior programming experience (including functions, recursion, and lists, as would be covered in high-school Advanced Placement Computer Science A courses, but not necessarily AP CS Principles courses). Either CS 1101 or CS 1102 provides sufficient background for further courses in the CS department. Undergraduate credit may not be earned for both this course and CS 1101. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="12" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Sakire Arslan Ay" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Sakire Arslan Ay" room="Fuller Labs PHL Perreault Hall - Lower Section " seats="100" section="AL01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" building="" days="wed" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Sakire Arslan Ay" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Sakire Arslan Ay" room="Kaven Hall 202 Stats Computer Lab " seats="25" section="AX02" starts="2:00PM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" crn="000000334127334129" max_waitlist="0" number="AL01/AX03" part-of-term="A Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" In the first half of the term, this course covers the same functional programming material as CS 1101 at roughly twice the pace. The second half of the term is a preview of selected advanced Computer Science topics, such as the design and implementation of application-specific languages, macros, programming with the HTTP protocol and continuation-passing style. Students will be expected to complete an open-ended individual programming project.Recommended background: Substantial prior programming experience (including functions, recursion, and lists, as would be covered in high-school Advanced Placement Computer Science A courses, but not necessarily AP CS Principles courses). Either CS 1101 or CS 1102 provides sufficient background for further courses in the CS department. Undergraduate credit may not be earned for both this course and CS 1101. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="12" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Sakire Arslan Ay" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Sakire Arslan Ay" room="Fuller Labs PHL Perreault Hall - Lower Section " seats="100" section="AL01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" building="" days="wed" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Sakire Arslan Ay" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Sakire Arslan Ay" room="Kaven Hall 202 Stats Computer Lab " seats="25" section="AX03" starts="3:00PM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" crn="000000334127334130" max_waitlist="0" number="AL01/AX04" part-of-term="A Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" In the first half of the term, this course covers the same functional programming material as CS 1101 at roughly twice the pace. The second half of the term is a preview of selected advanced Computer Science topics, such as the design and implementation of application-specific languages, macros, programming with the HTTP protocol and continuation-passing style. Students will be expected to complete an open-ended individual programming project.Recommended background: Substantial prior programming experience (including functions, recursion, and lists, as would be covered in high-school Advanced Placement Computer Science A courses, but not necessarily AP CS Principles courses). Either CS 1101 or CS 1102 provides sufficient background for further courses in the CS department. Undergraduate credit may not be earned for both this course and CS 1101. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="12" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Sakire Arslan Ay" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Sakire Arslan Ay" room="Fuller Labs PHL Perreault Hall - Lower Section " seats="100" section="AL01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" building="" days="wed" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Sakire Arslan Ay" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Sakire Arslan Ay" room="Kaven Hall 202 Stats Computer Lab " seats="25" section="AX04" starts="12:00PM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces students to the structure and behavior of modern digital computers and the way they execute programs. Machine organization topics include the Von Neumann model of execution, functional organization of computer hardware, the memory hierarchy, caching performance, and pipelining. Assembly language topics include representations of numbers in computers, basic instruction sets, addressing modes, stacks and procedures, low-level I/O, and the functions of compilers, assemblers, linkers, and loaders. The course also presents how code and data structures of higher-level languages are mapped into the assembly language and machine representations of a modern processor. Programming projects will be carried out in the C language and the assembly language of a modern processor. Recommended background: CS 2301 or CS 2303, or a significant knowledge of C/C++. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Introduction To Machine Organization And Assembly Language" number="2011"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000335402335404" max_waitlist="0" number="BL01/BX01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces students to the structure and behavior of modern digital computers and the way they execute programs. Machine organization topics include the Von Neumann model of execution, functional organization of computer hardware, the memory hierarchy, caching performance, and pipelining. Assembly language topics include representations of numbers in computers, basic instruction sets, addressing modes, stacks and procedures, low-level I/O, and the functions of compilers, assemblers, linkers, and loaders. The course also presents how code and data structures of higher-level languages are mapped into the assembly language and machine representations of a modern processor. Programming projects will be carried out in the C language and the assembly language of a modern processor. Recommended background: CS 2301 or CS 2303, or a significant knowledge of C/C++. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Taylor Andrews" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Taylor Andrews" room="Fuller Labs PHL Perreault Hall - Lower Section " seats="102" section="BL01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="wed" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Taylor Andrews" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Taylor Andrews" room="Atwater Kent 013 Computer Lab " seats="20" section="BX01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000335402335405" max_waitlist="0" number="BL01/BX02" part-of-term="B Term" seats="21" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces students to the structure and behavior of modern digital computers and the way they execute programs. Machine organization topics include the Von Neumann model of execution, functional organization of computer ha