02, CS 210X, CS 2103. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="12" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Joshua Cuneo" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Joshua Cuneo" room="Unity Hall 400 " seats="100" section="BL01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" building="" days="thu" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Not Assigned" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Not Assigned" room="Fuller Labs A21 Zoo Lab Restricted " seats="25" section="BX04" starts="1:00PM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. IThis course introduces students to an object-oriented model of programming, with an emphasis on the programming approaches useful in creating software applications. Students will be expected to design, implement, and debug object-oriented programs. Topics include inheritance, user interfaces, and database access. This course is for non-CS majors with prior programming experience and an interest in building software applications.Recommended background: Some programming experience such as found in CS 1101, CS 1102, or CS 1004. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Application Building With Object-Oriented Concepts" number="2119"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="6" crn="000000336914336926" max_waitlist="0" number="DL01/DX01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="24" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis course introduces students to an object-oriented model of programming, with an emphasis on the programming approaches useful in creating software applications. Students will be expected to design, implement, and debug object-oriented programs. Topics include inheritance, user interfaces, and database access. This course is for non-CS majors with prior programming experience and an interest in building software applications.Recommended background: Some programming experience such as found in CS 1101, CS 1102, or CS 1004. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="28" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Shubbhi Taneja" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Shubbhi Taneja" room="Salisbury Labs 104 " seats="48" section="DL01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="6" building="" days="wed" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Shubbhi Taneja" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Shubbhi Taneja" room="Atwater Kent 013 Computer Lab " seats="24" section="DX01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lab"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="22" crn="000000336914336915" max_waitlist="0" number="DL01/DX02" part-of-term="D Term" seats="24" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis course introduces students to an object-oriented model of programming, with an emphasis on the programming approaches useful in creating software applications. Students will be expected to design, implement, and debug object-oriented programs. Topics include inheritance, user interfaces, and database access. This course is for non-CS majors with prior programming experience and an interest in building software applications.Recommended background: Some programming experience such as found in CS 1101, CS 1102, or CS 1004. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="28" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Shubbhi Taneja" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Shubbhi Taneja" room="Salisbury Labs 104 " seats="48" section="DL01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="22" building="" days="wed" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Shubbhi Taneja" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Shubbhi Taneja" room="Atwater Kent 013 Computer Lab " seats="24" section="DX02" starts="9:00AM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I Building on a fundamental knowledge of data structures, data abstraction techniques, and mathematical tools, a number of examples of algorithm design and analysis — worst case and average case — will be developed. Topics include greedy algorithms, divide-and-conquer, dynamic programming, heuristics, and probabilistic algorithms. Problems will be drawn from areas such as sorting, graph theory, and string processing. The influence of the computational model on algorithm design will be discussed. Students will be expected to perform analysis on a variety of algorithms. Recommended background: CS 2102 or CS 2103, and CS 2022. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Algorithms" number="2223"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000333994" max_waitlist="20" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="80" sec_desc=" Cat. I Building on a fundamental knowledge of data structures, data abstraction techniques, and mathematical tools, a number of examples of algorithm design and analysis — worst case and average case — will be developed. Topics include greedy algorithms, divide-and-conquer, dynamic programming, heuristics, and probabilistic algorithms. Problems will be drawn from areas such as sorting, graph theory, and string processing. The influence of the computational model on algorithm design will be discussed. Students will be expected to perform analysis on a variety of algorithms. Recommended background: CS 2102 or CS 2103, and CS 2022. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Yu-Shan Sun" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Yu-Shan Sun" room="Higgins Labs 116 " seats="80" section="A01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="35" crn="000000000000335068" max_waitlist="70" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="140" sec_desc=" Cat. IBuilding on a fundamental knowledge of data structures, data abstraction techniques, and mathematical tools, a number of examples 