tual_waitlist="0" availableseats="122" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Yu-Shan Sun" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Yu-Shan Sun" room="Fuller Labs PHU Perreault Hall - Upper Section " seats="140" section="CL02" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="22" building="" days="wed" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Not Assigned" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Not Assigned" room="Kaven Hall 203 CARS Computer Lab " seats="25" section="CX17" starts="3:00PM" type="Lab"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course provides the student with an understanding of the basic components of a general-purpose operating system. Topics include processes, process management, synchronization, input/output devices and their programming, interrupts, memory management, resource allocation, and an introduction to file systems. Students will be expected to design and implement a large piece of system software in the C programming language. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 502. Recommended background: CS 2303 or CS 2301, and CS 2011. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Operating Systems" number="3013"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="27" crn="000000000000334178" max_waitlist="20" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="102" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course provides the student with an understanding of the basic components of a general-purpose operating system. Topics include processes, process management, synchronization, input/output devices and their programming, interrupts, memory management, resource allocation, and an introduction to file systems. Students will be expected to design and implement a large piece of system software in the C programming language. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 502. Recommended background: CS 2303 or CS 2301, and CS 2011. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="27" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Craig Wills" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Craig Wills" room="Fuller Labs PHL Perreault Hall - Lower Section " seats="102" section="A01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" crn="000000000000334166" max_waitlist="10" number="A02" part-of-term="A Term" seats="75" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course provides the student with an understanding of the basic components of a general-purpose operating system. Topics include processes, process management, synchronization, input/output devices and their programming, interrupts, memory management, resource allocation, and an introduction to file systems. Students will be expected to design and implement a large piece of system software in the C programming language. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 502. Recommended background: CS 2303 or CS 2301, and CS 2011. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Craig Shue" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Craig Shue" room="Unity Hall 520 " seats="75" section="A02" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="32" crn="000000000000334348" max_waitlist="15" number="A03" part-of-term="A Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course provides the student with an understanding of the basic components of a general-purpose operating system. Topics include processes, process management, synchronization, input/output devices and their programming, interrupts, memory management, resource allocation, and an introduction to file systems. Students will be expected to design and implement a large piece of system software in the C programming language. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 502. Recommended background: CS 2303 or CS 2301, and CS 2011. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="32" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Mark Sheahan" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Mark Sheahan" room="Kaven Hall 116 " seats="60" section="A03" starts="8:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" crn="000000000000336378" max_waitlist="20" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="80" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis course provides the student with an understanding of the basic components of a general-purpose operating system. Topics include processes, process management, synchronization, input/output devices and their programming, interrupts, memory management, resource allocation, and an introduction to file systems. Students will be expected to design and implement a large piece ofsystem software in the C programming language.Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 502.Recommended background: CS 2303 or CS 2301, and CS 2011. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Shubbhi Taneja" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Shubbhi Taneja" room="Washburn 229 " seats="80" section="C01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" crn="000000000000336451" max_waitlist="20" number="C02" part-of-term="C Term" seats="75" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis course provides the student with an understanding of the basic components of a general-purpose operating system. Topics include processes, process management, synchronization, input/output devices and their programming, interrupts, memory management, resource allocation, and an introduction to file systems. Students will be expected to design and implement a large piece ofsystem software in the C programming language.Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 502.Recommended background: CS 2303 or CS 2301, and CS 2011. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="24" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Shubbhi Taneja" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Shubbhi Taneja" room="Unity Hall 520 " seats="75" section="C02" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="42" crn="000000000000336461" max_waitlist="15" number="C03" part-of-term="C Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course provides the student with an understanding of the basic components of a general-purpose operating system. Topics include processes, process management, synchronization, input/output devices and their programming, interrupts, memory management, resource allocation, and an introduction to file systems. Students will be expected to design and implement a large piece of system software in the C programming language. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 502. Recommended background: CS 2303 or CS 2301, and CS 2011. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="42" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Mark Sheahan" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Mark Sheahan" room="Salisbury Labs 411 " seats="60" section="C03" starts="8:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course develops in the student an understanding of the nature and importance of problems concerning the efficiency and effectiveness of human interaction with computer-based systems. Topics include the design and evaluation of interactive computer systems, basic psychological considerations of interaction, interactive language design, interactive hardware design, and special input/output techniques. Students will be expected to complete several projects. A project might be a software evaluation, interface development, or an experiment. Recommended background: CS 2102, CS 2103, or CS 2119. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Human-Computer Interaction" number="3041"><section actual_waitlist="3" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000335073" max_waitlist="5" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course develops in the student an understanding of the nature and importance of problems concerning the efficiency and effectiveness of human interaction with computer-based systems. Topics include the design and evaluation of interactive computer systems, basic psychological considerations of interaction, interactive language design, interactive hardware design, and special input/output techniques. Students will be expected to complete several projects. A project might be a software evaluation, interface development, or an experiment. Recommended background: CS 2102, CS 2103, or CS 2119. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="3" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Matthew Ahrens" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Matthew Ahrens" room="Innovation Studio 205 Active Learning Classroom North " seats="50" section="B01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="47" crn="000000000000347119" max_waitlist="15" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="50" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course develops in the student an understanding of the nature and importance of problems concerning the efficiency and effectiveness of human interaction with computer-based systems. Topics include the design and evaluation of interactive computer systems, basic psychological considerations of interaction, interactive language design, interactive hardware design, and special input/output techniques. Students will be expected to complete several projects. A project might be a software evaluation, interface development, or an experiment. Recommended background: CS 2102, CS 2103, or CS 2119. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="47" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Maxim Lisnic" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Maxim Lisnic" room="Innovation Studio 205 Active Learning Classroom North " seats="50" section="C01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" crn="000000000000338752" max_waitlist="5" number="D02" part-of-term="D Term" seats="54" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course develops in the student an understanding of the nature and importance of problems concerning the efficiency and effectiveness of human interaction with computer-based systems. Topics include the design and evaluation of interactive computer systems, basic psychological considerations of interaction, interactive language design, interactive hardware design, and special input/output techniques. Students will be expected to complete several projects. A project might be a software evaluation, interface development, or an experiment. Recommended background: CS 2102, CS 2103, or CS 2119. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="3" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Erin Solovey" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Erin Solovey" room="Innovation Studio 205 Active Learning Classroom North " seats="54" section="D02" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course makes the student aware of the social, moral, ethical, and philosophical impact of computers and computer-based systems on society, both now and in the future. Topics include major computer-based applications and their impact, human-machine relationships, and the major problems of controlling the use of computers. Students will be expected to contribute to classroom discussions and to complete a number of significant writing assignments. This course is recommended for juniors and seniors. Recommended background: a general knowledge of computers and computer systems. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Social Implications Of Information Processing" number="3043"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000334480" max_waitlist="15" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="30" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course makes the student aware of the social, moral, ethical, and philosophical impact of computers and computer-based systems on society, both now and in the future. Topics include major computer-based applications and their impact, human-machine relationships, and the major problems of controlling the use of computers. Students will be expected to contribute to classroom discussions and to complete a number of significant writing assignments. This course is recommended for juniors and seniors. Recommended background: a general knowledge of computers and computer systems. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="5:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Keith Pray" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Keith Pray" room="Innovation Studio 205 Active Learning Classroom North " seats="30" section="A01" starts="4:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000334481" max_waitlist="10" number="A02" part-of-term="A Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course makes the student aware of the social, moral, ethical, and philosophical impact of computers and computer-based systems on society, both now and in the future. Topics include major computer-based applications and their impact, human-machine relationships, and the major problems of controlling the use of computers. Students will be expected to contribute to classroom discussions and to complete a number of significant writing assignments. This course is recommended for juniors and seniors. Recommended background: a general knowledge of computers and computer systems. " term="20220