y, including NP-completeness. Students will be expected to complete a variety of exercises and proofs. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 5003. Recommended Background: Discrete mathematics (CS 2022 or equivalent) and algorithms (CS 2223 or equivalent). Students who have credit for CS 4121 may not receive credit for CS 3133. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Foundations Of Computer Science" number="3133"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="49" crn="000000000000334482" max_waitlist="20" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="100" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the theoretical foundations of computer science. These form the basis for a more complete understanding of the proficiency in computer science. Topics include computational models, formal languages, and an introduction to compatibility and complexity theory, including NP-completeness. Students will be expected to complete a variety of exercises and proofs. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 5003. Recommended Background: Discrete mathematics (CS 2022 or equivalent) and algorithms (CS 2223 or equivalent). Students who have credit for CS 4121 may not receive credit for CS 3133. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="49" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Hanmeng Zhan" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Hanmeng Zhan" room="Salisbury Labs 115 Kinnicutt Hall " seats="100" section="A01" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="41" crn="000000000000335570" max_waitlist="20" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="100" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the theoretical foundations of computer science. These form the basis for a more complete understanding of the proficiency in computer science. Topics include computational models, formal languages, and an introduction to compatibility and complexity theory, including NP-completeness. Students will be expected to complete a variety of exercises and proofs. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 5003. Recommended Background: Discrete mathematics (CS 2022 or equivalent) and algorithms (CS 2223 or equivalent). Students who have credit for CS 4121 may not receive credit for CS 3133. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="41" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Gabor Sarkozy" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Gabor Sarkozy" room="Salisbury Labs 115 Kinnicutt Hall " seats="100" section="B01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" crn="000000000000336386" max_waitlist="20" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="100" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis course introduces the theoretical foundations of computer science. These form the basis for a more complete understanding of the proficiency in computer science.Topics include computational models, formal languages, and an introduction to compatibility and complexity theory, including NP-completeness.Students will be expected to complete a variety of exercises and proofs.Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 5003.Recommended Background: Discrete mathematics (CS 2022 or equivalent) and algorithms (CS 2223 or equivalent).Students who have credit for CS 4121 may not receive credit for CS 3133. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Michael Engling" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Michael Engling" room="Unity Hall 400 " seats="100" section="C01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="30" crn="000000000000337593" max_waitlist="20" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="100" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the theoretical foundations of computer science. These form the basis for a more complete understanding of the proficiency in computer science. Topics include computational models, formal languages, and an introduction to compatibility and complexity theory, including NP-completeness. Students will be expected to complete a variety of exercises and proofs. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 5003. Recommended Background: Discrete mathematics (CS 2022 or equivalent) and algorithms (CS 2223 or equivalent). Students who have credit for CS 4121 may not receive credit for CS 3133. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="30" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Daniel Reichman" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Daniel Reichman" room="Salisbury Labs 115 Kinnicutt Hall " seats="100" section="D01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc="   CS 340X Introduction to Security. Category 2.       This course provides students with an introduction to security, covering both technical and social aspects. Students will develop an understanding of security that will serve as the foundation for 4K-level security-focused courses. Topics may include foundational security concepts (e.g., access control), common attacks (e.g., social engineering) and defenses, case studies of large-scale attacks and failures (e.g., Heartbleed), and security processes (e.g., software testing and vulnerability disclosure).        Recommended Background: Basic knowledge of program design and programming languages. (CS 2303 or equivalent).       Suggested Background: None    " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Introduction to Security" number="340X"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="2" crn="000000000000338695" max_waitlist="20" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="50" sec_desc="   CS 340X Introduction to Security. Category 2.       This course provides students with an introduction to security, covering both technical and social aspects. Students will develop an understanding of security that will serve as the foundation for 4K-level security-focused courses. Topics may include foundational security concepts (e.g., access control), common attacks (e.g., social engineering) and defenses, case studies of large-scale attacks and failures (e.g., Heartbleed), and security processes (e.g., software testing and vulnerability disclosure).        Recommended Background: Basic knowledge of program design and programming languages. (CS 2303 or equivalent).       Suggested Background: None    " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="2" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Robert Walls" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Robert Walls" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="50" section="C01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the student to the design, use, and application of database management systems. Topics include the relational data model, relational query languages, design theory, and conceptual data design and modeling for relational database design. Techniques that provide for data independence and minimal redundancy will be discussed. Students will be expected to design and implement database system applications. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 4431 or CS 542. Recommended background: CS 2022 and either CS 2102, CS 2103, or CS 2119. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Database Systems I" number="3431"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="-1" crn="000000000000334483" max_waitlist="40" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="75" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the student to the design, use, and application of database management systems. Topics include the relational data model, relational query languages, design theory, and conceptual data design and modeling for relational database design. Techniques that provide for data independence and minimal redundancy will be discussed. Students will be expected to design and implement database system applications. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 4431 or CS 542. Recommended background: CS 2022 and either CS 2102, CS 2103, or CS 2119. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="-1" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="40" professor="Wilson Wong" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Wilson Wong" room="Washburn 229 " seats="75" section="A01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="22" crn="000000000000333990" max_waitlist="40" number="A02" part-of-term="A Term" seats="75" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the student to the design, use, and application of database management systems. Topics include the relational data model, relational query languages, design theory, and conceptual data design and modeling for relational database design. Techniques that provide for data independence and minimal redundancy will be discussed. Students will be expected to design and implement database system applications. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 4431 or CS 542. Recommended background: CS 2022 and either CS 2102, CS 2103, or CS 2119. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="22" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="40" professor="Rodica Neamtu" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Rodica Neamtu" room="Washburn 229 " seats="75" section="A02" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="31" crn="000000000000335126" max_waitlist="20" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="75" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the student to the design, use, and application of database management systems. Topics include the relational data model, relational query languages, design theory, and conceptual data design and modeling for relational database design. Techniques that provide for data independence and minimal redundancy will be discussed. Students will be expected to design and implement database system applications. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 4431 or CS 542. Recommended background: CS 2022 and either CS 2102, CS 2103, or CS 2119. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="31" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Rodica Neamtu" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Rodica Neamtu" room="Unity Hall 420 " seats="75" section="B01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="46" crn="000000000000336387" max_waitlist="20" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="75" sec_desc=" Cat. IThis course introduces the student to the design, use, and application of database management systems.Topics include the relational data model, relational query languages, design theory, and conceptual data design and modeling for relational database design. Techniques that provide for data independence and minimal redundancy will be discussed.Students will be expected to design and implement database system applications.Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 4431or CS 542.Recommended background: CS 2022 and either CS 2102, CS 2103, or CS 2119. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="46" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Rodica Neamtu" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Rodica Neamtu" room="Fuller Labs PHL Perreault Hall - Lower Section " seats="75" section="C01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" crn="000000000000337440" max_waitlist="20" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="75" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the student to the design, use, and application of database management systems. Topics include the relational data model, relational query languages, design theory, and conceptual data design and modeling for relational database design. Techniques that provide for data independence and minimal redundancy will be discussed. Students will be expected to design and implement database system applications. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 4431 or CS 542. Recommended background: CS 2022 and either CS 2102, CS 2103, or CS 2119. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="10" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Sakire Arslan Ay" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Sakire Arslan Ay" room="Unity Hall 420 " seats="75" section="D01" starts="12:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course provides a broad view of computer networks. The course exposes students to all seven layers of OSI Reference Model while providing an introduction into newer topics such as wireless networking and Internet traffic concerns. The objective is to focus on an understanding of fundamental concepts of modern computer network architecture from a design and performance perspective. Topics covered include physical layer considerations, network protocols, wide area networks, local area networks, wireless networks, switches and routing, congestion, Internet traffic, and network security. Students will be expected to do extensive systems/network programming and will be expected to make use of simulation and measurement tools to gain an appreciation of current network design and performance issues. This course is also highly recommended for RBE and IMGD majors. Recommended background: CS 2301 or CS 2303, or a significant knowledge of C/C++. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Computer Networks" number="3516"><section actual_waitlist="8" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000335077" max_waitlist="20" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="70" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course provides a broad view of computer networks. The course exposes students to all seven layers of OSI Reference Model while providing an introduction into newer topics such as wireless networking and Internet traffic concerns. The objective is to focus on an understanding of fundamental concepts of modern computer network architecture from a design and performance perspective. Topics covered include physical layer considerations, network protocols, wide area networks, local area networks, wireless networks, switches and routing, congestion, Internet traffic, and network security. Students will be expected to do extensive systems/network programming and will be expected to make use of simulation and measurement tools to gain an appreciation of current network design and performance issues. This course is also highly recommended for RBE and IMGD majors. Recommended background: CS 2301 or CS 2303, or a significant knowledge of C/C++. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="8" availableseats="0" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Xiaoyan Sun" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Xiaoyan Sun" room="Salisbury Labs 115 Kinnicutt Hall " seats="70" section="B01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" crn="000000000000336768" max_waitlist="0" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course provides a broad view of computer networks. The course exposes students to all seven layers of OSI Reference Model while providing an introduction into newer topics such as wireless networking and Internet traffic concerns. The objective is to focus on an understanding of fundamental concepts of modern computer network architecture from a design and performance perspective. Topics covered include physical layer considerations, network protocols, wide area networks, local area networks, wireless networks, switches and routing, congestion, Internet traffic, and network security. Students will be expected to do extensive systems/network programming and will be expected to make use of simulation and measurement tools to gain an appreciation of current network design and performance issues. This course is also highly recommended for RBE and IMGD majors. Recommended background: CS 2301 or CS 2303, or a significant knowledge of C/C++. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Xiaoyan Sun" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Xiaoyan Sun" room="Fuller Labs 320 " seats="60" section="C01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" crn="000000000000337265" max_waitlist="20" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="65" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course provides a broad view of computer networks. The course exposes students to all seven layers of OSI Reference Model while providing an introduction into newer topics such as wireless networking and Internet traffic concerns. The objective is to focus on an understanding of fundamental concepts of modern computer network architecture from a design and performance perspective. Topics covered include physical layer considerations, network protocols, wide area networks, local area networks, wireless networks, switches and routing, congestion, Internet traffic, and network security. Students will be expected to do extensive systems/network programming and will be expected to make use of simulation and measurement tools to gain an appreciation of current network design and performance issues. This course is also highly recommended for RBE and IMGD majors. Recommended background: CS 2301 or CS 2303, or a significant knowledge of C/C++. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Yanhua Li" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Yanhua Li" room="Atwater Kent 233 " seats="65" section="D01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the fundamental principles of software engineering. Modern software development techniques and life cycles are emphasized. Topics include requirements analysis and specification, analysis and design, architecture, implementation, testing and quality, configuration management, and project management. Students will be expected to complete a project that employs techniques from the topics studied. This course should be taken before any course requiring a large programming project. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 509.  Recommended background: CS 2102, CS 2103, or CS 2119. " max-credits="0.0" min-credits="0.0" name="Software Engineering" number="3733"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000335536" max_waitlist="999" number="Interest List-B Term" part-of-term="B Term" seats="0" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the fundamental principles of software engineering. Modern software development techniques and life cycles are emphasized. Topics include requirements analysis and specification, analysis and design, architecture, implementation, testing and quality, configuration management, and project management. Students will be expected to complete a project that employs techniques from the topics studied. This course should be taken before any course requiring a large programming project. Undergraduate credit may not be earned both for this course and for CS 509.  Recommended background: CS 2102, CS 2103, or CS 2119. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="?" ends="12:00PM" max_waitlist="999" professor="N/A" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="N/A" room="U