roduction and refrigeration.  Recommended background: basic physics, (PH 1110, PH 1111) elementary differential and integral calculus (MA 1021, MA 1022) or equivalents. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Introduction To Thermodynamics" number="3001"><section actual_waitlist="7" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000334179" max_waitlist="40" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="70" sec_desc=" Cat. I  This course emphasizes system and control volume modeling using conservation of mass and the First and Second Laws of Thermodynamics. Topics include an introduction to heat, work, energy, and power, properties of simple substances, and cycle analysis for power production and refrigeration.  Recommended background: basic physics, (PH 1110, PH 1111) elementary differential and integral calculus (MA 1021, MA 1022) or equivalents. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="7" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="40" professor="Alireza Mahdavi Nejad" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Alireza Mahdavi Nejad" room="Higgins Labs 218 Discovery Classroom " seats="70" section="A01" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="67" crn="000000000000335059" max_waitlist="40" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="120" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course emphasizes system and control volume modeling using conservation of mass and the First and Second Laws of Thermodynamics. Topics include an introduction to heat, work, energy, and power, properties of simple substances, and cycle analysis for power production and refrigeration. Recommended background: basic physics, (PH 1110, PH 1111) elementary differential and integral calculus (MA 1021, MA 1022) or equivalents. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="67" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="9:50AM" max_waitlist="40" professor="Reza Ziazi" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Reza Ziazi" room="Unity Hall 400 " seats="120" section="B01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000335395" max_waitlist="20" number="B02" part-of-term="B Term" seats="70" sec_desc=" Cat. I  This course emphasizes system and control volume modeling using conservation of mass and the First and Second Laws of Thermodynamics. Topics include an introduction to heat, work, energy, and power, properties of simple substances, and cycle analysis for power production and refrigeration.  Recommended background: basic physics, (PH 1110, PH 1111) elementary differential and integral calculus (MA 1021, MA 1022) or equivalents. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Aswin Gnanaskandan" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Aswin Gnanaskandan" room="Kaven Hall 116 " seats="70" section="B02" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="4" crn="000000000000336371" max_waitlist="20" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="90" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course emphasizes system and control volume modeling using conservation of mass and the First and Second Laws of Thermodynamics. Topics include an introduction to heat, work, energy, and power, properties of simple substances, and cycle analysis for power production and refrigeration. Recommended background: basic physics, (PH 1110, PH 1111) elementary differential and integral calculus (MA 1021, MA 1022) or equivalents. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="4" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="1:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Selcuk Guceri" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Selcuk Guceri" room="Higgins Labs 116 " seats="90" section="C01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="25" crn="000000000000337577" max_waitlist="20" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="70" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course emphasizes system and control volume modeling using conservation of mass and the First and Second Laws of Thermodynamics. Topics include an introduction to heat, work, energy, and power, properties of simple substances, and cycle analysis for power production and refrigeration. Recommended background: basic physics, (PH 1110, PH 1111) elementary differential and integral calculus (MA 1021, MA 1022) or equivalents. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="25" building="" days="mon,tue,wed,thu,fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="20" professor="Yu Zhong" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Yu Zhong" room="Higgins Labs 116 " seats="70" section="D01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the student to the phenomena of diffusion and mass transfer. These occur in processes during which a change in chemical composition of one or more phases occurs. Diffusion and mass transfer can take place in living systems, in the environment, and in chemical processes. This course will show how to handle quantitative calculations involving diffusion and/or mass transfer, including design of process equipment. Topics may include: fundamentals of diffusional transport, diffusion in thin films; unsteady diffusion; diffusion in solids; convective mass transfer; dispersion; transport in membranes; diffusion with chemical reaction; simultaneous heat and mass transfer; selected mass transfer operations such as absorption, drying, humidification, extraction, crystallization, adsorption, etc. Recommended background: fundamentals of chemical thermodynamics, fluid flow and heat transfer; ordinary differential equations (MA 2051 or equivalent). " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Mass Transfer" number="3002"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="28" crn="000000336470336471" max_waitlist="0" number="CL01/CD01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the student to the phenomena of diffusion and mass transfer. These occur in processes during which a change in chemical composition of one or more phases occurs. Diffusion and mass transfer can take place in living systems, in the environment, and in chemical processes. This course will show how to handle quantitative calculations involving diffusion and/or mass transfer, including design of process equipment. Topics may include: fundamentals of diffusional transport, diffusion in thin films; unsteady diffusion; diffusion in solids; convective mass transfer; dispersion; transport in membranes; diffusion with chemical reaction; simultaneous heat and mass transfer; selected mass transfer operations such as absorption, drying, humidification, extraction, crystallization, adsorption, etc. Recommended background: fundamentals of chemical thermodynamics, fluid flow and heat transfer; ordinary differential equations (MA 2051 or equivalent). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="28" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Elizabeth Stewart" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Elizabeth Stewart" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="60" section="CL01" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="28" building="" days="wed" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Elizabeth Stewart" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Elizabeth Stewart" room="Goddard Hall 227 Classroom " seats="60" section="CD01" starts="10:00AM" type="Discussion"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" crn="000000337578337580" max_waitlist="0" number="DL01/DD01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="60" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course introduces the student to the phenomena of diffusion and mass transfer. These occur in processes during which a change in chemical composition of one or more phases occurs. Diffusion and mass transfer can take place in living systems, in the environment, and in chemical processes. This course will show how to handle quantitative calculations involving diffusion and/or mass transfer, including design of process equipment. Topics may include: fundamentals of diffusional transport, diffusion in thin films; unsteady diffusion; diffusion in solids; convective mass transfer; dispersion; transport in membranes; diffusion with chemical reaction; simultaneous heat and mass transfer; selected mass transfer operations such as absorption, drying, humidification, extraction, crystallization, adsorption, etc. Recommended background: fundamentals of chemical thermodynamics, fluid flow and heat transfer; ordinary differential equations (MA 2051 or equivalent). " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Andrew Teixeira" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Andrew Teixeira" room="Fuller Labs 320 " seats="60" section="DL01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="18" building="" days="wed" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Andrew Teixeira" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Andrew Teixeira" room="Fuller Labs 320 " seats="60" section="DD01" starts="2:00PM" type="Discussion"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I  This course presents the fundamentals of heat transfer in the three modes of conduction, convection, and radiation. Topics include steady-state and transient heat conduction, forced external and internal convection, natural convection, heat exchanger analysis, radiation properties, and radiative exchange between surfaces.  Recommended background: knowledge of thermodynamics, fluid mechanics, and ordinary differential equations (ES 3001, ES 3004, and MA 2051) or equivalents. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Heat Transfer" number="3003"><section actual_waitli