andom variables; special distributions including the Bernoulli, Binomial, Geometric, Poisson, Uniform, Normal, Exponential, Chisquare, Gamma, Weibull, and Beta distributions; multivariate distributions; conditional and marginal distributions; independence; expectation; transformations of univariate random variables.  Recommended background: MA 1024. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="8" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Michael Smith" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Michael Smith" room="Unity Hall 500 " seats="90" section="BL01" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="7" building="" days="wed" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Michael Smith" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Michael Smith" room="Higgins Labs 202 " seats="30" section="BD04" starts="12:00PM" type="Discussion"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" crn="000000334938334918" max_waitlist="0" number="BL02/BD05" part-of-term="B Term" seats="35" sec_desc=" MA 2621: Probability for Applications (Cat I, 1/3 Units) This course is an application-oriented course, primarily designed for non-Mathematical Sciences majors, and introduces the student to applied probability. Topics to be covered are: basic probability theory including Bayes theorem; discrete and continuous random variables; special distributions including the Bernoulli, Binomial, Geometric, Poisson, Uniform, Normal, Exponential, Chi-square, Gamma, Weibull, and Beta distributions; multivariate distributions; conditional and marginal distributions; independence; expectation; transformations of univariate random variables. Credit may not be earned both for this course and for MA 2631 Probability Theory. Recommended Background: MA 1024. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="0" professor="Tatiana Doytchin