Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" MU 1100 Foundations of Music Theory and Aural Skills (1/3 unit; Cat. I) This course introduces basic music theory concepts and helps students develop aural skills. Course topics include scales, intervals, chords, harmonic progressions, and rhythm. Activities include both written work in music notation and ear training exercises. Recommended Background: some basic knowledge of reading music " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="21" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Mitchell Lutch" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Mitchell Lutch" room="Alden Hall B06 Janet Earle Room " seats="25" section="D02" starts="3:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="20" crn="000000000000337290" max_waitlist="10" number="D03" part-of-term="D Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" MU 1100 Foundations of Music Theory and Aural Skills (1/3 unit; Cat. I) This course introduces basic music theory concepts and helps students develop aural skills. Course topics include scales, intervals, chords, harmonic progressions, and rhythm. Activities include both written work in music notation and ear training exercises. Recommended Background: some basic knowledge of reading music " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="20" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Monica Hatch Moysey" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Monica Hatch Moysey" room="Alden Hall B06 Janet Earle Room " seats="25" section="D03" starts="4:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" MU 2001: History of Western Art Music Before 1750 This course provides a historical survey of Western music from Medieval through Baroque periods with an emphasis on understanding stylistic traits and theoretical concepts of the eras. Topics include Gregorian chant and secular monophony; evolution of musical notation; development of polyphonic music; and vocal and instrumental genres such as mass, motet, madrigal, opera, cantata, sonata, and concerto, among others. No prior background in music is necessary.  Students may not receive credit for both MU 2720 and MU 2001 " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="History of Western Art Music Before 1750" number="2001"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" crn="000000000000334033" max_waitlist="10" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="35" sec_desc=" MU 2001: History of Western Art Music Before 1750 This course provides a historical survey of Western music from Medieval through Baroque periods with an emphasis on understanding stylistic traits and theoretical concepts of the eras. Topics include Gregorian chant and secular monophony; evolution of musical notation; development of polyphonic music; and vocal and instrumental genres such as mass, motet, madrigal, opera, cantata, sonata, and concerto, among others. No prior background in music is necessary.  Students may not receive credit for both MU 2720 and MU 2001 " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="1" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Brent Wetters" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Brent Wetters" room="Alden Hall B06 Janet Earle Room " seats="35" section="A01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" MU 2002: History of Western Art Music After 1750 This course provides a historical survey of Western music from the Classical period to the present with an emphasis on understanding stylistic traits and theoretical concepts of the eras. Topics include the development of genres such as sonata, string quartet, concerto, symphony, symphonic poem, character piece, Lied, and opera; and 20th century trends of impressionism, primitivism, atonality, serialism, minimalism, aleatoric music, and electronic music. No prior background in music is necessary.  Students may not receive credit for both MU 2721 and MU 2002 " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="History of Western Art Music After 1750" number="2002"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000334935" max_waitlist="10" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="35" sec_desc=" MU 2002: History of Western Art Music After 1750 This course provides a historical survey of Western music from the Classical period to the present with an emphasis on understanding stylistic traits and theoretical concepts of the eras. Topics include the development of genres such as sonata, string quartet, concerto, symphony, symphonic poem, character piece, Lied, and opera; and 20th century trends of impressionism, primitivism, atonality, serialism, minimalism, aleatoric music, and electronic music. No prior background in music is necessary.  Students may not receive credit for both MU 2721 and MU 2002 " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="0" building="" days="tue,fri" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Brent Wetters" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Brent Wetters" room="Alden Hall B06 Janet Earle Room " seats="35" section="B01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. II Through an introduction to the musical contributions of Louis Armstrong, Duke Ellington, Charlie Parker, Miles Davis and others, students are exposed to the chronological development of the language of jazz. Each jazz era is examined in detail including the musical and social contexts which helped define it. Participants are expected to build aural skills with the goal of identifying specific historical periods through the recognition of particular musical characteristics. Students examine in depth one artist of their choice. This course will be offered in 2020-21, and in alternating years thereafter. [This replaces MU 4623. Credit is not allowed for both MU 4623 and MU 2719.] " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Jazz History" number="2010"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" crn="000000000000337357" max_waitlist="10" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="35" sec_desc=" Cat. II Through an introduction to the musical contributions of Louis Armstrong, Duke Ellington, Charlie Parker, Miles Davis and others, students are exposed to the chronological development of the language of jazz. Each jazz era is examined in detail including the musical and social contexts which helped define it. Participants are expected to build aural skills with the goal of identifying specific historical periods through the recognition of particular musical characteristics. Students examine in depth one artist of their choice. This course will be offered in 2020-21, and in alternating years thereafter. [This replaces MU 4623. Credit is not allowed for both MU 4623 and MU 2719.] " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Douglas Olsen" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Douglas Olsen" room="Alden Hall B06 Janet Earle Room " seats="35" section="D01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I This course will explore the uniqueness of America’s popular music and its origins in the music of Africa and the folk music of Europe. Particular emphasis will be given to the origins and history of rock ‘n’ roll examining its roots in blues and early American popular music. [This replaces MU 4625. Credit is not allowed for both MU 4625 and MU 2722.] " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="History Of American Popular Music" number="2020"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="26" crn="000000000000343132" max_waitlist="10" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="35" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course will explore the uniqueness of America’s popular music and its origins in the music of Africa and the folk music of Europe. Particular emphasis will be given to the origins and history of rock ‘n’ roll examining its roots in blues and early American popular music. [This replaces MU 4625. Credit is not allowed for both MU 4625 and MU 2722.] " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="26" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Lucy Caplan" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Lucy Caplan" room="Alden Hall B06 Janet Earle Room " seats="35" section="B01" starts="10:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" crn="000000000000336195" max_waitlist="15" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="35" sec_desc=" Cat. I This course will explore the uniqueness of America’s popular music and its origins in the music of Africa and the folk music of Europe. Particular emphasis will be given to the origins and history of rock ‘n’ roll examining its roots in blues and early American popular music. [This replaces MU 4625. Credit is not allowed for both MU 4625 and MU 2722.] " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="14" building="" days="mon,tue,thu,fri" ends="4:50PM" max_waitlist="15" professor="Alan Vaudreuil" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Alan Vaudreuil" room="Alden Hall B06 Janet Earle Room " seats="35" section="C01" starts="4:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I  This course will investigate the sonic organization of musical works and performances, focusing on fundamental questions involved in the process of composition: How do I connect different ideas? How can I make a larger work out of smaller parts? How can I vary statements to create interest without compromising coherence? Where do I start? A progressive series of composition projects will build techniques in relevant areas including rhythm, harmony, melody, and form. Exercises in mechanics will be complemented by contemplation and discussion of artistic, aesthetic and philosophical ideas that are equally important in the compositional process. We will examine the relationship between musical works and how they are communicated as instructions to others (e.g. orally, as symbols, prose, graphic images, or computer programs). Weekly listening, reading, and composition assignments draw on a broad range of musical styles and intellectual traditions from various cultures and historical periods.    Recommended background: understanding of basic music theory through coursework (e.g. MU 1511, Introduction to Music or MU 1611, Fundamentals of Music) or equivalent experience. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Music Composition" number="2100"><section actual_waitlist="4" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000335130" max_waitlist="10" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I  This course will investigate the sonic organization of musical works and performances, focusing on fundamental questions involved in the process of composition: How do I connect different ideas? How can I make a larger work out of smaller parts? How can I vary statements to create interest without compromising coherence? Where do I start? A progressive series of composition projects will build techniques in relevant areas including rhythm, harmony, melody, and form. Exercises in mechanics will be complemented by contemplation and discussion of artistic, aesthetic and philosophical ideas that are equally important in the compositional process. We will examine the relationship between musical works and how they are communicated as instructions to others (e.g. orally, as symbols, prose, graphic images, or computer programs). Weekly listening, reading, and composition assignments draw on a broad range of musical styles and intellectual traditions from various cultures and historical periods.    Recommended background: understanding of basic music theory through coursework (e.g. MU 1511, Introduction to Music or MU 1611, Fundamentals of Music) or equivalent experience. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="4" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="David Ibbett" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="David Ibbett" room="Alden Hall B30 Music Technology Lab " seats="20" section="B01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="2" crn="000000000000336196" max_waitlist="5" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I  This course will investigate the sonic organization of musical works and performances, focusing on fundamental questions involved in the process of composition: How do I connect different ideas? How can I make a larger work out of smaller parts? How can I vary statements to create interest without compromising coherence? Where do I start? A progressive series of composition projects will build techniques in relevant areas including rhythm, harmony, melody, and form. Exercises in mechanics will be complemented by contemplation and discussion of artistic, aesthetic and philosophical ideas that are equally important in the compositional process. We will examine the relationship between musical works and how they are communicated as instructions to others (e.g. orally, as symbols, prose, graphic images, or computer programs). Weekly listening, reading, and composition assignments draw on a broad range of musical styles and intellectual traditions from various cultures and historical periods.  Recommended background: understanding of basic music theory through coursework (e.g. MU 1511, Introduction to Music or MU 1611, Fundamentals of Music) or equivalent experience. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="2" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="5" professor="Not Assigned" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Not Assigned" room="Alden Hall B30 Music Technology Lab " seats="20" section="C01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="4" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000337356" max_waitlist="10" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" Cat. I  This course will investigate the sonic organization of musical works and performances, focusing on fundamental questions involved in the process of composition: How do I connect different ideas? How can I make a larger work out of smaller parts? How can I vary statements to create interest without compromising coherence? Where do I start? A progressive series of composition projects will build techniques in relevant areas including rhythm, harmony, melody, and form. Exercises in mechanics will be complemented by contemplation and discussion of artistic, aesthetic and philosophical ideas that are equally important in the compositional process. We will examine the relationship between musical works and how they are communicated as instructions to others (e.g. orally, as symbols, prose, graphic images, or computer programs). Weekly listening, reading, and composition assignments draw on a broad range of musical styles and intellectual traditions from various cultures and historical periods.    Recommended background: understanding of basic music theory through coursework (e.g. MU 1511, Introduction to Music or MU 1611, Fundamentals of Music) or equivalent experience. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="4" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="3:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Jonathan Blumhofer" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Jonathan Blumhofer" room="Alden Hall B30 Music Technology Lab " seats="20" section="D01" starts="2:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" MU 2101: Arranging and Orchestration (1/3 Unit; Cat. I) Students will study specific characteristics of instruments and the voice to enable them to successfully arrange vocal and instrumental music. Students will need to possess a basic knowledge of music theory.  Students may not receive credit for both MU 3002 and MU 2101 Suggested background for this course is MU 1100 (Foundations of Music Theory and Aural Skills) or its equivalent " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Arranging And Orchestration" number="2101"><section actual_waitlist="2" availableseats="-1" crn="000000000000334026" max_waitlist="10" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" MU 2101: Arranging and Orchestration (1/3 Unit; Cat. I) Students will study specific characteristics of instruments and the voice to enable them to successfully arrange vocal and instrumental music. Students will need to possess a basic knowledge of music theory.  Students may not receive credit for both MU 3002 and MU 2101 Suggested background for this course is MU 1100 (Foundations of Music Theory and Aural Skills) or its equivalent " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="2" availableseats="-1" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Joshua Rohde" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Joshua Rohde" room="First Baptist Church Gordon " seats="20" section="A01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" crn="000000000000336308" max_waitlist="10" number="C01" part-of-term="C Term" seats="20" sec_desc=" MU 2101: Arranging and Orchestration (1/3 Unit; Cat. I) Students will study specific characteristics of instruments and the voice to enable them to successfully arrange vocal and instrumental music. Students will need to possess a basic knowledge of music theory.  Students may not receive credit for both MU 3002 and MU 2101 Suggested background for this course is MU 1100 (Foundations of Music Theory and Aural Skills) or its equivalent " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="5" building="" days="mon,wed" ends="10:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Matthew Scinto" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Matthew Scinto" room="Alden Hall B30 Music Technology Lab " seats="20" section="C01" starts="9:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. II This course will present ways to facilitate musicianship through the use of technology. Course topics include an introduction to music notation software, MIDI and audio recording, signal processing, and interactive music system programming. The course will address past, current, and emerging trends in music technology as they relate to facilitating an understanding of musical concepts. Students may not receive credit for both MU 2300 and MU 230X. Suggested background: a basic understanding of music notation and the fundamentals of music. This course will be offered in 2020-21, and in alternating years thereafter. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Foundations Of Music Technology" number="2300"><section actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000333954" max_waitlist="10" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" Cat. II This course will present ways to facilitate musicianship through the use of technology. Course topics include an introduction to music notation software, MIDI and audio recording, signal processing, and interactive music system programming. The course will address past, current, and emerging trends in music technology as they relate to facilitating an understanding of musical concepts. Students may not receive credit for both MU 2300 and MU 230X. Suggested background: a basic understanding of music notation and the fundamentals of music. This course will be offered in 2020-21, and in alternating years thereafter. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="0" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="V Manzo" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="V Manzo" room="Alden Hall B30 Music Technology Lab " seats="25" section="A01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="0" building="" days="wed" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="V Manzo" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="V Manzo" room=" Alden Hall B30 Music Technology Lab " seats="25" section="A01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000340553" max_waitlist="10" number="B01" part-of-term="B Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" Cat. II This course will present ways to facilitate musicianship through the use of technology. Course topics include an introduction to music notation software, MIDI and audio recording, signal processing, and interactive music system programming. The course will address past, current, and emerging trends in music technology as they relate to facilitating an understanding of musical concepts. Students may not receive credit for both MU 2300 and MU 230X. Suggested background: a basic understanding of music notation and the fundamentals of music. This course will be offered in 2020-21, and in alternating years thereafter. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="0" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="V Manzo" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="V Manzo" room="Alden Hall B30 Music Technology Lab " seats="25" section="B01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="0" building="" days="wed" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="V Manzo" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="V Manzo" room=" Alden Hall B30 Music Technology Lab " seats="25" section="B01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="13" crn="000000000000337485" max_waitlist="10" number="D01" part-of-term="D Term" seats="25" sec_desc="Cat. II This course will present ways to facilitate musicianship through the use of technology. Course topics include an introduction to music notation software, MIDI and audio recording, signal processing, and interactive music system programming. The course will address past, current, and emerging trends in music technology as they relate to facilitating an understanding of musical concepts. Students may not receive credit for both MU 2300 and MU 230X. Suggested background: a basic understanding of music notation and the fundamentals of music. This course will be offered in 2020-21, and in alternating years thereafter." term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="13" building="" days="wed" ends="12:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="V Manzo" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="V Manzo" room="Alden Hall B30 Music Technology Lab " seats="25" section="D01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="13" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="11:50AM" max_waitlist="10" professor="V Manzo" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="V Manzo" room=" Alden Hall B30 Music Technology Lab " seats="25" section="D01" starts="11:00AM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. II This course will explore automatic mechanical (electro)acoustic instruments, the people that design and build them and the music that they make. The subject is inherently interdisciplinary, so activities will engage with historical, musical, and technical matters. The history of automatic mechanical instruments reaches back centuries: we will study past designs so that we can better contextualize modern efforts, which have progressed alongside increasing computational power and machine sensing abilities. We will consider the music that has been composed using such instruments including the works of Anthiel, Nancarrow, Ligeti, Gann, and Metheny. In doing so, we will develop analytical tools required to understand such music and will illuminate relationships between electromechanical capabilities and musical ideas. The technical components of this course will introduce students to principles involved in instrument design, actuators, electronic circuits, microcontrollers, and musical programming environments. We will do all of this with our mind open to the question of how we can design new machines to make new kinds of music. Recommended Background: Fundamentals of Music I and / or Fundamentals of Music II, experience with programming and electronic circuits is helpful. This course will be offered in 2020-21, and in alternating years thereafter. " max-credits="3.0" min-credits="3.0" name="Making Music With Machines" number="2301"><section actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="0" crn="000000000000334060" max_waitlist="10" number="A01" part-of-term="A Term" seats="25" sec_desc=" Cat. II This course will explore automatic mechanical (electro)acoustic instruments, the people that design and build them and the music that they make. The subject is inherently interdisciplinary, so activities will engage with historical, musical, and technical matters. The history of automatic mechanical instruments reaches back centuries: we will study past designs so that we can better contextualize modern efforts, which have progressed alongside increasing computational power and machine sensing abilities. We will consider the music that has been composed using such instruments including the works of Anthiel, Nancarrow, Ligeti, Gann, and Metheny. In doing so, we will develop analytical tools required to understand such music and will illuminate relationships between electromechanical capabilities and musical ideas. The technical components of this course will introduce students to principles involved in instrument design, actuators, electronic circuits, microcontrollers, and musical programming environments. We will do all of this with our mind open to the question of how we can design new machines to make new kinds of music. Recommended Background: Fundamentals of Music I and / or Fundamentals of Music II, experience with programming and electronic circuits is helpful. This course will be offered in 2020-21, and in alternating years thereafter. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="1" availableseats="0" building="" days="mon,thu" ends="2:50PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Scott Barton" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Scott Barton" room="Alden Hall B30 Music Technology Lab " seats="25" section="A01" starts="1:00PM" type="Lecture"/></section></course><course course_desc=" Cat. I The Glee Club is one of WPI’s choral ensembles and the oldest student organization on campus. Glee Club performs many styles and periods of the vast repertoire of music featuring tenor and bass voices. Several times each year the Glee Club and Alden Voices (soprano and alto voices) join forces as the WPI Festival Chorus to perform major works of the repertoire. The Glee Club regularly performs on campus, throughout the Worcester area, and takes international and domestic tours. Rehearsals are held weekly. No audition is required. The course is open to all who are interested and sing in the tenor and bass range. " max-credits="1.5" min-credits="1.5" name="Glee Club" number="2401"><section actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="952" crn="000000000000335942" max_waitlist="10" number="F01" part-of-term="A Term, B Term" seats="999" sec_desc=" Cat. I The Glee Club is one of WPI’s choral ensembles and the oldest student organization on campus. Glee Club performs many styles and periods of the vast repertoire of music featuring tenor and bass voices. Several times each year the Glee Club and Alden Voices (soprano and alto voices) join forces as the WPI Festival Chorus to perform major works of the repertoire. The Glee Club regularly performs on campus, throughout the Worcester area, and takes international and domestic tours. Rehearsals are held weekly. No audition is required. The course is open to all who are interested and sing in the tenor and bass range. " term="202201"><period actual_waitlist="0" availableseats="952" building="" days="tue,thu" ends="6:30PM" max_waitlist="10" professor="Joshua Rohde" professor_email="look@it.up" professor_sort_name="Joshua Rohde" room="First Baptist Church Gordon " seats="999" section="F01" 